export default {"cls": "geo","label": "Geometry","items": [{"id": "Geo.1-01","std": "Geo.1","q": "Simplify √50 completely.","a": "5√2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["10√5","25√2","2√5","5√2"],"steps": ["Find the biggest perfect square that divides evenly into 50.","Split √{50} into √{25} × √{2}.","Simplify √{25}, keep √{2}, and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-02","std": "Geo.1","q": "Simplify the expression: 3√7 + 5√7.","a": "8√7","lvl": 1,"choices": ["8√14","15√7","8√49","8√7"],"steps": ["Both terms have the same root, √{7}, so they are like terms.","Add the numbers in front of √{7}: 3 + 5.","Keep √{7} unchanged and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-03","std": "Geo.1","q": "Multiply and simplify: √3√12.","a": "6","lvl": 1,"choices": ["6","12","3√4","√15"],"steps": ["When multiplying roots, multiply the numbers inside one root.","Write √{3} × √{12} as √{3 × 12}.","Take the square root of the product and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-04","std": "Geo.1","q": "A triangle has side lengths √8 cm, √18 cm, and √32 cm. What is its perimeter in simplest radical form?","a": "9√2 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["9√2 cm","11√2 cm","9√6 cm","√58 cm"],"steps": ["Perimeter is the sum of all sides, so simplify each root first.","Pull out square factors: √{4 × 2}, √{9 × 2}, and √{16 × 2}.","Add the like √{2} terms and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-05","std": "Geo.1","q": "A rectangle has a length of 4√6 inches and a width of 2√3 inches. What is its area in simplest radical form?","a": "24√2 square inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["24√2 square inches","8√18 square inches","12√6 square inches","6√9 square inches"],"steps": ["Area is length × width, so multiply outside and inside parts.","Set up (4 × 2) × √{6 × 3}, then look for a square factor.","Simplify the root, multiply outside numbers, and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-06","std": "Geo.1","q": "Simplify by rationalizing the denominator: 12 / √3.","a": "4√3","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12√3","4 / √3","3√4","4√3"],"steps": ["Remove the root from the bottom without changing the value.","Multiply top and bottom of 12 ÷ √{3} by √{3}.","Simplify the bottom to a whole number, divide, and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-07","std": "Geo.1","q": "A square has an area of 24 square units. The diagonal of any square is √2 times its side length. What is the exact length of this square's diagonal in simplest radical form?","a": "4√3 units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["4√3 units","6√2 units","4√6 units","2√12 units"],"steps": ["Find the side length from the area, then find the diagonal.","Use side = √{24}, then multiply √{24} × √{2}.","Pull out the largest square factor and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.1-08","std": "Geo.1","q": "A right triangle has legs of length √20 meters and √45 meters. What is the exact length of the hypotenuse in simplest radical form?","a": "√65 meters","lvl": 3,"choices": ["25 meters","√65 meters","5√5 meters","13√5 meters"],"steps": ["Use a² + b² = c² to find the hypotenuse c.","Square each leg to remove the roots, then add the values.","Take the square root, check for square factors, and pick a choice."]},{"id": "Geo.1-09","std": "Geo.1","q": "In a pattern of squares, Square 1 has side length √2, Square 2 has side length √8, and Square 3 has side length √18. If this pattern continues, what is the perimeter of Square 5 in simplest radical form?","a": "20√2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["20√2","16√2","25√2","5√2"],"steps": ["Simplify each given side to see how the side length grows.","Extend the pattern to Square 5, then multiply its side by 4.","Keep the result in simplest radical form and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.2-01","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "A rectangular garden is 12 feet long and 8 feet wide. Which unit is appropriate for reporting its area?","a": "square feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["feet per second","cubic feet","feet","square feet"],"steps": ["Area measures flat space covered by a 2D shape.","Multiplying feet × feet gives a squared unit of length.","Match that unit type to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.2-02","std": "Geo.2","part": "b","q": "A bar graph displays monthly rainfall in inches with the vertical axis starting at 10 inches instead of 0 inches. How does this affect the visual appearance of the bars?","a": "It exaggerates the differences between months.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["It changes the actual rainfall values.","It makes all months look equal.","It exaggerates the differences between months.","It hides the largest rainfall month."],"steps": ["Compare a bar graph starting at 0 to one cut off at 10.","Cutting off the bottom makes small height gaps look much bigger.","Match this visual effect to the best choice."]},{"id": "Geo.2-03","std": "Geo.2","part": "d","q": "A student measures the length of a pencil with a ruler marked only to the nearest tenth of a centimeter. Which measurement is reported to an appropriate level of accuracy?","a": "14.3 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["14.32 cm","14.3 cm","14.328 cm","14.3000 cm"],"steps": ["Match the reported decimal places to what the ruler can read.","A tenth of a centimeter means one digit after the decimal point.","Pick the choice with that exact level of precision."]},{"id": "Geo.2-04","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "A rectangular prism has a length of 2 feet, a width of 18 inches, and a height of 12 inches. What is its volume in cubic feet?","a": "3 cubic feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["432 cubic feet","3 cubic feet","36 cubic feet","2.5 cubic feet"],"steps": ["Change all three measurements to feet before finding volume.","Divide inches by 12, then set up V = l × w × h in feet.","Multiply the three foot measures and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.2-05","std": "Geo.2","part": "c","q": "A city planner wants to model how crowded different public parks are. Which quantity is most appropriate to define for this model?","a": "visitors per acre","lvl": 2,"choices": ["total visitors","acres per year","total acres","visitors per acre"],"steps": ["Crowding compares the number of people to the size of the space.","Write a rate with people on top and land area on the bottom.","Match that rate to the best choice."]},{"id": "Geo.2-06","std": "Geo.2","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot shows car mileage from 0 to 150,000 miles on the x-axis and resale price from §0 to §30,000 on the y-axis. Which scale is most appropriate for the x-axis?","a": "25,000 miles per grid line","lvl": 2,"choices": ["100 miles per grid line","1,000 miles per grid line","150,000 miles per grid line","25,000 miles per grid line"],"steps": ["A good axis scale uses a neat number of grid lines, about 5 to 10.","Divide 150,000 by each step size to count how many grid lines fit.","Choose the scale that fits cleanly on a normal graph."]},{"id": "Geo.2-07","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "A cylindrical water tank has a radius of 3 feet and a height of 60 inches. Water flows into the tank at 2 cubic feet per minute. About how many minutes will it take to fill the tank? Use π = 3.14.","a": "70.7 minutes","lvl": 3,"choices": ["28.3 minutes","70.7 minutes","847.8 minutes","141.3 minutes"],"steps": ["Convert height to feet so all units match cubic feet.","Find V = 3.14 × r² × h, then divide by the flow rate.","Round to the nearest tenth and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.2-08","std": "Geo.2","part": "d","q": "A carpenter measures a rectangular tabletop as 4.2 ft by 2.5 ft using a tape measure accurate to the nearest tenth of a foot. A calculator gives 4.2 × 2.5 = 10.5, and the carpenter divides by 3 to get 3.500000 ft² per section. Why is reporting 3.500000 ft² inappropriate?","a": "It implies greater precision than the original measurements allow.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The area should be rounded to the nearest hundred.","Square feet cannot be written as decimals.","It implies greater precision than the original measurements allow.","The calculation 10.5 / 3 is incorrect."],"steps": ["Compare the precision of the tool to the digits in the final number.","Extra trailing zeros claim accuracy the tape measure never had.","Match this idea to the best choice."]},{"id": "Geo.2-09","std": "Geo.2","part": "c","q": "A delivery company wants to compare fuel efficiency across trucks carrying different loads over different distances. Which derived quantity best captures efficiency for this model?","a": "ton-miles per gallon","lvl": 3,"choices": ["ton-miles per gallon","tons per hour","gallons per truck","miles per hour"],"steps": ["Combine load weight, distance traveled, and fuel used in one rate.","Put work done (weight × distance) per unit of fuel used.","Match that combined unit to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-01","std": "Geo.3","q": "Find the coordinates of the vertex formed by the intersection of the lines y = 2x + 1 and y = x + 4.","a": "(3, 7)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(7, 3)","(−3, −5)","(3, 7)","(5, 11)"],"steps": ["Lines meet where both equations give the same x and y values.","Set 2x + 1 = x + 4 and solve for x.","Plug x back in to find y, then check (x, y) against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-02","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two sides of a polygon lie on the lines x = 4 and y = 3x − 5. What are the coordinates of the vertex where these two lines intersect?","a": "(4, 7)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(4, 17)","(4, 7)","(7, 4)","(3, 4)"],"steps": ["You already know x = 4, so use it to find the matching y.","Plug 4 in for x in y = 3x − 5.","Write the point as (x, y) and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-03","std": "Geo.3","q": "Find the intersection point of the lines y = −x + 6 and y = 2.","a": "(4, 2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, 4)","(4, 2)","(8, 2)","(−4, 2)"],"steps": ["You already know y = 2, so find the x that makes it true.","Substitute 2 for y to get 2 = −x + 6, then solve for x.","Write the ordered pair (x, y) and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-04","std": "Geo.3","q": "A triangle is bounded by the lines x = 0, y = 0, and y = −2x + 6. What are the coordinates of its three vertices?","a": "(0, 0), (3, 0), and (0, 6)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0, 0), (6, 0), and (0, 3)","(0, 0), (−3, 0), and (0, 6)","(0, 0), (2, 0), and (0, 6)","(0, 0), (3, 0), and (0, 6)"],"steps": ["Each vertex is where a pair of the three lines crosses.","Find where x = 0 meets y = 0, then plug x = 0 and y = 0 into the line.","List all three (x, y) pairs and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-05","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two edges of a quadrilateral lie on the lines 2x + y = 10 and y = x − 2. What are the coordinates of the vertex where they meet?","a": "(4, 2)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, 2)","(6, 4)","(2, 4)","(3, 1)"],"steps": ["Use substitution to find the (x, y) point on both lines.","Replace y in 2x + y = 10 with x − 2, then solve for x.","Find y using y = x − 2 and check your point against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-06","std": "Geo.3","q": "A triangle has two sides along the lines y = (1/2)x + 1 and y = −x + 7. What is the vertex formed by these two lines?","a": "(4, 3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(6, 4)","(3, 4)","(4, 3)","(2, 5)"],"steps": ["Set the two expressions for y equal to find where the lines meet.","Solve {1/2}x + 1 = −x + 7 by grouping x terms on one side.","Plug x into either line for y and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-07","std": "Geo.3","q": "A triangle is formed by the lines y = x, y = −x + 8, and y = 0. Find the vertices and then calculate the area of the triangle.","a": "16 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["32 square units","8 square units","24 square units","16 square units"],"steps": ["Find all three vertices first so you can get the base and height.","Find both intercepts on y = 0 and the top vertex where x = −x + 8.","Use A = {1/2}bh with those lengths and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-08","std": "Geo.3","q": "A trapezoid is bounded by the lines y = 1, y = 5, x = 0, and y = −2x + 9. What are the coordinates of the vertex on the top base that does not lie on the y-axis?","a": "(2, 5)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(7, 5)","(2, 5)","(5, 2)","(4, 1)"],"steps": ["The top base lies on y = 5, and the slanted side is y = −2x + 9.","Plug y = 5 into y = −2x + 9 and solve for x.","Pair that x with y = 5 and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.3-09","std": "Geo.3","q": "A parallelogram is bounded by the lines y = 2, y = 6, y = 2x, and y = 2x − 8. What are the coordinates of all four vertices?","a": "(1, 2), (3, 6), (5, 2), and (7, 6)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(1, 2), (3, 6), (5, 2), and (7, 6)","(0, 2), (3, 6), (4, 2), and (7, 6)","(2, 1), (6, 3), (2, 5), and (6, 7)","(1, 2), (3, 6), (4, 2), and (6, 6)"],"steps": ["Each corner is where one horizontal line meets one slanted line.","Plug y = 2 and y = 6 into both y = 2x and y = 2x − 8.","Solve for all four x values and match the points to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-01","std": "Geo.4","q": "The area of a rectangle is given by A = lw. Rearrange the formula to solve for w.","a": "w = A / l","lvl": 1,"choices": ["w = l / A","w = A − l","w = Al","w = A / l"],"steps": ["Get w alone on one side of the equation.","Since w is multiplied by l, undo it by dividing both sides by l.","Write the new formula for w and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-02","std": "Geo.4","q": "The circumference of a circle is C = 2π × r. Rearrange the formula to solve for r.","a": "r = C / (2π)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["r = C / (2π)","r = (2π) / C","r = C − 2π","r = 2π × C"],"steps": ["Isolate r by undoing what is multiplied by it.","Divide both sides of C = 2πr by 2π.","Keep 2π together in the denominator and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-03","std": "Geo.4","q": "The perimeter of a rectangle is P = 2l + 2w. Rearrange the formula to solve for l.","a": "l = (P − 2w) / 2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["l = P / 2 − 2w","l = (P + 2w) / 2","l = P − 2w","l = (P − 2w) / 2"],"steps": ["Isolate the 2l term first, then solve for l.","Subtract 2w from both sides, then divide the whole side by 2.","Make sure both P and −2w are divided by 2, then check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-04","std": "Geo.4","q": "The area of a triangle is A = (1/2)bh. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = 2A / b","lvl": 2,"choices": ["h = 2A / b","h = A / (2b)","h = b / (2A)","h = 2A − b"],"steps": ["Undo the fraction {1/2} and the factor b to isolate h.","Multiply both sides by 2, then divide both sides by b.","Check your rearranged formula against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-05","std": "Geo.4","q": "The volume of a cylinder is V = π × r² × h. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = V / (π × r²)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["h = V − π × r²","h = V × π × r²","h = V / (π × r²)","h = (π × r²) / V"],"steps": ["Notice that h is multiplied by the whole product πr².","Divide both sides of the formula by πr².","Keep πr² together on the bottom and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-06","std": "Geo.4","q": "The area of a trapezoid is A = (1/2)(b1 + b2)h. Rearrange the formula to solve for b1.","a": "b1 = (2A / h) − b2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["b1 = (2A / h) − b2","b1 = (A / (2h)) − b2","b1 = (2A / h) + b2","b1 = (2A − b2) / h"],"steps": ["Clear the numbers outside the parentheses before isolating b1.","Multiply by 2, divide both sides by h, then subtract b2.","Check the order of operations in your result against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-07","std": "Geo.4","q": "The volume of a cone is V = (1/3) × π × r² × h. Rearrange the formula to solve for the positive radius r.","a": "r = √(3V / (π × h))","lvl": 3,"choices": ["r = √(V / (3π × h))","r = 3V / (π × h)","r = √(3V − π × h)","r = √(3V / (π × h))"],"steps": ["Isolate r² first, then undo the square for a positive radius.","Multiply by 3, divide by πh, then take the square root.","Ensure the whole fraction sits under the root and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-08","std": "Geo.4","q": "The surface area of a cylinder is S = 2π × r² + 2π × r × h. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = (S − 2π × r²) / (2π × r)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["h = S − r","h = (S + 2π × r²) / (2π × r)","h = (S − 2π × r²) / (2π × r)","h = S / (2π × r) − 2π × r²"],"steps": ["Move the term without h to the other side before dividing.","Subtract 2πr² from S, then divide the entire difference by 2πr.","Check that parentheses group the top, then match with choices."]},{"id": "Geo.4-09","std": "Geo.4","q": "In a right triangle, a² + b² = c² where a, b, and c are positive lengths. Solve for b in terms of a and c.","a": "b = √(c² − a²)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["b = √(a² − c²)","b = √(c² + a²)","b = c − a","b = √(c² − a²)"],"steps": ["Get b² alone before taking the square root.","Subtract a² from c², then take the square root of the whole side.","Remember √{c² − a²} does not split, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.5-01","std": "Geo.5","q": "Which point lies on the graph of the linear equation y = 3x − 2?","a": "(2, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(4, 2)","(2, 8)","(2, 4)","(1, −1)"],"steps": ["A point lies on the line if its (x, y) makes the equation true.","Plug each choice's x into 3x − 2 to see if it gives that y.","Pick the ordered pair that makes both sides equal."]},{"id": "Geo.5-02","std": "Geo.5","q": "Does the point (3, 5) lie on the graph of 2x + y = 11?","a": "Yes, because 2(3) + 5 = 11.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because 2(5) + 3 = 13.","No, because 3 + 5 = 8.","Yes, because 3 × 5 = 15.","Yes, because 2(3) + 5 = 11."],"steps": ["Test the point by plugging x = 3 and y = 5 into the equation.","Evaluate 2 × 3 + 5 and compare the result to 11.","Choose Yes or No with the matching reason from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.5-03","std": "Geo.5","q": "If the point (4, y) lies on the graph of y = −2x + 11, what is the value of y?","a": "3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["−3","19","3","7"],"steps": ["Use the given x-value of 4 to find its matching y-value.","Substitute 4 into y = −2 × 4 + 11.","Finish the arithmetic carefully and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.5-04","std": "Geo.5","q": "Which linear equation has a graph that passes through both (1, 5) and (3, 11)?","a": "y = 3x + 2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 4x + 1","y = 3x − 2","y = 2x + 3","y = 3x + 2"],"steps": ["The right equation must work for both points, not just one.","Find the slope {(11 − 5)/(3 − 1)} or test both points in each choice.","Verify both points make the equation true and pick that choice."]},{"id": "Geo.5-05","std": "Geo.5","q": "Find the value of k so that the point (k, 6) is a solution to 4x − 3y = 10.","a": "7","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4","−2","7","14"],"steps": ["Since (k, 6) is a solution, x = k and y = 6 make the equation true.","Plug in k and 6 to get 4k − 3 × 6 = 10, then solve for k.","Test your k back in 4x − 3y = 10 and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.5-06","std": "Geo.5","q": "Which of the following points does NOT lie on the graph of 3x − 2y = 12?","a": "(2, 3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(2, 3)","(4, 0)","(0, −6)","(2, −3)"],"steps": ["Look for the one point that fails to make 3x − 2y = 12 true.","Plug each choice's x and y into 3x − 2y and watch the minus sign.","Select the point whose result does not equal 12."]},{"id": "Geo.5-07","std": "Geo.5","q": "The points (1, 4), (3, 10), and (5, k) all lie on the same straight line. What must the value of k be?","a": "16","lvl": 3,"choices": ["18","16","15","14"],"steps": ["Points on a straight line share a constant rate of change.","Find how much y rises when x goes up by 2 from (1, 4) to (3, 10).","Apply that same step from (3, 10) to x = 5 and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.5-08","std": "Geo.5","q": "A student plots four solutions of 2x + y = 8 and connects them with a line. Why does every point on that line represent a solution to the equation?","a": "Any point (x, y) on the line has a constant rate of change of −2 from (0, 8), so 2x + y always equals 8.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Only integer coordinates on the line are solutions.","Any point (x, y) on the line has a constant rate of change of −2 from (0, 8), so 2x + y always equals 8.","Every line in the coordinate plane has the same solutions.","Because 2 + 1 = 3, which divides 8."],"steps": ["Solve 2x + y = 8 for y to see its y-intercept and slope.","Think about how every point on a line keeps the same slope.","Pick the choice that explains why every point satisfies 2x + y = 8."]},{"id": "Geo.5-09","std": "Geo.5","q": "A table lists four points: (−2, −5), (0, −1), (2, 3), and (4, 8). Do all four points lie on the graph of a single linear equation?","a": "No, because the first three points satisfy y = 2x − 1, but (4, 8) does not.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["No, because the first three points satisfy y = 2x − 1, but (4, 8) does not.","No, because (−2, −5) has negative coordinates.","Yes, because all four points satisfy y = 2x − 1.","Yes, because x increases by 2 each time."],"steps": ["Check whether the slope between consecutive points stays constant.","Find the equation from the first three points, then test (4, 8).","Use your test of (4, 8) to pick the matching explanation."]},{"id": "Geo.6-01","std": "Geo.6","q": "What is the equation of a circle with center (2, −3) and radius 5?","a": "(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(x + 2)² + (y − 3)² = 25","(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 25","(x − 2)² − (y + 3)² = 25","(x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 5"],"steps": ["Use standard circle form: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r².","Plug in h = 2, k = −3, and r = 5; watch the sign of y − (−3).","Square the radius on the right and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-02","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (0, 0) and (6, 8). What are the coordinates of the center of the circle?","a": "(3, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(4, 3)","(3, 4)","(12, 16)","(6, 8)"],"steps": ["The center of a circle is the midpoint of its diameter.","Average the x-values {(0 + 6)/2} and the y-values {(0 + 8)/2}.","Write the midpoint as (x, y) and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-03","std": "Geo.6","part": "b","q": "Use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the distance between the points (1, 2) and (4, 6).","a": "5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["√7","5","25","7"],"steps": ["Think of the segment as the hypotenuse of a right triangle.","Find legs 4 − 1 and 6 − 2, then square and add them.","Take the square root of the sum and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-04","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle has the equation (x + 5)² + (y − 1)² = 49. What are its center and radius?","a": "center (−5, 1), radius 7","lvl": 2,"choices": ["center (−5, 1), radius 7","center (5, −1), radius 7","center (5, −1), radius 49","center (−5, 1), radius 49"],"steps": ["Compare to (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r² to read h, k, and r.","Flip the signs inside parentheses for (h, k), and take √{49} for r.","Match your center and radius to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-05","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (−2, 1) and (4, 9). What is the equation of the circle?","a": "(x − 1)² + (y − 5)² = 25","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(x − 1)² + (y − 5)² = 25","(x − 1)² + (y − 5)² = 100","(x + 1)² + (y + 5)² = 25","(x − 3)² + (y − 4)² = 25"],"steps": ["Find the center with the midpoint, then find r² to an endpoint.","Average the endpoints for (h, k), then find distance squared to (4, 9).","Put (h, k) and r² into circle form and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-06","std": "Geo.6","part": "b","q": "To derive the distance formula between (x1, y1) and (x2, y2), a right triangle is drawn with horizontal leg |x2 − x1| and vertical leg |y2 − y1|. Which equation results from applying the Pythagorean Theorem?","a": "d = √((x2 − x1)² + (y2 − y1)²)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["d = √((x2 − x1)² + (y2 − y1)²)","d = √((x2 − x1)² − (y2 − y1)²)","d = √((x2 + x1)² + (y2 + y1)²)","d = (x2 − x1) + (y2 − y1)"],"steps": ["Set the hypotenuse d in a² + b² = d² using the two legs.","Square each leg, add them together, then take the square root.","Check the signs inside and between squares against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-07","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle has equation (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 34. Does the point (5, 4) lie inside, on, or outside the circle?","a": "on the circle","lvl": 3,"choices": ["on the circle","cannot be determined","outside the circle","inside the circle"],"steps": ["Compare the point's distance squared from center to r² = 34.","Plug x = 5 and y = 4 into (x − 2)² + (y + 1)².","Less means inside, equal means on, more means outside; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-08","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle has center (3, −2) and passes through the point (6, 2). What is the equation of the circle?","a": "(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 25","(x + 3)² + (y − 2)² = 25","(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 5","(x − 6)² + (y − 2)² = 25"],"steps": ["You know the center (h, k); find r² from the center to (6, 2).","Compute (6 − 3)² + (2 − (−2))² to get r².","Write (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r² and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.6-09","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (−3, 4) and (5, −2). What is the equation of the circle?","a": "(x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = 25","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x − 4)² + (y − 3)² = 25","(x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = 25","(x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = 100","(x + 1)² + (y + 1)² = 25"],"steps": ["Use the diameter endpoints to get the center first, then r².","Find midpoint (h, k), then compute distance squared to either endpoint.","Build the circle equation with r² (not d²) and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.7-01","std": "Geo.7","q": "Which of the following is an example of bivariate quantitative data?","a": "the height and weight of each student in a class","lvl": 1,"choices": ["the number of pets owned by each family","the test scores of 30 students","the height and weight of each student in a class","the favorite color of each student"],"steps": ["Bivariate means two variables; quantitative means measured numbers.","Look for a choice that measures two different numbers per person.","Rule out single-variable or category choices, then pick the match."]},{"id": "Geo.7-02","std": "Geo.7","q": "A scatter plot of daily high temperature and ice cream sales shows points rising from left to right along a straight band. What conclusion is best supported?","a": "Higher temperatures are associated with higher ice cream sales.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["There is no relationship between temperature and sales.","Ice cream sales stay constant at all temperatures.","Higher temperatures are associated with lower ice cream sales.","Higher temperatures are associated with higher ice cream sales."],"steps": ["Points rising from left to right show a positive association.","That means as x (temperature) goes up, y (sales) tends to go up.","Match that direction to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.7-03","std": "Geo.7","q": "In a safety test of 200 bike helmets, 6 failed impact standards. What is the empirical risk (probability) that a randomly chosen helmet fails?","a": "3%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30%","0.3%","6%","3%"],"steps": ["Empirical risk is the failed count divided by the total tested.","Set up {6/200}, then convert the fraction to a percent.","Check your percent carefully against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.7-04","std": "Geo.7","q": "Two delivery routes both have a mean travel time of 35 minutes. Route A has travel times ranging from 32 to 38 minutes, while Route B ranges from 20 to 55 minutes. Which route carries less risk of being late for a 40-minute deadline?","a": "Route A, because its variability is much smaller.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Both routes have the exact same risk because their means are equal.","Neither route can meet a 40-minute deadline.","Route B, because it can be as fast as 20 minutes.","Route A, because its variability is much smaller."],"steps": ["Compare how often each route can go past the 40-minute mark.","Check both ranges: one stays under 40, while the other goes past it.","Pick the choice linking smaller spread to lower late risk."]},{"id": "Geo.7-05","std": "Geo.7","q": "Data on car speed (mph) and stopping distance (feet) show a strong positive linear association between 20 mph and 60 mph. If a driver increases speed from 30 mph to 50 mph, what happens to the risk of a longer stopping distance?","a": "The expected stopping distance increases.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The expected stopping distance decreases.","The expected stopping distance stays the same.","The expected stopping distance increases.","Speed has no effect on stopping distance."],"steps": ["Positive association means both variables move in the same direction.","Since speed increases from 30 to 50 mph, follow the upward trend.","Match what happens to stopping distance with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.7-06","std": "Geo.7","q": "A factory checks the weight of 50 cereal boxes labeled 16 oz. The mean weight is 16.0 oz, but 15 boxes weigh under 15.5 oz. What is the best conclusion?","a": "The mean meets the label, but high variability creates a risk of underfilled boxes.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The factory should lower the mean weight.","The mean meets the label, but high variability creates a risk of underfilled boxes.","Every box is properly filled because the mean is 16.0 oz.","All boxes weigh less than 15.5 oz."],"steps": ["Look at both the average weight and how far weights spread out.","A good mean does not stop many boxes from being underweight.","Match this balance of center and spread to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.7-07","std": "Geo.7","q": "Two investments have the following annual returns over 5 years. Fund X: 5%, 6%, 6%, 7%, 6%. Fund Y: −4%, 12%, 2%, 15%, 5%. Both have a mean return of 6%. Which fund is better for an investor who wants to minimize the risk of losing money?","a": "Fund X, because its returns are tightly clustered around 6% with no negative years.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Both funds have the same risk because both average 6%.","Fund X, because its returns are tightly clustered around 6% with no negative years.","Fund Y, because it has a wider range.","Fund Y, because its highest return is 15%."],"steps": ["Both means are equal, so compare spread and negative years.","Notice which fund stays steady and avoids any loss below 0%.","Pick the choice that ties lower spread to lower loss risk."]},{"id": "Geo.7-08","std": "Geo.7","q": "A bridge engineer collects bivariate data on daily truck traffic and annual pavement wear, finding a strong positive linear association. The city proposes doubling truck traffic on the bridge. How should the engineer use this data to assess risk?","a": "Predict higher pavement wear and plan for earlier maintenance or load limits.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Ignore the linear trend because traffic is quantitative.","Assume wear will stay the same since the bridge is already built.","Conclude that pavement wear causes more truck traffic.","Predict higher pavement wear and plan for earlier maintenance or load limits."],"steps": ["Use the positive trend to predict what doubling traffic will do.","Connect higher predicted wear to a smart safety or repair plan.","Avoid choices claiming reverse cause, then pick the best match."]},{"id": "Geo.7-09","std": "Geo.7","q": "A clinic compares two medicines for lowering blood pressure. Medicine A lowers pressure by a median of 12 points (IQR = 3). Medicine B lowers pressure by a median of 13 points (IQR = 14). Which conclusion best balances effectiveness and risk?","a": "Medicine A is more consistent and predictable, even though Medicine B has a slightly higher median.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Medicine A has no effect on blood pressure.","Medicine A is more consistent and predictable, even though Medicine B has a slightly higher median.","Medicine B is always better because 13 is greater than 12.","The IQR does not matter when comparing treatments."],"steps": ["Compare both the typical drop (median) and consistency (IQR).","Notice the medians are close, but one IQR is much smaller.","Choose the statement that weighs both center and consistency."]},{"id": "Geo.8-01","std": "Geo.8","q": "A spreadsheet contains 5,000 rows of student attendance records. Which tool is best for viewing only the records from 10th grade?","a": "Filter","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Spell check","Find and replace","Merge cells","Filter"],"steps": ["You want to show only rows that match one rule and hide the rest.","Recall which spreadsheet tool hides rows that do not meet a rule.","Match that tool name to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.8-02","std": "Geo.8","q": "What is the primary purpose of 'cleaning' a large data set before analyzing it?","a": "To fix errors, remove duplicates, and standardize formats.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["To convert all numbers into text.","To change values so they fit a straight line.","To delete half the data so it is smaller.","To fix errors, remove duplicates, and standardize formats."],"steps": ["Think about what makes raw data reliable before you analyze it.","Cleaning fixes mistakes and messy labels without faking results.","Pick the choice that describes preparing accurate data."]},{"id": "Geo.8-03","std": "Geo.8","q": "A weather station logs 10,000 hourly temperatures in a spreadsheet. Which feature quickly puts the temperatures in order from lowest to highest?","a": "Sort ascending","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Split column","Freeze panes","Wrap text","Sort ascending"],"steps": ["You need to arrange numeric rows from smallest to largest.","Ordering from low to high is called ascending order.","Match that spreadsheet feature to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.8-04","std": "Geo.8","q": "In a survey of 2,000 high school students, the 'Age' column contains one entry of '160'. What is the most reasonable data-cleaning action?","a": "Flag it as a likely data-entry error and verify or exclude it before computing the mean.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Leave it in because all data must be averaged.","Delete the entire dataset.","Replace it with 0.","Flag it as a likely data-entry error and verify or exclude it before computing the mean."],"steps": ["Check if an age of 160 is realistic for a high school student.","Decide how to handle a clear typo without ruining the rest of data.","Choose the action that checks or excludes the bad entry safely."]},{"id": "Geo.8-05","std": "Geo.8","q": "A retail database has 15,000 sales rows with columns for Region, Product, and Revenue. Which spreadsheet tool best summarizes total revenue by Region?","a": "Pivot table","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Conditional formatting","Pivot table","Text wrapping","Page break"],"steps": ["You need a tool that groups rows by category and totals numbers.","Distinguish summary tools from display tools like text wrapping.","Pick the spreadsheet feature built to group and total data."]},{"id": "Geo.8-06","std": "Geo.8","q": "A state column in a 4,000-row table contains 'AL', 'Alabama', and 'al.' for the same state. What happens if you group by state without cleaning this column first?","a": "Software will treat them as three separate categories.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Software will automatically merge them into one category.","Software will treat them as three separate categories.","The numbers in the table will be deleted.","The spreadsheet will not open."],"steps": ["Computers match text by exact spelling, not human meaning.","Count how many different text strings appear for that one state.","Match what the software will do to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.8-07","std": "Geo.8","q": "A sensor logs 50,000 temperature readings, some in Celsius and some in Fahrenheit, with 200 blank cells. Which sequence of steps best prepares the data for analysis?","a": "Standardize all readings to one unit, handle blank cells without replacing them with 0, then summarize.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Sort the data alphabetically and delete every odd row.","Replace blank cells with 0, then average all numbers together.","Keep both units so the dataset stays larger.","Standardize all readings to one unit, handle blank cells without replacing them with 0, then summarize."],"steps": ["Spot the two data issues: mixed units and missing entries.","Make all units match and decide how blanks differ from zero.","Choose the sequence that cleans both issues before summarizing."]},{"id": "Geo.8-08","std": "Geo.8","q": "Why should missing values in a quantitative column (such as daily rainfall) NOT automatically be replaced with 0?","a": "Because a missing reading is unknown, and replacing it with 0 artificially lowers the mean.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because spreadsheets cannot calculate with 0.","Because a missing reading is unknown, and replacing it with 0 artificially lowers the mean.","Because 0 is not a real number.","Because replacing with 0 increases the maximum value."],"steps": ["Think about the difference between no rain and no record.","Ask how adding fake zeros shifts the average of real values.","Pick the choice explaining why replacing blanks with 0 skews data."]},{"id": "Geo.8-09","std": "Geo.8","q": "An analyst uses software to group 3,000 commute times (ranging from 5 to 90 minutes) into a histogram. What is the effect of choosing a very large bin width of 50 minutes?","a": "It hides important details of the distribution's shape, such as peaks and gaps.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It hides important details of the distribution's shape, such as peaks and gaps.","It eliminates all outliers from the dataset.","It changes the actual commute times in the spreadsheet.","It shows every individual commute time clearly."],"steps": ["See how many bars cover 5 to 90 minutes with a 50-minute width.","Putting thousands of values into just two bars loses fine detail.","Match that loss of shape detail to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-01","std": "Geo.9","q": "Which plot is most appropriate for displaying the exact individual values of 15 students' quiz scores?","a": "dot plot","lvl": 1,"choices": ["circle graph","two-way table","dot plot","scatter plot"],"steps": ["This is one numeric variable with a small set of 15 values.","Pick a plot that marks each single value on a number line.","Match that plot type to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-02","std": "Geo.9","q": "Which plot is most appropriate for representing the relationship between two quantitative variables, such as study hours and exam score?","a": "scatter plot","lvl": 1,"choices": ["scatter plot","box plot","dot plot","histogram"],"steps": ["Count the variables: study hours and exam score are two numbers.","Bivariate numeric data is plotted as (x, y) pairs on two axes.","Match that graph name to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-03","std": "Geo.9","q": "Which plot is most appropriate for showing the distribution of 500 continuous commute times grouped into 5-minute intervals?","a": "histogram","lvl": 1,"choices": ["scatter plot","bar graph","dot plot","histogram"],"steps": ["You have one large set of continuous numbers grouped into bins.","Recall which graph uses touching bars for number intervals.","Match that display name to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-04","std": "Geo.9","q": "A box plot of test scores has a minimum of 55, Q1 of 70, median of 80, Q3 of 90, and maximum of 98. What is the interquartile range (IQR)?","a": "20","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10","20","80","43"],"steps": ["IQR measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data.","Use the formula IQR = Q3 − Q1 with the given quartiles.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-05","std": "Geo.9","q": "A histogram of plant heights has bars for 0-10 cm (4 plants), 10-20 cm (9 plants), 20-30 cm (7 plants), and 30-40 cm (5 plants). How many plants are at least 20 cm tall?","a": "12","lvl": 2,"choices": ["25","13","12","7"],"steps": ["At least 20 cm means 20 cm or taller.","Identify the bars starting at 20 cm and add their counts.","Check your total against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-06","std": "Geo.9","q": "A researcher wants to compare the medians and spreads of test scores across 4 large schools (300 students each) on a single number line. Which display is most effective?","a": "side-by-side box plots","lvl": 2,"choices": ["a pie chart","four separate dot plots","a single scatter plot","side-by-side box plots"],"steps": ["Look for a plot that highlights medians and spreads for big groups.","Compare how cleanly four large groups stack on one number line.","Match that comparison display to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-07","std": "Geo.9","q": "A box plot has a minimum of 10, Q1 of 14, median of 16, Q3 of 22, and maximum of 45. What does this box plot indicate about the shape of the distribution?","a": "It is skewed right.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It is symmetric.","It is skewed left.","It is skewed right.","It is uniform."],"steps": ["Find the distance from the median to the min and to the max.","Compare the left whisker length to the right whisker length.","Name the skew after the longer tail and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-08","std": "Geo.9","q": "Why can you NOT determine the exact mean or exact individual data values from a histogram alone?","a": "A histogram groups data into intervals (bins), so individual values within each bin are not shown.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A histogram removes the largest and smallest values.","A histogram only shows the median and quartiles.","A histogram groups data into intervals (bins), so individual values within each bin are not shown.","A histogram only displays categorical data."],"steps": ["Think about what a histogram bar shows versus what it hides.","Each bar counts how many values fall in a range, not exact values.","Match that limitation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.9-09","std": "Geo.9","q": "A data set of 1,000 home prices has several multi-million-dollar mansions. If you use technology to create a box plot instead of a histogram, what key feature of the data might the box plot hide?","a": "Whether the distribution has two separate peaks (bimodal shape).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Whether the distribution has two separate peaks (bimodal shape).","The interquartile range.","The maximum home price.","The median home price."],"steps": ["Recall the five numbers a box plot shows: min, Q1, med, Q3, max.","Think about shape details inside the box that five numbers miss.","Pick the feature a box plot cannot reveal from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-01","std": "Geo.10","q": "Which pair of statistics is most appropriate for describing the center and variability of a symmetric data set with no outliers?","a": "mean and standard deviation","lvl": 1,"choices": ["mode and range","median and IQR","mean and standard deviation","median and standard deviation"],"steps": ["Symmetric data with no outliers does not pull the mean off center.","Pair the mean with its matching measure of spread.","Select that pair from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-02","std": "Geo.10","q": "Which pair of statistics is most appropriate for describing the center and variability of a strongly skewed data set?","a": "median and interquartile range","lvl": 1,"choices": ["mean and standard deviation","mean and range","median and interquartile range","mode and standard deviation"],"steps": ["Skewed data pulls the mean, so choose measures that resist pull.","Use the middle value for center and the middle 50% for spread.","Match that resistant pair to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-03","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "What is the population standard deviation of the data set {5, 5, 5, 5}?","a": "0","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2.5","0","1","5"],"steps": ["Standard deviation measures how far values spread from the mean.","Compare each number in {5, 5, 5, 5} to the set's mean.","Use that spread to pick the matching value from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-04","std": "Geo.10","q": "Class A has a mean score of 82 and standard deviation of 4. Class B has a mean score of 82 and standard deviation of 11. Both distributions are symmetric. Which statement is true?","a": "Class A's scores are more closely clustered around 82 than Class B's scores.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Class A's scores are more closely clustered around 82 than Class B's scores.","Class A has a wider spread of scores than Class B.","Class B has no scores below 82.","Class B has a higher average score than Class A."],"steps": ["Both classes have the same center, so compare their SD values.","A smaller standard deviation means scores stay closer to the mean.","Match that comparison to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-05","std": "Geo.10","part": "a","q": "How does calculating standard deviation differ from calculating mean absolute deviation (MAD)?","a": "Standard deviation squares the deviations from the mean, averages them, and takes the square root, whereas MAD averages the absolute values of the deviations.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Standard deviation uses the median instead of the mean.","Standard deviation ignores values below the mean.","MAD squares the deviations, while standard deviation uses absolute values.","Standard deviation squares the deviations from the mean, averages them, and takes the square root, whereas MAD averages the absolute values of the deviations."],"steps": ["Both start by finding how far each value sits from the mean.","Compare how each handles signs: absolute value versus squaring.","Remember SD finishes with a square root, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-06","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "Calculate the population standard deviation of the data set {2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9}.","a": "2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2","3.5","4","5"],"steps": ["Find the mean first, then measure each value's gap from the mean.","Square each gap, average all 8 squared gaps, then take the root.","Simplify the square root and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-07","std": "Geo.10","q": "Team 1's game scores are symmetric (mean 74, median 74, SD 5, IQR 7). Team 2's game scores are skewed right due to one high-scoring game (mean 76, median 71, SD 14, IQR 6). Which measures should be used to compare the two teams fairly?","a": "median and IQR, because Team 2's distribution is skewed","lvl": 3,"choices": ["range only, because both teams play the same sport","mean and SD, because Team 1 is symmetric","median and IQR, because Team 2's distribution is skewed","mean for Team 1 and median for Team 2"],"steps": ["Use the same pair of measures for both teams to compare fairly.","When either group is skewed, pick measures resistant to skew.","Match that pair and reason to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-08","std": "Geo.10","part": "a","q": "Why does standard deviation respond more strongly to an extreme outlier than mean absolute deviation (MAD) does?","a": "Because squaring a large deviation makes its contribution grow much faster than taking its absolute value.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because MAD is always larger than standard deviation.","Because squaring a large deviation makes its contribution grow much faster than taking its absolute value.","Because MAD ignores outliers completely.","Because standard deviation divides by the median."],"steps": ["Compare what MAD and SD do to one very large gap from the mean.","Test an example: compare |10| to 10² when a point is far out.","Match that effect of squaring to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.10-09","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "Calculate the population standard deviation of the data set {10, 12, 14, 16, 18}, rounded to the nearest hundredth.","a": "2.83","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2.40","3.16","8.00","2.83"],"steps": ["Find the mean of the 5 numbers, then find each deviation.","Square the 5 gaps, divide their sum by 5, then take the square root.","Round the square root to two decimals and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-01","std": "Geo.11","q": "A data set is {10, 12, 12, 14, 15, 100}. Which measure of center is most affected by the outlier 100?","a": "Mean","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Median","Interquartile range","Mode","Mean"],"steps": ["Compare how each measure responds to one very high number.","One measure adds all values together, so 100 pulls it up.","Name that pulled measure of center and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-02","std": "Geo.11","q": "A distribution of test scores is strongly skewed to the right. Which pair of statistics best describes its center and spread?","a": "Median and interquartile range","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Median and interquartile range","Mean and range","Mean and standard deviation","Mode and standard deviation"],"steps": ["Skewed data pulls averages, so pick resistant measures.","Use the middle value for center and middle 50% for spread.","Match that resistant center-and-spread pair to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-03","std": "Geo.11","q": "Adding an extreme high outlier to a symmetric data set will cause the standard deviation to do what?","a": "Increase","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Increase","Become zero","Decrease","Stay the same"],"steps": ["Standard deviation measures average distance from the mean.","Think about what happens to spread when a far-out point joins.","Decide how the spread changes and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-04","std": "Geo.11","q": "A data set is {4, 6, 8, 10, 12}. If the value 12 is replaced by 42, by how much does the mean increase?","a": "6","lvl": 2,"choices": ["30","5","14","6"],"steps": ["Find how much the total sum grows when 12 becomes 42.","Compute 42 − 12, then divide that change by the 5 values.","Finish the division and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-05","std": "Geo.11","q": "Two classes take the same quiz. Class A has a mean of 78 and a standard deviation of 4. Class B has a mean of 78 and a standard deviation of 11. Which statement is true?","a": "Class B's scores are more spread out from the mean than Class A's scores.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Every student in Class B scored higher than every student in Class A.","Class A has a higher median score than Class B.","Class B's scores are more spread out from the mean than Class A's scores.","Class A's scores are more spread out from the mean than Class B's scores."],"steps": ["Both classes have the same mean, so compare their SD values.","A larger standard deviation means scores spread farther out.","Match that comparison of spread to the correct choice."]},{"id": "Geo.11-06","std": "Geo.11","q": "Five daily temperatures (°F) are 68, 70, 71, 72, and 74. If a sixth day has a temperature of 40°F, how do the mean and median change?","a": "The mean decreases by about 5.2°F, while the median decreases by 0.5°F.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Both the mean and median decrease by about 5.2°F.","Neither the mean nor the median changes.","The mean decreases by about 5.2°F, while the median decreases by 0.5°F.","The median decreases by about 5.2°F, while the mean decreases by 0.5°F."],"steps": ["Find the original mean and median, then include 40°F.","New mean is 395 ÷ 6; new median averages the middle two.","Subtract to find both drops and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-07","std": "Geo.11","q": "A company's 9 employees have a mean salary of §50,000 and a median salary of §45,000. The CEO is added with a salary of §250,000. What is the new mean salary of all 10 people?","a": "§70,000","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§70,000","§65,000","§150,000","§75,000"],"steps": ["Find the combined salary total for all 10 people first.","Set up (9 × 50,000 + 250,000) ÷ 10.","Simplify to find the new mean and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-08","std": "Geo.11","q": "Data set X has 6 values with a mean of 20 and a standard deviation of 0. If a 7th value of 34 is added, what is the new mean?","a": "22","lvl": 3,"choices": ["24","22","27","21"],"steps": ["Use the original mean and count to find the starting sum.","Add 34 to 6 × 20, then divide the new total by 7.","Finish dividing and verify your result against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-09","std": "Geo.11","q": "In a right-skewed distribution of house prices with several multi-million-dollar mansions, how do the mansions affect the mean, the median, and the standard deviation?","a": "They pull the mean above the median and increase the standard deviation","lvl": 3,"choices": ["They pull the mean above the median and increase the standard deviation","They raise the mean and median equally and do not change the spread","They pull the median above the mean and increase the standard deviation","They pull the mean above the median and decrease the standard deviation"],"steps": ["Compare how extreme high values affect mean versus median.","Explain how distance from the mean changes standard deviation.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.12-01","std": "Geo.12","q": "On a scatter plot, as the number of hours studied increases, test scores generally increase in a straight-line pattern. What type of association is shown?","a": "Positive linear association","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Positive linear association","No association","Nonlinear association","Negative linear association"],"steps": ["Notice two clues: both variables rise, and the path is straight.","Rising together means positive; a straight path means linear.","Combine both terms and match your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-02","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "A linear model predicts y = 18 when x = 5, and the actual data point is (5, 21). What is the residual for this point?","a": "3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["39","5","3","−3"],"steps": ["A residual is actual y-value minus predicted y-value.","Set up 21 − 18 using the y-values at x = 5.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-03","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "When using technology to find the least-squares line of best fit, what quantity is being minimized?","a": "The sum of the squared residuals","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The sum of the x-values","The slope of the line","The number of points above the line","The sum of the squared residuals"],"steps": ["The name least-squares hints at what the method minimizes.","It squares each vertical gap (residual) and adds them up.","Match that minimized quantity to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-04","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "For the linear model y = 2x + 3, find the sum of the squared residuals for the three data points (1, 6), (2, 7), and (3, 7).","a": "5","lvl": 2,"choices": ["9","3","5","−1"],"steps": ["Find the predicted y for x = 1, 2, and 3 using y = 2x + 3.","Find each residual (actual − predicted), then square each one.","Add the three squared residuals and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-05","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "A residual plot for a linear model shows a clear U-shaped curved pattern. What does this indicate?","a": "A linear model is not a good fit; a nonlinear model would be better.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The linear model is an exact fit.","All residuals are positive.","A linear model is not a good fit; a nonlinear model would be better.","The correlation coefficient must be 1."],"steps": ["A good linear model leaves residuals randomly scattered.","A U-shaped curve shows a pattern the straight line missed.","Match what a curved residual pattern means to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-06","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "Find the least-squares line of best fit for the data points (1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7), and (4, 9).","a": "y = 2x + 1","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 2x + 1","y = 2x + 3","y = 0.5x + 2","y = 3x + 1"],"steps": ["Check if the points already lie on one exact straight line.","Find slope m = {(5 − 3)/(2 − 1)}, then use (1, 3) to find b.","Write y = mx + b and verify all points against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-07","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "Line A produces residuals of 2, −1, 3, and −2. Line B produces residuals of 1, −2, 2, and −1. Based on the sum of squared residuals (SSR), which line is a better fit and what is its SSR?","a": "Line B, with SSR = 10","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Line B, with SSR = 0","Line A, with SSR = 18","Line B, with SSR = 10","Line A, with SSR = 2"],"steps": ["Square every residual for each line, then sum each line's set.","Compare 2² + (−1)² + 3² + (−2)² to Line B's squared sum.","The smaller SSR is the better fit; check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-08","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "Use technology or the least-squares formulas to find the line of best fit for the data points (0, 2), (2, 4), (4, 10), and (6, 12).","a": "y = 1.8x + 1.6","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 1.6x + 1.8","y = 2x + 1","y = 1.5x + 2","y = 1.8x + 1.6"],"steps": ["Find the mean of x and mean of y, or use linear regression.","Use deviations from the means to get slope m, then find b.","Write y = mx + b and check it against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.12-09","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "A linear model has a residual of −4.5 at x = 10. What does this residual mean?","a": "The actual y-value is 4.5 units below the value the line predicts","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The line predicts a y-value of −4.5 at x = 10","The actual y-value is 4.5 units above the value the line predicts","The actual y-value is 4.5 units below the value the line predicts","The actual data point is 4.5 units to the left of the line"],"steps": ["Recall that residual = actual y − predicted y.","Interpret what a negative difference means for actual vs. line.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.13-01","std": "Geo.13","q": "Which correlation coefficient represents the strongest linear relationship?","a": "r = −0.89","lvl": 1,"choices": ["r = −0.89","r = 0.45","r = −0.15","r = 0.72"],"steps": ["Strength depends on how close |r| is to 1, not the sign.","Compare the absolute values: 0.72, 0.89, 0.45, and 0.15.","Pick the choice with the largest absolute value."]},{"id": "Geo.13-02","std": "Geo.13","q": "All points on a scatter plot lie exactly on a straight line with a negative slope. What is the correlation coefficient r?","a": "−1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0","0.5","1","−1"],"steps": ["Exact points on a line mean a perfect linear correlation.","Match the sign of r to the negative slope of the line.","Combine perfect strength and sign, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-03","std": "Geo.13","q": "Which correlation coefficient indicates almost no linear relationship between two variables?","a": "r = 0.04","lvl": 1,"choices": ["r = −0.95","r = 0.88","r = −0.76","r = 0.04"],"steps": ["Weak or no linear correlation has r very close to 0.","Ignore signs and find which choice is nearest to 0.","Verify your pick against the other choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-04","std": "Geo.13","q": "A study finds r = 0.86 between daily temperature and lemonade sales. How should this relationship be described?","a": "Strong positive linear correlation","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Weak positive linear correlation","Strong negative linear correlation","Strong positive linear correlation","No linear correlation"],"steps": ["Use the sign of r for direction and |r| for strength.","Check whether 0.86 is positive or negative, and near 1 or 0.","Match both direction and strength to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-05","std": "Geo.13","q": "Which value is NOT a possible correlation coefficient?","a": "r = 1.25","lvl": 2,"choices": ["r = 1.25","r = 0.99","r = 0","r = −1"],"steps": ["Recall the valid range for every correlation coefficient r.","Every valid r lies between −1 and 1, inclusive.","Identify the choice outside that range."]},{"id": "Geo.13-06","std": "Geo.13","q": "What is the correlation coefficient r for the data set {(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6), (4, 8)}?","a": "1","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1","0","2","0.5"],"steps": ["Check if all four points lie on one exact straight line.","Test whether y = 2x fits every point and check the slope sign.","Use the exact fit and sign to pick r from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-07","std": "Geo.13","q": "A scatter plot consists of the points (−3, 9), (−2, 4), (−1, 1), (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 4), and (3, 9), which lie on the parabola y = x². What is the correlation coefficient r?","a": "0, because r only measures the strength of a linear relationship","lvl": 3,"choices": ["0, because r only measures the strength of a linear relationship","−1, because the left side decreases","1, because the points follow an exact formula","0.5, because half the points go up and half go down"],"steps": ["Picture the symmetric U-shape of y = x² across the y-axis.","Notice the left side falls while the right side rises equally.","Recall what r measures and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-08","std": "Geo.13","q": "A bivariate data set has a correlation coefficient of r = 0.75. If every y-value is multiplied by 2 and then increased by 5, what is the new correlation coefficient?","a": "0.75","lvl": 3,"choices": ["1.00","0.375","0.75","1.50"],"steps": ["Think about whether scaling or shifting changes r.","Multiplying by a positive number and adding keeps z-scores equal.","Determine how r behaves under linear rescaling; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.13-09","std": "Geo.13","q": "A scatter plot has a correlation coefficient of r = −0.92. One extreme outlier is added far above the line on the right side of the graph. How will this outlier affect r?","a": "r will move closer to 0 (become less negative).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["r will stay at −0.92.","r will move closer to −1.","r will become greater than 1.","r will move closer to 0 (become less negative)."],"steps": ["Picture a downward-sloping line with one stray point high right.","Decide whether that stray point strengthens or weakens the line.","Match that shift in correlation strength to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.14-01","std": "Geo.14","q": "Which statement is true about correlation and causation?","a": "Correlation does not imply causation.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Correlation does not imply causation.","Strong correlation always proves causation.","Causation can exist only when r = 0.","Negative correlation means one variable causes the other to decrease."],"steps": ["Recall the core statistical rule linking two associated variables.","Two variables can move together without one causing the other.","Select the statement that reflects this rule."]},{"id": "Geo.14-02","std": "Geo.14","q": "Ice cream sales and sunburn cases both increase in July. What best explains this relationship?","a": "Correlation caused by a lurking variable (hot, sunny weather)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Eating ice cream causes sunburns.","There is no correlation between the two variables.","Getting a sunburn causes people to buy ice cream.","Correlation caused by a lurking variable (hot, sunny weather)"],"steps": ["Ask if ice cream causes sunburns or if a third factor drives both.","Think about what happens in July that raises both numbers.","Match that outside factor explanation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.14-03","std": "Geo.14","q": "Which pair of variables most likely shows a causal relationship?","a": "Increasing a car's constant speed and decreasing the time needed to drive 100 miles","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Shoe size and reading score in elementary school","Number of swimmers at a beach and daily air conditioner usage","Increasing a car's constant speed and decreasing the time needed to drive 100 miles","Number of umbrellas sold and number of car accidents"],"steps": ["Look for a direct physical link where changing x forces y to change.","Test which pair follows a real formula, not a lurking variable.","Rule out coincidence pairs and pick the causal choice."]},{"id": "Geo.14-04","std": "Geo.14","q": "A city finds a strong positive correlation (r = 0.91) between the number of firefighters sent to a fire and the amount of property damage. Does sending more firefighters cause more damage?","a": "No; larger fires both require more firefighters and cause more damage.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No; larger fires both require more firefighters and cause more damage.","Yes, because r = 0.91 is very close to 1.","No; the correlation must be negative for causation.","Yes, because firefighters always increase property damage."],"steps": ["Ask whether a high r alone proves one variable causes the other.","Look for a third factor that increases both trucks and damage.","Match that lurking-variable explanation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.14-05","std": "Geo.14","q": "A survey of students in grades K-8 finds a strong positive correlation between shoe size and reading test scores. What is the most likely lurking variable?","a": "Age or grade level","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Age or grade level","Shoe brand","Number of siblings","School lunch choice"],"steps": ["A lurking variable drives increases in both shoe size and reading.","Think about what grows together across grades K through 8.","Match that underlying factor to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.14-06","std": "Geo.14","q": "Which situation demonstrates causation rather than just correlation?","a": "Turning up a heater in a closed room increases the room's temperature.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Turning up a heater in a closed room increases the room's temperature.","People who own more books tend to live longer.","Months with more movie releases have higher electricity bills.","Cities with more hospitals have more traffic jams."],"steps": ["Causation means directly changing one thing produces the effect.","Check each choice for a direct physical cause, not a third factor.","Eliminate lurking-variable pairs and select the match."]},{"id": "Geo.14-07","std": "Geo.14","q": "An observational study finds a strong negative correlation between hours of sleep and number of typos made at work. Why is a controlled experiment needed to establish causation?","a": "Observational data cannot rule out lurking variables such as stress or workload.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Controlled experiments always produce r = 1.","Observational studies cannot calculate a correlation coefficient.","Negative correlations can never represent causation.","Observational data cannot rule out lurking variables such as stress or workload."],"steps": ["Compare what an observational study controls versus an experiment.","Think about outside factors that vary when you only observe.","Match that key limitation to the best choice."]},{"id": "Geo.14-08","std": "Geo.14","q": "A town notices that years with higher umbrella sales also have higher corn yields. Which statement best explains this relationship?","a": "It is correlation, not causation; more rainfall likely raises both","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Buying umbrellas causes corn to grow better","There is no correlation because umbrellas and corn are unrelated","Higher corn yields cause people to buy more umbrellas","It is correlation, not causation; more rainfall likely raises both"],"steps": ["Explain why buying umbrellas does not directly make corn grow.","Name the weather factor that increases both sales and crops.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.14-09","std": "Geo.14","q": "A researcher finds r = 0.88 between the number of coffee shops in a neighborhood and the average home price. Which conclusion is valid?","a": "Neighborhoods with more coffee shops tend to have higher home prices, but opening coffee shops does not necessarily cause home prices to rise.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["High home prices force residents to open coffee shops.","There is no relationship between coffee shops and home prices.","Neighborhoods with more coffee shops tend to have higher home prices, but opening coffee shops does not necessarily cause home prices to rise.","Opening a new coffee shop will directly raise nearby home prices."],"steps": ["Separate what the positive correlation shows from causal claims.","A strong r shows a trend, not proof that A causes B or B causes A.","Pick the choice that states the trend without claiming causation."]},{"id": "Geo.15-01","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A taxi fare is modeled by y = 2.5x + 4, where x is miles driven and y is total cost in dollars. What is the predicted cost for a 6-mile ride?","a": "§19","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§19","§39","§26.50","§15"],"steps": ["Plug the given distance, x = 6, into the cost equation.","Evaluate y = 2.5 × 6 + 4 using order of operations.","Add the results and check your dollar amount with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-02","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "In the linear model y = 3.5x + 20, where x is hours worked and y is total pay in dollars, what does the 20 represent?","a": "The base pay for 0 hours worked","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The base pay for 0 hours worked","The hourly wage","The number of hours worked","The total pay after 20 hours"],"steps": ["In y = mx + b, b is the starting value when x = 0.","Plug x = 0 hours into y = 3.5x + 20 to see what 20 means.","Match that starting-pay meaning to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-03","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "A candle's height h (in inches) after t hours is modeled by h = −0.75t + 12. What does the slope −0.75 represent?","a": "The candle burns down by 0.75 inches each hour.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The candle starts at 0.75 inches tall.","The candle grows by 0.75 inches each hour.","The candle burns down by 0.75 inches each hour.","The candle lasts 0.75 hours."],"steps": ["The slope shows how much height changes for each 1-hour step.","Notice the minus sign means the height goes down over time.","Match that hourly decrease to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-04","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "The linear model y = 4.2x + 55 predicts a student's test score y from x hours of studying. How many hours of studying predict a score of 76?","a": "5 hours","lvl": 2,"choices": ["18 hours","6 hours","4 hours","5 hours"],"steps": ["Substitute 76 for the score y and solve for study hours x.","Set up 76 = 4.2x + 55; subtract 55, then divide by 4.2.","Finish dividing and check your hours against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-05","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "A car's value V (in dollars) after t years is modeled by V = −1800t + 24000. Which interpretation is correct?","a": "The car starts at §24,000 when new and loses §1,800 in value each year.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The car loses §24,000 in value every 1,800 years.","The car starts at §24,000 when new and loses §1,800 in value each year.","The car is worth §0 after 1,800 years.","The car starts at §1,800 and gains §24,000 each year."],"steps": ["Identify the starting value (b) and yearly rate of change (m).","Check the value at t = 0 and what the negative slope means.","Match both parts of the interpretation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-06","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A gym charges a §35 sign-up fee plus §25 per month. What is the linear model for total cost C after m months, and what is the total cost for 8 months?","a": "C = 25m + 35; §235","lvl": 2,"choices": ["C = 25m + 35; §235","C = 60m; §480","C = 25m + 35; §200","C = 35m + 25; §305"],"steps": ["Put the monthly rate with m and add the one-time sign-up fee.","Write C = 25m + 35, then plug in m = 8.","Compute the total cost and match both parts to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-07","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A water tank contains 120 gallons at t = 0 minutes and 84 gallons at t = 6 minutes. Assuming a constant linear rate, after how many minutes will the tank be completely empty?","a": "20 minutes","lvl": 3,"choices": ["14 minutes","24 minutes","18 minutes","20 minutes"],"steps": ["Find the draining rate per minute using the two given points.","Write gallons = 120 + m × t, then set gallons equal to 0.","Solve for t and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-08","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "A linear model relating adult height x (in inches) to shoe size y is y = 0.5x − 24. Why does the y-intercept (−24) NOT have a meaningful real-world interpretation?","a": "An adult cannot have a height of 0 inches, and a negative shoe size is impossible.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because 24 is not a whole number of feet.","An adult cannot have a height of 0 inches, and a negative shoe size is impossible.","Because shoe sizes can only be modeled by nonlinear equations.","Because the slope is positive."],"steps": ["Remember the y-intercept occurs when x = 0 inches.","Ask whether x = 0 height or y = −24 shoe size makes sense.","Match that real-world limitation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.15-09","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "Company A charges y = 15x + 60 and Company B charges y = 20x + 35, where x is hours of labor and y is total cost in dollars. For how many hours do both companies charge the same amount, and what is that cost?","a": "5 hours; §135","lvl": 3,"choices": ["5 hours; §115","6 hours; §150","5 hours; §135","4 hours; §120"],"steps": ["Same cost means both equations give the same y for the same x.","Solve 15x + 60 = 20x + 35 for x, then plug x back in for y.","Verify both formulas give the same cost and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-01","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right circular cylinder is sliced by a plane parallel to its circular bases. What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Circle","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Triangle","Trapezoid","Circle","Rectangle"],"steps": ["Imagine slicing a soup can straight across, parallel to its lid.","A cut parallel to a prism or cylinder base matches the base.","Name that base shape and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-02","std": "Geo.16","q": "A rectangle is rotated 360° around one of its sides. What three-dimensional solid is generated?","a": "Cylinder","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Sphere","Rectangular prism","Cone","Cylinder"],"steps": ["Picture spinning a rectangular door all the way around its hinge.","The top and bottom edges sweep out two equal parallel circles.","Identify the solid formed and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-03","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right triangle is rotated 360° around one of its legs. What three-dimensional solid is generated?","a": "Cone","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Sphere","Cylinder","Cone","Pyramid"],"steps": ["Picture spinning a right triangle around one flat leg.","The other leg sweeps a circular base rising to a single tip.","Name that pointed 3D solid and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-04","std": "Geo.16","q": "A square pyramid is sliced by a plane perpendicular to its base that passes through the top vertex (apex). What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Triangle","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rectangle","Square","Triangle","Pentagon"],"steps": ["Picture slicing straight down from the peak to the square base.","Count the cut edges: one across the base and two sloped sides.","Name the polygon with those edges and match the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-05","std": "Geo.16","q": "A semicircle is rotated 360° around its straight diameter. What three-dimensional solid is formed?","a": "Sphere","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Cylinder","Sphere","Hemisphere","Cone"],"steps": ["Picture spinning a half-circle a full turn around its flat edge.","Every point on the curve stays the same distance from center.","Name the full round 3D solid and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-06","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right circular cylinder is sliced by a plane perpendicular to its bases (passing through both bases). What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Rectangle","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Ellipse","Rectangle","Circle","Triangle"],"steps": ["Picture cutting a cylinder straight down from top to bottom.","The cut makes two straight {top/bottom} chords and two tall sides.","Name that four-sided shape with right angles; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-07","std": "Geo.16","q": "A cube is sliced by a single flat plane that intersects all six faces of the cube. What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Hexagon","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Square","Pentagon","Octagon","Hexagon"],"steps": ["Each flat face the plane slices produces one straight edge.","Count how many edges form when a plane meets all 6 faces.","Name the polygon with that edge count and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-08","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right triangle with legs of length 6 cm and 8 cm is rotated 360° around the leg of length 8 cm. What are the radius and height of the resulting cone?","a": "Radius = 6 cm, height = 8 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Radius = 3 cm, height = 8 cm","Radius = 8 cm, height = 6 cm","Radius = 6 cm, height = 8 cm","Radius = 6 cm, height = 10 cm"],"steps": ["The leg on the rotation axis stays fixed as the cone's height.","The other leg swings around in a circle, forming the radius.","Assign 6 cm and 8 cm to radius and height; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.16-09","std": "Geo.16","q": "A rectangle with width 4 in. and height 10 in. is rotated 360° around a line parallel to its 10 in. side, located 2 in. outside the rectangle. What solid is formed?","a": "A hollow cylinder 10 in. tall, inner radius 2 in., outer radius 6 in.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A hollow cylinder 10 in. tall, inner radius 2 in., outer radius 4 in.","A cone 10 in. tall with radius 6 in.","A hollow cylinder 10 in. tall, inner radius 2 in., outer radius 6 in.","A solid cylinder 10 in. tall with radius 6 in."],"steps": ["Picture the gap between the axis and rectangle sweeping a hole.","Inner radius is the 2 in. gap; outer radius adds the 4 in. width.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.17-01","std": "Geo.17","q": "Find the volume of a right circular cylinder with radius 3 cm and height 10 cm. Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "90π cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60π cm³","90π cm³","30π cm³","300π cm³"],"steps": ["Use the cylinder volume formula V = πr²h.","Plug in r = 3 and h = 10: V = π × 3² × 10.","Square 3 first, multiply by 10, keep π, and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-02","std": "Geo.17","q": "Find the volume of a cone with radius 6 in. and height 5 in. Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "60π in.³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60π in.³","30π in.³","120π in.³","180π in.³"],"steps": ["Use the cone volume formula V = {1/3}πr²h.","Plug in r = 6 and h = 5: V = {1/3} × π × 6² × 5.","Compute the product, divide by 3, keep π, and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-03","std": "Geo.17","part": "a","q": "Two stacks of 20 identical coins have the same height. One stack is straight vertical and the other is slanted. By Cavalieri's Principle, how do their volumes compare?","a": "Their volumes are equal.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The slanted stack has a greater volume.","Their volumes cannot be compared without the slant angle.","Their volumes are equal.","The straight stack has a greater volume."],"steps": ["Cavalieri's Principle compares heights and slices at every level.","Both stacks have the same height and identical coin areas.","Apply the principle to compare volumes and pick a choice."]},{"id": "Geo.17-04","std": "Geo.17","part": "b","q": "A cylinder has a volume of 144π cm³ and a height of 9 cm. What is its radius?","a": "4 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4 cm","12 cm","16 cm","8 cm"],"steps": ["Start with V = πr²h and substitute V = 144π and h = 9.","Divide both sides by 9π to isolate r².","Take the positive square root and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-05","std": "Geo.17","q": "A composite solid consists of a cylinder of radius 3 m and height 6 m topped with a hemisphere of radius 3 m. What is the total volume in terms of π?","a": "72π m³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["90π m³","72π m³","54π m³","108π m³"],"steps": ["Find the cylinder volume and hemisphere volume, then add.","Set up π × 3² × 6 and {2/3}π × 3³ for the two parts.","Add both π terms and check your total against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-06","std": "Geo.17","part": "b","q": "A rectangular prism has a volume of 240 in.³, a length of 8 in., and a width of 5 in. What is its surface area?","a": "236 in.²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["236 in.²","118 in.²","216 in.²","240 in.²"],"steps": ["Find the missing height from V = l × w × h first.","Solve 240 = 8 × 5 × h, then plug into SA = 2(lw + lh + wh).","Add all six face areas and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-07","std": "Geo.17","q": "A solid cube with edge length 10 cm has a cylindrical hole of radius 2 cm drilled straight through its center from top to bottom. What is the remaining volume in terms of π?","a": "1000 − 40π cm³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["1000 − 20π cm³","1000 − 40π cm³","1000 − 160π cm³","600 − 40π cm³"],"steps": ["Subtract the drilled cylinder's volume from the cube's volume.","Set up 10³ − π × 2² × 10 using h = 10 for the hole.","Simplify each part, keep π exact, and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-08","std": "Geo.17","part": "b","q": "A sphere has a surface area of 100π cm². What is its volume in terms of π?","a": "(500/3) × π cm³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["500π cm³","(100/3) × π cm³","(500/3) × π cm³","(250/3) × π cm³"],"steps": ["Use SA = 4πr² to find the radius first.","Solve 100π = 4πr² for r, then plug r into V = {4/3}πr³.","Leave the fraction in terms of π and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.17-09","std": "Geo.17","part": "a","q": "An oblique triangular prism and a right triangular prism have the same base area B and the same perpendicular height h. Why do they have the same volume by Cavalieri's Principle?","a": "Every cross-section parallel to the base has area B at each height","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An oblique prism is a right prism that has been rotated","Both prisms have the same surface area","Their slanted edges have the same length","Every cross-section parallel to the base has area B at each height"],"steps": ["State the two conditions Cavalieri's Principle requires.","Explain why both prisms match in height and slice area B.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.18-01","std": "Geo.18","q": "A rectangle has vertices at (1, 2), (7, 2), (7, 5), and (1, 5). What is its perimeter?","a": "18","lvl": 1,"choices": ["24","21","18","9"],"steps": ["Find the horizontal width and vertical height from coordinates.","Subtract x-values (7 − 1) and y-values (5 − 2), then use 2l + 2w.","Add all four side lengths and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-02","std": "Geo.18","q": "A right triangle has vertices at (0, 0), (6, 0), and (0, 8). What is its area?","a": "24 square units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["24 square units","14 square units","10 square units","48 square units"],"steps": ["The legs lie along the x- and y-axes, giving base and height.","Plug b = 6 and h = 8 into A = {1/2}bh.","Multiply and divide by 2, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-03","std": "Geo.18","q": "Find the distance between the points A(1, 2) and B(4, 6).","a": "5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["√7","5","25","7"],"steps": ["Use the distance formula d = √{(x2 − x1)² + (y2 − y1)²}.","Plug in the points: √{(4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)²}.","Square, add, take the square root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-04","std": "Geo.18","q": "A triangle has vertices at A(0, 0), B(6, 0), and C(3, 4). What is its perimeter?","a": "16","lvl": 2,"choices": ["14","20","16","12"],"steps": ["Find all three side lengths, then add them for perimeter.","AB lies on the x-axis; use distance formula for AC and BC.","Sum all three sides and check your total against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-05","std": "Geo.18","q": "A parallelogram has vertices at (0, 0), (5, 0), (7, 4), and (2, 4). What is its area?","a": "20 square units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["14 square units","28 square units","20 square units","24 square units"],"steps": ["Use A = b × h with the horizontal base and vertical height.","Find base from (0, 0) to (5, 0) and height from y = 0 to y = 4.","Multiply base by height and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-06","std": "Geo.18","q": "A square has consecutive vertices at (0, 0), (3, 4), (−1, 7), and (−4, 3). What is its area?","a": "25 square units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["20 square units","12 square units","25 square units","50 square units"],"steps": ["For a square, area equals one side length squared (s²).","Find s² between (0, 0) and (3, 4): (3 − 0)² + (4 − 0)².","Simplify the sum (no root needed for s²) and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-07","std": "Geo.18","q": "Find the area of the triangle with vertices at A(1, 1), B(5, 2), and C(3, 6).","a": "9 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["8 square units","11 square units","18 square units","9 square units"],"steps": ["Enclose the triangle in a 4-by-5 box from (1, 1) to (5, 6).","Find the box area, then subtract the three corner right triangles.","Double-check each corner area and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-08","std": "Geo.18","q": "A parallelogram has consecutive vertices at (0, 0), (4, 0), (6, 3), and (2, 3). What is its exact perimeter?","a": "8 + 2√13","lvl": 3,"choices": ["8 + 2√13","12 + 2√13","14","8 + 2√5"],"steps": ["Opposite sides match, so find one flat side and one slanted side.","Flat side is 4 − 0; slanted side is √{(6 − 4)² + (3 − 0)²}.","Double both lengths, keep the root exact, and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.18-09","std": "Geo.18","q": "Find the area of the quadrilateral with consecutive vertices at (0, 2), (4, 0), (6, 4), and (2, 6).","a": "20 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["24 square units","40 square units","16 square units","20 square units"],"steps": ["Check consecutive side lengths and slopes to spot the shape.","Find (4 − 0)² + (0 − 2)² and check perpendicular slopes.","Compute base × height (or box minus corners) and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-01","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar rectangles have a scale factor of 3. If the smaller rectangle has a perimeter of 14 cm, what is the perimeter of the larger rectangle?","a": "42 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["28 cm","42 cm","17 cm","126 cm"],"steps": ["Perimeters of similar figures scale by the scale factor k.","Multiply the smaller perimeter by the scale factor: 14 × 3.","Finish multiplying and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-02","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar triangles have a scale factor of 2. If the area of the smaller triangle is 15 in.², what is the area of the larger triangle?","a": "60 in.²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60 in.²","30 in.²","45 in.²","120 in.²"],"steps": ["Areas of similar figures scale by the scale factor squared (k²).","Multiply the smaller area by 2²: 15 × 2².","Evaluate the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-03","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar cubes have a scale factor of 3. What is the ratio of the volume of the smaller cube to the volume of the larger cube?","a": "1:27","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1:81","1:9","1:3","1:27"],"steps": ["Volumes of similar 3D solids scale by the cube of k (k³).","Cube both parts of the 1:3 linear ratio: 1³ : 3³.","Simplify the cubed ratio and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-04","std": "Geo.19","q": "The ratio of the areas of two similar polygons is 16:49. What is the ratio of their perimeters?","a": "4:7","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4:7","64:343","16:49","8:24.5"],"steps": ["Area ratio equals (perimeter ratio)² for similar polygons.","Take the square root of each number: √{16} : √{49}.","Simplify both roots and check your ratio against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-05","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar cylinders have radii of 3 cm and 6 cm. If the smaller cylinder has a volume of 45π cm³, what is the volume of the larger cylinder?","a": "360π cm³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["360π cm³","180π cm³","720π cm³","90π cm³"],"steps": ["Find the linear scale factor k from the two radii first.","Cube k = {6/3}, then multiply 45π by k³.","Keep π in your volume and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-06","std": "Geo.19","q": "A scale model of a statue is built at a 1:5 scale. If the model requires 2 ounces of paint to cover its surface, how many ounces of paint are needed for the full-size statue?","a": "50 ounces","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10 ounces","25 ounces","50 ounces","250 ounces"],"steps": ["Paint covers surface area, which scales by k², not k.","Multiply the model's paint by 5²: 2 × 5².","Compute the ounces needed and match with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-07","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar cones have volumes of 24 cm³ and 648 cm³. If the surface area of the smaller cone is 30 cm², what is the surface area of the larger cone?","a": "270 cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["90 cm²","180 cm²","270 cm²","810 cm²"],"steps": ["Divide volumes to get k³, then find k, then square it for area.","Compute 648 ÷ 24 for k³, find k, then multiply 30 by k².","Finish the multiplication and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-08","std": "Geo.19","q": "A rectangular prism has a volume of 125 in.³. If all of its dimensions are multiplied by 2/5, what is the new volume?","a": "8 in.³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["50 in.³","16 in.³","20 in.³","8 in.³"],"steps": ["Scaling all three dimensions by k multiplies volume by k³.","Set up 125 × ({2/5})³ by cubing both 2 and 5.","Simplify the fraction product and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.19-09","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar pyramids have surface areas of 36 m² and 100 m². If the larger pyramid has a volume of 250 m³, what is the volume of the smaller pyramid?","a": "54 m³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["90 m³","54 m³","150 m³","32.4 m³"],"steps": ["Take square roots of the surface areas to find scale factor k.","Find √{36} ÷ √{100} for k, cube it, then multiply by 250.","Compute the smaller volume and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-01","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a radius of 6 cm. What is the length of an arc intercepted by a 90° central angle? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "3π cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["9π cm","3π cm","6π cm","1.5π cm"],"steps": ["Arc length is a fraction of circumference: {θ/360} × 2πr.","Plug in θ = 90 and r = 6: {90/360} × 2π × 6.","Simplify the fraction and multiply, keeping π; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-02","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a radius of 4 in. What is the area of a sector with a 90° central angle? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "4π in.²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["4π in.²","2π in.²","8π in.²","16π in.²"],"steps": ["Sector area is a fraction of circle area: {θ/360} × πr².","Plug in θ = 90 and r = 4: {90/360} × π × 4².","Simplify to exact form with π and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-03","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a circumference of 40 cm. What is the length of an arc with a central angle of 72°?","a": "8 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["16 cm","8 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"steps": ["Multiply the full circumference by the angle's fraction of 360°.","Set up {72/360} × 40 and reduce the fraction first.","Finish multiplying and check your length against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-04","std": "Geo.20","q": "Find the area of a sector of a circle with radius 12 m and a central angle of 60°. Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "24π m²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12π m²","4π m²","24π m²","72π m²"],"steps": ["Use sector area = {θ/360} × πr².","Plug in θ = 60 and r = 12: {60/360} × π × 12².","Reduce {60/360}, multiply by 144π, and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-05","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a radius of 9 cm. What is the length of an arc with a central angle of 120°? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "6π cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["3π cm","6π cm","27π cm","12π cm"],"steps": ["Use arc length = {θ/360} × 2πr.","Plug in θ = 120 and r = 9: {120/360} × 2π × 9.","Simplify {120/360} × 18π and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-06","std": "Geo.20","q": "A 16-inch diameter pizza is cut into 8 equal slices. What is the area of one slice in terms of π?","a": "8π in.²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4π in.²","2π in.²","8π in.²","32π in.²"],"steps": ["Cut the diameter in half to find the radius first.","Find full area π × r², then divide by 8 equal slices.","Keep π in your slice area and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-07","std": "Geo.20","q": "An arc of a circle has length 5π cm and a central angle of 75°. What is the radius of the circle?","a": "12 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["24 cm","12 cm","10 cm","6 cm"],"steps": ["Plug arc length and angle into s = {θ/360} × 2πr.","Set up 5π = {75/360} × 2πr and reduce {75/360} first.","Cancel π, solve for r, and check your radius with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-08","std": "Geo.20","q": "A sector of a circle has an area of 15π in.² and a radius of 10 in. What is the measure of its central angle in degrees?","a": "54°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["54°","27°","108°","60°"],"steps": ["Plug area and radius into A = {θ/360} × πr² to find θ.","Set up 15π = {θ/360} × 100π and divide both sides by π.","Solve for θ in degrees and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.20-09","std": "Geo.20","q": "A sector of a circle has an arc length of 4π cm and a radius of 8 cm. What is the area of the sector in terms of π?","a": "16π cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["64π cm²","16π cm²","8π cm²","32π cm²"],"steps": ["Find what fraction of the circle the arc covers, or use {1/2}rs.","Divide 4π by circumference 2π × 8, then multiply by π × 8².","Simplify in terms of π and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-01","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Apply the transformation rule (x, y) → (x + 4, y − 3) to the point (2, 5). What are the coordinates of the image?","a": "(6, 2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(6, 2)","(−2, 8)","(8, −15)","(6, 8)"],"steps": ["Replace x with 2 and y with 5 in the rule.","Add 4 to the x-value and subtract 3 from the y-value.","Write the new point (x, y) and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-02","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "Which of the following transformations does NOT preserve distance between points?","a": "Dilation with scale factor 2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Rotation 90° about the origin","Reflection across the y-axis","Translation 5 units left","Dilation with scale factor 2"],"steps": ["Rigid motions keep lengths the same; look for one that resizes.","Slides, flips, and turns keep size; scaling changes side lengths.","Match the size-changing transformation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-03","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Which function rule represents a reflection across the y-axis?","a": "(x, y) → (−x, y)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(x, y) → (x, −y)","(x, y) → (y, x)","(x, y) → (−x, y)","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],"steps": ["Flipping across the y-axis moves a point left or right.","The x-value changes sign, while the y-value stays the same.","Pick the rule that flips x and keeps y from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-04","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Point P(3, −2) is translated by (x, y) → (x − 5, y + 4) and then reflected across the x-axis. What are the final coordinates of the image?","a": "(−2, −2)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(−2, −2)","(2, 2)","(−2, 2)","(2, −2)"],"steps": ["Do the translation first, then reflect the new point.","Find (3 − 5, −2 + 4), then flip the sign of y across the x-axis.","Write the final ordered pair and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-05","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "Compare the transformations T1: (x, y) → (2x, 2y) and T2: (x, y) → (2x, y). Which statement is true?","a": "T1 preserves angle measure, while T2 does not.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["T1 preserves angle measure, while T2 does not.","Neither T1 nor T2 preserves angle measure.","T2 preserves angle measure, while T1 does not.","Both T1 and T2 preserve distance and angle measure."],"steps": ["Check whether x and y are scaled by the same factor.","Equal scaling keeps shape and angles; stretching one axis distorts.","Match how T1 and T2 treat angles to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-06","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "A composition translates a point by (x, y) → (x + 2, y − 1) and then by (x, y) → (x − 6, y + 5). What single function rule represents this composition?","a": "(x, y) → (x − 4, y + 4)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(x, y) → (x + 4, y − 4)","(x, y) → (x + 8, y + 6)","(x, y) → (x − 4, y + 4)","(x, y) → (x − 12, y − 5)"],"steps": ["Combine both x-shifts together and both y-shifts together.","Add +2 and −6 for the total x change, and −1 and +5 for y.","Write one rule with both totals and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-07","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Point A(4, 1) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the origin and then translated 3 units right and 2 units down. What are the final coordinates?","a": "(2, 2)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−4, 6)","(4, −6)","(2, 2)","(7, −1)"],"steps": ["Apply the 90° counterclockwise turn first, then shift.","Use (x, y) → (−y, x) on (4, 1), then add 3 to x and −2 to y.","Check your final (x, y) coordinates against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-08","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "A triangle undergoes the composition (x, y) → (x, −y) followed by (x, y) → (3x, 3y). How do the image's side lengths and angle measures compare to the preimage?","a": "Side lengths are multiplied by 3; angle measures remain unchanged.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Both side lengths and angle measures are multiplied by 3.","Side lengths are multiplied by 3; angle measures remain unchanged.","Both side lengths and angle measures remain unchanged.","Side lengths remain unchanged; angle measures are multiplied by 3."],"steps": ["Identify both moves: a reflection and a dilation by 3.","Recall how dilations scale lengths while keeping angles equal.","Match both side and angle effects to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.21-09","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Point B(−2, 5) is mapped to B'(2, 1) by a reflection across the y-axis followed by a translation. What is the combined rule for the composition?","a": "(x, y) → (−x, y − 4)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x, y) → (−x, y + 4)","(x, y) → (x + 4, y − 4)","(x, y) → (−x, y − 4)","(x, y) → (−x + 4, y − 4)"],"steps": ["Reflect (−2, 5) across the y-axis first.","Find the shift from that reflected point to (2, 1), then combine.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.22-01","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC has vertex A at (2, 3). What are the coordinates of A' after a reflection across the x-axis?","a": "(2, −3)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, −3)","(−2, 3)","(3, 2)","(−2, −3)"],"steps": ["Flipping across the x-axis moves a point up or down.","Keep x = 2 the same and change the sign of y = 3.","Write the new point A' and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-02","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "How many lines of symmetry does a regular pentagon have?","a": "5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["5","1","10","2"],"steps": ["A regular polygon has equal sides and equal angles.","Count how many fold lines match a 5-sided regular shape.","Match that number of symmetry lines to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-03","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Point (5, −1) is rotated 180° about the origin. What are the coordinates of its image?","a": "(−5, 1)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(1, 5)","(−5, 1)","(5, 1)","(−1, −5)"],"steps": ["A 180° turn about the origin sends (x, y) to (−x, −y).","Change the signs of both 5 and −1.","Write the new ordered pair and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-04","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "Segment AB has endpoints A(1, 2) and B(4, 2). Segment A'B' has endpoints A'(−1, 5) and B'(−4, 5). Which sequence of transformations maps AB onto A'B'?","a": "Reflection across the y-axis, then translation 3 units up","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Reflection across the y-axis, then translation 3 units up","Translation 2 units left and 3 units up","Reflection across the x-axis, then translation 3 units up","Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin"],"steps": ["Compare x-values and y-values of A to A' and B to B'.","Notice x-values change sign while y-values each increase by 3.","Match that flip and vertical shift to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-05","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "What is the smallest angle of rotational symmetry (greater than 0°) for a regular octagon?","a": "45°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["45°","60°","36°","90°"],"steps": ["A regular octagon has 8 equal sides and turns onto itself 8 times.","Divide a full 360° turn by the number of sides: 360° ÷ 8.","Finish dividing and check your angle against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-06","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Point P(−3, 4) is rotated 90° clockwise about the origin. What are the coordinates of P'?","a": "(4, 3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(3, −4)","(−4, −3)","(−4, 3)","(4, 3)"],"steps": ["Use the 90° clockwise rule: (x, y) → (y, −x).","Swap −3 and 4, then change the sign of the original x-value.","Check your signs and match the new point to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-07","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "A triangle with vertices (1, 1), (3, 1), and (1, 4) is mapped onto a triangle with vertices (−1, −1), (−1, −3), and (−4, −1). Which single transformation maps the first triangle onto the second?","a": "Reflection across the line y = −x","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflection across the line y = x","Reflection across the line y = −x","Rotation 180° about the origin","Reflection across the y-axis"],"steps": ["Track how each (x, y) pair changes from the first set to the next.","Compare (3, 1) to (−1, −3) and (1, 4) to (−4, −1) to find the rule.","Identify which single reflection uses that rule; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-08","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "Which quadrilateral always has 180° rotational symmetry about its center but does NOT necessarily have any lines of symmetry?","a": "Parallelogram","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Kite","Parallelogram","Rhombus","Isosceles trapezoid"],"steps": ["Test each shape for half-turn symmetry and fold lines.","Eliminate shapes with fold lines, or shapes that fail a 180° turn.","Pick the shape that fits both rules from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.22-09","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "Which sequence of two rigid motions maps triangle DEF with D(1, 2), E(4, 2), F(1, 6) onto triangle D'E'F' with D'(−1, −4), E'(−4, −4), F'(−1, −8)?","a": "Rotate 180° about the origin, then translate 2 units down","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflect across the x-axis, then translate 2 units down","Rotate 180° about the origin, then translate 2 units up","Rotate 180° about the origin, then translate 2 units down","Reflect across the y-axis, then translate 6 units down"],"steps": ["Compare how both x and y signs flip from DEF to D'E'F'.","Try a turn or flip first, then find the shift that finishes the job.","Verify all three vertices map accurately with your two steps."]},{"id": "Geo.23-01","std": "Geo.23","q": "In a reflection across line m, how is line m related to the segment connecting a point P (not on m) and its image P'?","a": "Line m is the perpendicular bisector of segment PP'.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Line m is congruent to segment PP'.","Line m passes through P and P'.","Line m is parallel to segment PP'.","Line m is the perpendicular bisector of segment PP'."],"steps": ["A reflection places P' straight across line m at the same distance.","That means line m cuts segment PP' in half at a 90° angle.","Match that geometric term to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-02","std": "Geo.23","q": "In a translation of a figure, how are the line segments connecting each preimage point to its corresponding image point related?","a": "They are parallel and equal in length.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["They are parallel and equal in length.","They form concentric circles.","They all intersect at a single center point.","They are perpendicular and equal in length."],"steps": ["A translation slides every point the same distance and direction.","Same direction means parallel; same distance means equal length.","Pick the statement matching both facts from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-03","std": "Geo.23","q": "In a rotation about a fixed center O by an angle of 60°, every point P (other than O) moves along what geometric path?","a": "A circular arc centered at O with radius OP","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A line perpendicular to segment OP","A circular arc centered at O with radius OP","A straight line segment parallel to a given vector","A line passing through O"],"steps": ["During a rotation, distance from center O never changes.","Points at a fixed distance from O trace part of a circle.","Match that curved path description to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-04","std": "Geo.23","q": "Point P is reflected across line m to produce image P'. If the perpendicular distance from P to line m is 7 cm, what is the length of segment PP'?","a": "14 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["3.5 cm","7 cm","49 cm","14 cm"],"steps": ["Both P and P' are the same distance from the mirror line m.","Add the distance from P to m and from m to P': 7 + 7.","Find the total length of PP' and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-05","std": "Geo.23","q": "Under a rotation of 65° counterclockwise about center O, point A maps to A'. If OA = 9 cm, what are the length of OA' and the measure of angle AOA'?","a": "OA' = 9 cm and angle AOA' = 65°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["OA' = 65 cm and angle AOA' = 9°","OA' = 18 cm and angle AOA' = 65°","OA' = 9 cm and angle AOA' = 65°","OA' = 9 cm and angle AOA' = 115°"],"steps": ["A rotation keeps distance from the center unchanged.","Match OA' to OA and angle AOA' to the given turn angle.","Check both values together against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-06","std": "Geo.23","q": "Triangle ABC is translated so that A maps to A' and B maps to B'. If AA' = 12 units, what is true about segment BB'?","a": "BB' = 12 units and segment BB' is parallel to segment AA'.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["BB' = 6 units and segment BB' is parallel to segment AA'.","BB' = 12 units and segment BB' is perpendicular to segment AA'.","BB' = 24 units and segment BB' intersects segment AA'.","BB' = 12 units and segment BB' is parallel to segment AA'."],"steps": ["A slide moves every vertex by the exact same vector.","Compare the length and direction of BB' to segment AA'.","Match both the length and parallel fact to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-07","std": "Geo.23","q": "Point P(2, 5) is reflected across a line m to P'(8, 5). What is the equation of the line of reflection m?","a": "x = 5","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 3","y = 5","x = 6","x = 5"],"steps": ["Line m is the perpendicular bisector of segment PP'.","Find the midpoint of (2, 5) and (8, 5), and note PP' is flat.","Write the vertical line through that midpoint; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-08","std": "Geo.23","q": "A figure is reflected across line j and then reflected across line k, where j and k are parallel lines 6 units apart. What single transformation is equivalent to this composition?","a": "A translation of 12 units perpendicular to lines j and k","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A translation of 12 units perpendicular to lines j and k","A translation of 6 units perpendicular to lines j and k","A rotation of 180° about a point between j and k","A translation of 12 units parallel to lines j and k"],"steps": ["Test one point reflecting across two parallel lines 6 units apart.","Two flips across parallel lines slide by 2 × the gap, at 90°.","Find the distance and direction, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.23-09","std": "Geo.23","q": "Which is a precise definition of a rotation of a point P by an angle θ about a center O?","a": "P maps to P' so that OP = OP' and angle POP' measures θ","lvl": 3,"choices": ["P maps to P' so that PP' = θ and O is the midpoint of PP'","P maps to P' so that OP' = θ × OP","P maps to P' so that OP = OP' and angle POP' measures θ","P maps to P' on the line OP so that angle POP' measures θ"],"steps": ["State two facts linking P and P' to center O: distance and angle.","Describe the circle with radius OP and the directed angle POP'.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.24-01","std": "Geo.24","q": "In terms of rigid motions, when are two geometric figures congruent?","a": "When a sequence of translations, rotations, and/or reflections maps one figure onto the other","lvl": 1,"choices": ["When they have the same number of sides","When they have the same area","When a dilation maps one figure onto the other","When a sequence of translations, rotations, and/or reflections maps one figure onto the other"],"steps": ["Congruent figures have the exact same size and shape.","Recall which moves preserve size and map one shape onto another.","Match that rigid-motion definition to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-02","std": "Geo.24","q": "Which of the following transformations is NOT a rigid motion?","a": "Dilation","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Translation","Reflection","Rotation","Dilation"],"steps": ["Rigid motions keep all lengths and angle measures the same.","Identify which transformation enlarges or shrinks a figure.","Pick the non-rigid transformation from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-03","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle ABC is translated 4 units right to form triangle A'B'C'. If AB = 7 cm and angle C = 50°, what are A'B' and angle C'?","a": "A'B' = 7 cm and angle C' = 50°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A'B' = 11 cm and angle C' = 50°","A'B' = 28 cm and angle C' = 50°","A'B' = 7 cm and angle C' = 50°","A'B' = 7 cm and angle C' = 54°"],"steps": ["A translation is a rigid motion, so size and angles stay equal.","Match side A'B' to AB and angle C' to angle C without adding 4.","Check both unchanged measures against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-04","std": "Geo.24","q": "Rectangle ABCD has vertices (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 2), and (0, 2). Rectangle EFGH has vertices (3, 1), (7, 1), (7, 3), and (3, 3). Are the rectangles congruent, and why?","a": "Yes, because a translation 3 units right and 1 unit up maps ABCD onto EFGH.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because a translation 3 units right and 1 unit up maps ABCD onto EFGH.","No, because their perimeters are different.","Yes, because a dilation of scale factor 3 maps ABCD onto EFGH.","No, because their vertices are in different locations."],"steps": ["Compare each vertex of ABCD to its matching vertex in EFGH.","Check if adding the same (x, y) shift maps every corner over.","Match your conclusion and rigid motion to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-05","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle PQR has vertices P(1, 1), Q(4, 1), and R(1, 5). Triangle STU has vertices S(−1, 1), T(−1, 4), and U(−5, 1). Which single rigid motion shows they are congruent?","a": "Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rotation 180° about the origin","Translation 2 units left","Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin","Reflection across the y-axis"],"steps": ["Compare P(1, 1) to S(−1, 1), Q(4, 1) to T(−1, 4), and R to U.","Notice every point follows the rule (x, y) → (−y, x).","Match that coordinate rule to the right motion in the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-06","std": "Geo.24","q": "Two equilateral triangles both have angles of 60°, 60°, and 60°, but one has side length 4 cm and the other has side length 8 cm. Are they congruent?","a": "No, because no sequence of rigid motions can change side lengths.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because all their angles are congruent.","Yes, because a dilation of scale factor 2 maps one onto the other.","No, because equilateral triangles cannot be rotated.","No, because no sequence of rigid motions can change side lengths."],"steps": ["Congruent figures must match in both angles and side lengths.","Ask whether rigid motions can turn a 4 cm side into an 8 cm one.","Pick Yes or No with the matching reason from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-07","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(2, 1), B(5, 1), and C(2, 3). Triangle DEF has vertices D(2, −3), E(5, −3), and F(2, −5). Which sequence of rigid motions maps ABC onto DEF?","a": "Reflection across the x-axis, then translation 2 units down","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflection across the y-axis, then translation 4 units down","Translation 4 units down","Rotation 180° about the origin","Reflection across the x-axis, then translation 2 units down"],"steps": ["Notice x stays the same, while C is above AB and F is below DE.","Flip ABC over the x-axis first, then find the shift to DEF.","Test your sequence on all three vertices and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-08","std": "Geo.24","q": "A student claims that any two figures with the same area and the same perimeter must be congruent. Is this claim true?","a": "No, because two non-congruent shapes can have the same area and perimeter without a rigid motion mapping one to the other.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Yes, because rigid motions preserve area and perimeter.","No, because rigid motions do not preserve perimeter.","Yes, because only congruent shapes can have equal areas.","No, because two non-congruent shapes can have the same area and perimeter without a rigid motion mapping one to the other."],"steps": ["Think about whether equal area and perimeter force the same shape.","Compare a triangle and a rectangle with matching area and perimeter.","Tie your decision to rigid motions and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.24-09","std": "Geo.24","q": "Segment JK has endpoints J(0, 2) and K(3, 6). Segment LM has endpoints L(0, −2) and M(−3, −6). Which single rigid motion maps JK onto LM?","a": "A 180° rotation about the origin, (x, y) → (−x, −y)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A reflection across the y-axis, (x, y) → (−x, y)","A reflection across the x-axis, (x, y) → (x, −y)","A translation 4 units down, (x, y) → (x, y − 4)","A 180° rotation about the origin, (x, y) → (−x, −y)"],"steps": ["Look at how the signs of x and y change from J to L and K to M.","Test which rule sends J(0, 2) to L(0, −2) and K(3, 6) to M(−3, −6).","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.25-01","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "If triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DEF, which side must be congruent to side BC?","a": "Side EF","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Side EF","Side DE","Side DF","Side AC"],"steps": ["Letter order in a congruence statement shows matching parts.","B and C are the 2nd and 3rd letters; match them in DEF.","Name the matching side and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-02","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Which of the following is NOT a valid criterion to prove two triangles are congruent?","a": "AAA","lvl": 1,"choices": ["SAS","AAA","ASA","SSS"],"steps": ["Valid triangle congruence rules are SSS, SAS, ASA, AAS, and HL.","Check which choice only guarantees same shape, not same size.","Pick the invalid congruence rule from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-03","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In triangles ABC and XYZ, AB = XY, angle B = angle Y, and BC = YZ. Which congruence criterion proves triangle ABC is congruent to triangle XYZ?","a": "SAS","lvl": 1,"choices": ["AAS","SAS","ASA","SSS"],"steps": ["List the matching parts in order around each triangle.","Notice angle B sits right between sides AB and BC.","Name the side-angle-side pattern and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-04","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "Triangle PQR is congruent to triangle LMN. If angle P = 42° and angle M = 68°, what is the measure of angle R?","a": "70°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["70°","68°","42°","110°"],"steps": ["Match letters in order: angle Q equals angle M.","Triangle angles sum to 180°, so set up 180° − (42° + 68°).","Subtract to find angle R and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-05","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In triangles ABC and DEF, angle A = angle D, angle B = angle E, and BC = EF. Which criterion proves triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DEF?","a": "AAS","lvl": 2,"choices": ["SAS","AAS","ASA","SSS"],"steps": ["Check whether side BC lies between angles A and B or not.","Angles A and B are followed by non-included side BC.","Match that angle-angle-side order to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-06","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Why is SSA (Side-Side-Angle) not a general triangle congruence criterion?","a": "Two non-congruent triangles can have two congruent sides and a congruent non-included angle.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Because SSA only applies to equilateral triangles.","Two non-congruent triangles can have two congruent sides and a congruent non-included angle.","Because the angles of a triangle do not always add to 180°.","Because side lengths cannot be compared to angles."],"steps": ["Picture swinging the free side like a hinge from the top corner.","It can hit the base in two spots, making two different triangles.","Match that counterexample idea to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-07","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DEF. If AB = 3x + 2, DE = 5x − 8, and BC = 2x + 1, what is the length of EF?","a": "11","lvl": 3,"choices": ["11","17","5","21"],"steps": ["Match corresponding sides: AB = DE and EF = BC.","Solve 3x + 2 = 5x − 8 for x, then plug x into 2x + 1.","Finish finding EF (not just x) and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-08","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Triangles ABD and CBD share side BD. If angle ABD = angle CBD and angle ADB = angle CDB, which criterion proves triangle ABD is congruent to triangle CBD?","a": "ASA","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SAS","ASA","SSS","AAS"],"steps": ["Shared side BD is congruent to itself by the reflexive property.","Check where BD sits relative to angles ABD and ADB.","Match the included-side pattern to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.25-09","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Triangles ABC and DEF have AB = DE, angle A = angle D, and AC = DF. Which sequence of rigid motions verifies SAS congruence?","a": "Translate A to D, rotate AB onto DE, then reflect AC onto DF if needed","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflect ABC across line BC, then translate C to E","Translate B to F, then rotate until angle A lands on angle D","Dilate ABC by DE/AB, then translate A to D","Translate A to D, rotate AB onto DE, then reflect AC onto DF if needed"],"steps": ["Plan rigid motions to map A → D, then B → E, then C → F.","Use the given side and angle matches to place each vertex in turn.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.26-01","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "A line segment of length 6 cm is dilated by a scale factor of 3. What is the length of the image segment?","a": "18 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2 cm","54 cm","9 cm","18 cm"],"steps": ["Dilation multiplies segment lengths by the scale factor.","Set up 6 × 3 to find the new length.","Multiply and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-02","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "A line m does NOT pass through the center of dilation O. Under a dilation with scale factor 2, how is the image line m' related to line m?","a": "Line m' is parallel to line m.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Line m' is perpendicular to line m.","Line m' intersects line m at O.","Line m' is the same line as m.","Line m' is parallel to line m."],"steps": ["Dilations keep slopes and direction the same for every line.","Since m misses center O, the image line shifts without turning.","Name the relation between m and m' and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-03","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "A line k passes directly through the center of dilation O. Under a dilation with scale factor 4, what is the image of line k?","a": "Line k itself (unchanged)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A line perpendicular to k","A line parallel to k, 4 units away","Line k itself (unchanged)","A single point at O"],"steps": ["Dilation moves points straight along rays from center O.","When line k already contains O, every point stays on line k.","Match what happens to line k to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-04","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Point A(4, −6) is dilated with center at the origin and scale factor 1/2. What are the coordinates of A'?","a": "(2, −3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4.5, −5.5)","(−2, 3)","(8, −12)","(2, −3)"],"steps": ["With center (0, 0), multiply both coordinates by {1/2}.","Set up (4 × {1/2}, −6 × {1/2}).","Simplify both coordinates and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-05","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "The line y = 2x + 3 is dilated with center at the origin and scale factor 4. What is the equation of the image line?","a": "y = 2x + 12","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 8x + 12","y = 2x + 12","y = 2x + 7","y = 8x + 3"],"steps": ["Dilating about (0, 0) keeps the slope and scales the y-intercept.","Keep slope 2, and multiply y-intercept 3 by scale factor 4.","Write y = mx + b and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-06","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Segment PQ has length 15 in., and its dilated image P'Q' has length 10 in. What is the scale factor of the dilation?","a": "2/3","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1/5","3/2","2/3","5"],"steps": ["Scale factor is image length divided by preimage length.","Write the fraction {10/15} and reduce it to lowest terms.","Check your reduced fraction against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-07","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "The line y = −3x is dilated with center at the origin and scale factor 5. What is the equation of the image line?","a": "y = −3x","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = −3x","y = −3x − 5","y = −3x + 5","y = −15x"],"steps": ["Find where the center of the dilation is.","Recall what a dilation does to a line through its center.","Write the image line equation and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-08","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Point P(5, 7) is dilated with center C(1, 3) and scale factor 3. What are the coordinates of P'?","a": "(13, 15)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(13, 15)","(15, 21)","(16, 18)","(12, 12)"],"steps": ["Measure how far P is from center C(1, 3), not from (0, 0).","Multiply (5 − 1, 7 − 3) by 3, then add back to C(1, 3).","Finish finding the new (x, y) and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.26-09","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "Line m has equation y = x − 2. It is dilated with center at the origin and scale factor 3. What is the image line m', and how is it related to m?","a": "y = x − 6, parallel to m","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 3x − 2, intersecting m","y = x − 6, parallel to m","y = 3x − 6, intersecting m","y = x − 2, the same line as m"],"steps": ["Check if y = x − 2 hits (0, 0), then scale its y-intercept by 3.","Keep slope 1 unchanged and explain why equal slopes mean parallel.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.27-01","std": "Geo.27","q": "What is a similarity transformation?","a": "A dilation or a composition of a dilation with one or more rigid motions","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A reflection followed by a rotation only","A dilation or a composition of a dilation with one or more rigid motions","Any sequence of translations only","Any transformation that changes angle measures"],"steps": ["Similar shapes keep the same shape, even if their size changes.","Combine rigid motions with a dilation to map similar figures.","Match that definition to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-02","std": "Geo.27","q": "Circle A has center (0, 0) and radius 4. Circle B has center (0, 0) and radius 12. What single transformation maps Circle A onto Circle B?","a": "Dilation centered at (0, 0) with scale factor 3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Translation 8 units right","Dilation centered at (0, 0) with scale factor 8","Dilation centered at (0, 0) with scale factor 3","Dilation centered at (0, 0) with scale factor 1/3"],"steps": ["Both circles share center (0, 0), so only the radius changes.","Divide the new radius by the old radius: 12 ÷ 4.","Match the dilation and scale factor to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-03","std": "Geo.27","q": "Are all squares similar to each other?","a": "Yes, because rigid motions and a dilation can map any square onto any other square.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because squares can be rotated differently.","Yes, because all squares are congruent.","Yes, because rigid motions and a dilation can map any square onto any other square.","No, because squares can have different side lengths."],"steps": ["All squares have four 90° angles and four equal sides.","Rigid motions line them up, and a dilation matches side lengths.","Choose Yes or No with the right reason from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-04","std": "Geo.27","q": "Circle P has center (2, 1) and radius 3. Circle Q has center (5, −1) and radius 6. Which similarity transformation maps Circle P onto Circle Q?","a": "Translation by (x, y) → (x + 3, y − 2), then dilation centered at (5, −1) with scale factor 2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Translation by (x, y) → (x + 3, y − 2), then dilation centered at (5, −1) with scale factor 3","Translation by (x, y) → (x + 3, y − 2), then dilation centered at (5, −1) with scale factor 2","Dilation centered at (2, 1) with scale factor 1/2, then translation by (x, y) → (x + 3, y − 2)","Translation by (x, y) → (x − 3, y + 2), then dilation with scale factor 2"],"steps": ["First slide center (2, 1) to (5, −1), then resize the radius.","Find shift (5 − 2, −1 − 1) and scale factor 6 ÷ 3.","Match both the translation and dilation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-05","std": "Geo.27","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices (0, 0), (2, 0), and (0, 3). Triangle DEF has vertices (0, 0), (6, 0), and (0, 9). Are they similar, and what is the scale factor from ABC to DEF?","a": "Yes; scale factor 3","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes; scale factor 3","Yes; scale factor 2","No; they are not similar.","Yes; scale factor 1/3"],"steps": ["Check if one multiplier maps every ABC vertex to DEF.","Divide corresponding non-zero coordinates: 6 ÷ 2 and 9 ÷ 3.","Confirm both ratios match and pick the answer from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-06","std": "Geo.27","q": "Rectangle R has length 6 and width 4. Rectangle S has length 10 and width 6. Are the two rectangles similar?","a": "No, because the ratios of corresponding sides (4/6 and 6/10) are not equal.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No, because they have different areas.","No, because the ratios of corresponding sides (4/6 and 6/10) are not equal.","Yes, because you can add 2 to the width and 4 to the length.","Yes, because all rectangles have four right angles."],"steps": ["Similar rectangles must have equal ratios of matching sides.","Compare {4/6} and {6/10} by reducing both fractions.","Decide if the ratios match and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-07","std": "Geo.27","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 1), B(3, 1), and C(1, 2). Triangle DEF has vertices D(−2, 2), E(−6, 2), and F(−2, 4). Which sequence of transformations maps ABC onto DEF?","a": "Reflection across the y-axis, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflection across the y-axis, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2","Rotation 180° about the origin, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2","Translation 3 units left, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2","Reflection across the x-axis, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2"],"steps": ["Notice x-values flip sign and every coordinate doubles in size.","Name the reflection that flips x, and the dilation scale factor.","Test your sequence on all three vertices and check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-08","std": "Geo.27","q": "Square ABCD has vertices (0, 0), (2, 0), (2, 2), and (0, 2). Square EFGH has vertices (4, 1), (4, 7), (−2, 7), and (−2, 1). What is the scale factor from ABCD to EFGH?","a": "3","lvl": 3,"choices": ["6","4","3","2"],"steps": ["Find one side length of each square using consecutive vertices.","Divide the side length of EFGH (7 − 1) by ABCD's side (2 − 0).","Simplify the ratio and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.27-09","std": "Geo.27","q": "Circle 1 has center (−1, 4) and radius 5. Circle 2 has center (3, 2) and radius 2. Which similarity transformation maps Circle 1 onto Circle 2?","a": "Translate by (x + 4, y − 2), then dilate about (3, 2) by 2/5","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Translate by (x + 4, y − 2), then dilate about (3, 2) by 2/5","Dilate about the origin by 2/5 only","Translate by (x + 4, y − 2), then dilate about (3, 2) by 5/2","Translate by (x − 4, y + 2), then dilate about (3, 2) by 2/5"],"steps": ["Move Circle 1's center to Circle 2's center, then scale radius.","Find the shift between centers and divide new radius by old radius.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.28-01","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Which of the following is a valid criterion for proving two triangles are similar?","a": "AA","lvl": 1,"choices": ["SS","AA","SSA","AS"],"steps": ["Similar triangles need matching angles or proportional sides.","Check which choice alone guarantees matching shapes.","Select the valid similarity criterion from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-02","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF. If angle A = 35° and angle B = 80°, what is the measure of angle F?","a": "65°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["115°","35°","65°","80°"],"steps": ["Corresponding angles of similar triangles are equal, so F = C.","Find angle C using 180° − (35° + 80°).","Finish subtracting to get angle F and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-03","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Triangle ABC has side lengths 3, 4, and 5. Triangle DEF has side lengths 9, 12, and 15. Which criterion proves the triangles are similar?","a": "SSS similarity","lvl": 1,"choices": ["AA similarity","SSS similarity","ASA congruence","SAS similarity"],"steps": ["Only side lengths are given, so test their ratios in order.","Compare {9/3}, {12/4}, and {15/5} to see if all three match.","Name the three-side similarity rule and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-04","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF. If AB = 6, DE = 9, and BC = 8, what is the length of EF?","a": "12","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12","10","11","15"],"steps": ["Corresponding sides of similar triangles form equal ratios.","Set up the proportion {6/9} = {8/EF} and cross-multiply.","Solve for EF and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-05","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "In triangles PQR and STU, PQ = 4, PR = 6, ST = 10, SU = 15, and angle P = angle S = 50°. Are the triangles similar, and by which criterion?","a": "Yes, by SAS similarity","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, by AA similarity","Yes, by SAS similarity","No, they are not similar.","Yes, by SSS similarity"],"steps": ["Check if the included angles match and their sides are in ratio.","Compare {10/4} and {15/6}, and note P and S lie between them.","Match your conclusion and rule to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-06","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Triangle JKL has angles measuring 48° and 62°. Triangle MNO has angles measuring 62° and 70°. Are the two triangles similar?","a": "Yes, by AA similarity","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No, because 48° is not equal to 70°.","Yes, by AA similarity","Yes, by SAS similarity","No, because no side lengths are given."],"steps": ["Find the missing third angle in triangle JKL before deciding.","Compute 180° − (48° + 62°), then compare all angles to MNO.","Check if at least two angles match and pick a choice."]},{"id": "Geo.28-07","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF. If AB = x + 2, DE = 12, BC = 5, and EF = 10, what is the value of x?","a": "4","lvl": 3,"choices": ["3","6","22","4"],"steps": ["Match corresponding sides AB to DE and BC to EF.","Set up {(x + 2)/12} = {5/10} and solve for x.","Plug x back into the ratio to verify, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-08","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "In triangle ABC, segment DE is drawn parallel to side BC with D on AB and E on AC. Which criterion proves triangle ADE is similar to triangle ABC?","a": "AA similarity","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SSS similarity","AA similarity","ASA congruence","SAS similarity"],"steps": ["Look for equal angles made by parallel lines and shared vertex A.","Parallel lines give equal corresponding angles; both share A.","Identify which similarity rule uses two angles; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.28-09","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Why is the AA criterion enough to prove two triangles are similar?","a": "Angles sum to 180°, so the third angles match; a dilation then gives ASA","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Two pairs of equal angles force all sides to be equal","AA works only for right triangles, where the third angle is 90°","AA also requires one pair of proportional sides to be checked","Angles sum to 180°, so the third angles match; a dilation then gives ASA"],"steps": ["Start with why two matching angles force the third to match.","Use a dilation to match one side, then link to ASA congruence.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.29-01","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "Using geometry software, a student draws several different triangles and measures their three interior angles. What conjecture should the student make about the sum of the interior angles?","a": "The sum of the interior angles is always 180°.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The sum of the interior angles is always 90°.","The sum depends on the side lengths of the triangle.","The sum of the interior angles is always 180°.","The sum of the interior angles is always 360°."],"steps": ["Recall the constant sum of all three angles inside any triangle.","Check whether changing side lengths ever changes that angle sum.","Match that constant angle sum to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-02","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "Which set of properties is sufficient to guarantee that a quadrilateral is a rectangle?","a": "A parallelogram with one right angle","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A quadrilateral with one right angle","A quadrilateral with two congruent sides","A parallelogram with one right angle","A quadrilateral with perpendicular diagonals"],"steps": ["A rectangle needs four right angles, not just one.","Test which property set forces all four corners to be 90°.","Pick the choice that guarantees four right angles."]},{"id": "Geo.29-03","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "When constructing the perpendicular bisector of a segment AB with a compass and straightedge, what is true of every point on the bisector?","a": "It is equidistant from endpoints A and B.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["It forms an equilateral triangle with A and B.","It is closer to A than to B.","It lies on segment AB.","It is equidistant from endpoints A and B."],"steps": ["Think about the equal compass arcs drawn from A and from B.","Every point on the bisector forms matching right triangles to A, B.","Match that equal-distance property to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-04","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "A student constructs a quadrilateral whose diagonals are perpendicular and bisect each other. Which figure is guaranteed to be formed?","a": "Rhombus","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rectangle","Rhombus","Trapezoid","Kite with no parallel sides"],"steps": ["Diagonals bisecting each other guarantee a parallelogram.","Adding perpendicular diagonals forces all four sides equal.","Name the parallelogram with four equal sides; check choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-05","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "A student uses dynamic geometry software to drag the vertices of a parallelogram and measures its diagonals. Which conjecture is ALWAYS true?","a": "The diagonals bisect each other.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The diagonals bisect the vertex angles.","The diagonals bisect each other.","The diagonals are congruent.","The diagonals are perpendicular."],"steps": ["Picture dragging a parallelogram into a long, slanted shape.","Eliminate properties true only for rectangles or rhombuses.","Pick the diagonal rule that holds for every parallelogram."]},{"id": "Geo.29-06","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "Which set of properties precisely defines a square?","a": "A quadrilateral with four congruent sides and four right angles","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides","A quadrilateral with four right angles","A quadrilateral with four congruent sides","A quadrilateral with four congruent sides and four right angles"],"steps": ["A square is both a rhombus and a rectangle at the same time.","Combine all-equal-sides with all-right-angles in one choice.","Rule out partial definitions and pick the match from choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-07","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "A student constructs the midpoints of all four sides of several different irregular quadrilaterals and connects them in order. What shape is always formed by connecting the midpoints?","a": "A parallelogram","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A trapezoid","A rectangle","A square","A parallelogram"],"steps": ["Draw a diagonal to split the quadrilateral into two triangles.","By the midsegment theorem, opposite midpoint segments stay parallel.","Name the shape with two parallel pairs and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-08","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "A quadrilateral has diagonals that are congruent and bisect each other, but are NOT perpendicular. What is the most specific name for this quadrilateral?","a": "Rectangle","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rhombus","Isosceles trapezoid","Square","Rectangle"],"steps": ["Bisecting diagonals mean parallelogram; equal ones mean right angles.","Non-perpendicular diagonals rule out a rhombus or square.","Pick the most specific matching shape from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.29-09","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "A student draws a circle with diameter AB and places point C anywhere else on the circle to form triangle ABC. What should the student conjecture about angle ACB?","a": "It is always 90°; dragging C around the circle keeps it at 90°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It is always 45°; dragging C around the circle keeps it at 45°","It is always 90°; dragging C around the circle keeps it at 90°","It is always 180° because AB is a diameter","It changes as C moves, so no conjecture can be made"],"steps": ["Recall the inscribed angle theorem for a 180° semicircle arc.","State the constant measure of angle ACB and a way to drag C.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.30-01","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which of the following is considered an undefined term in Euclidean geometry?","a": "Point","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Circle","Point","Line segment","Angle"],"steps": ["Undefined terms are the basic building blocks not defined by others.","Test each choice: can you define it using simpler geometric words?","Pick the term that cannot be broken down further from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-02","std": "Geo.30","q": "What is the precise definition of a circle?","a": "The set of all points in a plane that are a given distance from a given point","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A round shape with no corners","A closed curve made of straight segments","The set of all points in space at a given distance from a line","The set of all points in a plane that are a given distance from a given point"],"steps": ["A precise definition uses points, a plane, and fixed distance.","Distinguish a flat 2D circle from a 3D sphere or vague words.","Match that exact point-and-distance rule to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-03","std": "Geo.30","q": "What is the precise definition of line segment AB?","a": "Points A and B together with all points on line AB that lie between A and B","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A ray starting at A and passing through B","The distance between A and B","Points A and B together with all points on line AB that lie between A and B","A line that goes on forever in both directions"],"steps": ["A segment stops at two endpoints and includes everything between.","Rule out endless lines, one-way rays, and plain distance numbers.","Pick the exact definition using points A and B from choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-04","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which statement precisely defines perpendicular lines?","a": "Two lines that intersect to form right (90°) angles","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Two lines that intersect to form right (90°) angles","Two lines that lie in different planes","Two lines that never intersect","Two lines that intersect at any angle"],"steps": ["Perpendicular lines cross at a very specific angle.","Look for intersecting lines that form a special square corner.","Select that precise definition from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-05","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which statement precisely defines parallel lines?","a": "Two coplanar lines that do not intersect","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Two lines that do not intersect","Two lines that intersect at a 180° angle","Two line segments of equal length","Two coplanar lines that do not intersect"],"steps": ["Parallel lines never meet AND must lie in the same flat plane.","Without the same plane, non-crossing lines could be skew.","Pick the choice that includes both non-crossing and same plane."]},{"id": "Geo.30-06","std": "Geo.30","q": "What is the precise definition of an angle?","a": "The union of two rays that share a common endpoint","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The space between two lines","The union of two rays that share a common endpoint","A corner of a polygon","Two line segments that cross each other"],"steps": ["Think of the geometric parts that form the two sides of an angle.","Avoid informal words like corner or space; use formal terms.","Match the precise geometric definition to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-07","std": "Geo.30","q": "Why must the precise definition of parallel lines include the word 'coplanar'?","a": "Because two lines in three-dimensional space can fail to intersect without being parallel (skew lines).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Because two lines in three-dimensional space can fail to intersect without being parallel (skew lines).","Because perpendicular lines are not coplanar.","Because lines can only exist in two dimensions.","Because coplanar lines always intersect."],"steps": ["Picture two lines in 3D space, like floor and ceiling edges.","Think about skew lines that never touch yet are not parallel.","Match that 3D counterexample to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-08","std": "Geo.30","q": "How is the radian measure of a central angle defined using distance along a line and distance around a circular arc?","a": "The ratio of the length of the intercepted circular arc to the radius of the circle","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The ratio of the length of the intercepted circular arc to the radius of the circle","The difference between the circumference and the diameter","The product of the arc length and the radius","The ratio of the radius to the diameter"],"steps": ["Radian measure compares arc length s to the circle's radius r.","Recall the relationship θ = {s/r} as a ratio of two lengths.","Match that ratio wording to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.30-09","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which is a precise definition of line segment AB?","a": "Points A and B and all points on line AB that lie between them","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The distance between points A and B","A straight piece of a line","Points A and B and all points on line AB that lie between them","All points on line AB, including points beyond A and B"],"steps": ["Point out what 'piece' leaves vague, such as exact endpoints.","Decide which choice says exactly which points belong to the segment.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.31-01","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Two vertical angles have measures (3x + 10)° and 70°. What is the value of x?","a": "20","lvl": 1,"choices": ["15","20","26.7","30"],"steps": ["Vertical angles are congruent, so their angle measures are equal.","Set up 3x + 10 = 70, then subtract 10 from both sides.","Divide both sides by 3 to find x, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-02","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "In triangle ABC, segment DE connects the midpoints of sides AB and AC. If BC = 18 cm, what is the length of DE?","a": "9 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["9 cm","6 cm","18 cm","36 cm"],"steps": ["A triangle midsegment is half the length of the side parallel to it.","Set up DE = {1/2} × 18 (or 18 ÷ 2) using the parallel side BC.","Compute the length of DE and check your result against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-03","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "In parallelogram ABCD, angle A measures 65°. What is the measure of consecutive angle B?","a": "115°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["115°","90°","130°","65°"],"steps": ["Consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary (sum to 180°).","Set up 65° + angle B = 180°, then rewrite as 180° − 65°.","Subtract to find angle B and check that both angles add to 180°."]},{"id": "Geo.31-04","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. A pair of alternate interior angles measure (4x − 15)° and (2x + 25)°. What is the measure of each angle?","a": "65°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["65°","20°","115°","85°"],"steps": ["Alternate interior angles between parallel lines are congruent.","Set 4x − 15 = 2x + 25 and solve the equation for x.","Plug x back into 2x + 25 to get the angle, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-05","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "An isosceles triangle has a vertex angle of 40°. What is the measure of each base angle?","a": "70°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["70°","140°","50°","40°"],"steps": ["Triangle angles sum to 180°, and base angles of an isosceles triangle match.","Subtract the vertex angle from 180°: 180° − 40° = 140° for both bases.","Divide by 2 to find one base angle, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-06","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "The diagonals of parallelogram ABCD intersect at point E. If AE = 3x − 4 and EC = x + 10, what is the total length of diagonal AC?","a": "34","lvl": 2,"choices": ["34","7","17","40"],"steps": ["Diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so AE = EC.","Solve 3x − 4 = x + 10 for x, then substitute to find AE and EC.","Add AE + EC (or double AE) for AC, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-07","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "In triangle ABC, a line parallel to side BC intersects side AB at D and side AC at E. If AD = 6, DB = 4, and AE = 9, what is the length of EC?","a": "6","lvl": 3,"choices": ["6","7","13.5","4"],"steps": ["A line parallel to one side divides the other two sides proportionally.","Write the proportion {6/4} = {9/EC} using matching side segments.","Cross-multiply to solve for EC, then check your result with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-08","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "Rhombus ABCD has diagonals of length 12 cm and 16 cm. What is the length of one side of the rhombus?","a": "10 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["28 cm","14 cm","20 cm","10 cm"],"steps": ["Rhombus diagonals bisect each other at 90°, forming four right triangles.","Halve each diagonal to get the legs, then apply a² + b² = c².","Take the square root to find the side length and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.31-09","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Angles 1 and 2 form a linear pair, and angles 2 and 3 form a linear pair. Which reasoning proves that angle 1 is congruent to angle 3?","a": "m∠1 + m∠2 = 180 = m∠2 + m∠3, so subtracting m∠2 gives m∠1 = m∠3","lvl": 3,"choices": ["m∠1 + m∠3 = 180, so the angles are congruent","Angles 1 and 3 are adjacent, so they are congruent","All angles in a linear pair measure 90°","m∠1 + m∠2 = 180 = m∠2 + m∠3, so subtracting m∠2 gives m∠1 = m∠3"],"steps": ["Recall that angles in a linear pair are supplementary and sum to 180°.","Set the two 180° angle sums equal using the transitive property.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.32-01","std": "Geo.32","q": "What is the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints A(2, 4) and B(8, 10)?","a": "(5, 7)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(3, 3)","(6, 6)","(10, 14)","(5, 7)"],"steps": ["The midpoint averages the x-coordinates and averages the y-coordinates.","Set up x = {(2 + 8)/2} and y = {(4 + 10)/2}.","Simplify each fraction to write (x, y), then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-02","std": "Geo.32","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(0, 0), B(6, 0), and C(3, 5). Which calculation proves that triangle ABC is isosceles?","a": "AC = √34 and BC = √34","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Midpoint of AB is (3, 0)","AC = √34 and BC = √34","AB = 6 and AC = 6","Slope of AC = 5/3 and slope of BC = 5/3"],"steps": ["An isosceles triangle has at least two sides of equal length.","Use distance √{(x2 − x1)² + (y2 − y1)²} on sides AC and BC.","Compare the two lengths and match your calculation to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-03","std": "Geo.32","q": "Does the point (3, 4) lie on the circle centered at the origin (0, 0) with radius 5?","a": "Yes, because 3² + 4² = 5².","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Yes, because 3 + 4 > 5.","Yes, because 3² + 4² = 5².","No, because 3 + 4 = 7, not 5.","No, because 3² + 4² = 49."],"steps": ["A point lies on a circle at the origin if x² + y² = r² holds.","Substitute x = 3, y = 4, and r = 5 into x² + y² and r².","Compare both sides to decide yes or no, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-04","std": "Geo.32","q": "Quadrilateral ABCD has vertices A(0, 0), B(4, 0), C(5, 3), and D(1, 3). Which statement algebraically proves that ABCD is a parallelogram?","a": "Slope of AB = slope of CD = 0, and slope of AD = slope of BC = 3.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The diagonals are perpendicular.","Slope of AB = slope of CD = 0, and slope of AD = slope of BC = 3.","Slope of AB = 0 and slope of AD = 1/3.","AB = 4 and BC = 4."],"steps": ["A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel (equal slopes).","Compute slope {(y2 − y1)/(x2 − x1)} for AB, CD, AD, and BC.","Compare opposite side slopes and match the valid proof to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-05","std": "Geo.32","q": "A circle centered at the origin passes through the point (1, √3). What is the radius of the circle?","a": "2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["√2","2","4","1 + √3"],"steps": ["The radius is the distance from the center (0, 0) to any point on the circle.","Use r = √{(1 − 0)² + (√{3} − 0)²} = √{1 + 3}.","Simplify the square root to get the radius and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-06","std": "Geo.32","q": "Triangle PQR has vertices P(0, 0), Q(4, 2), and R(2, 6). At which vertex is there a right angle?","a": "Vertex Q","lvl": 2,"choices": ["There is no right angle.","Vertex P","Vertex R","Vertex Q"],"steps": ["A right angle forms where two sides meet with perpendicular slopes.","Find the slopes of PQ, QR, and PR using {(y2 − y1)/(x2 − x1)}.","Find the pair whose product is −1, identify their shared vertex, and check."]},{"id": "Geo.32-07","std": "Geo.32","q": "Quadrilateral JKLM has vertices J(0, 0), K(3, 4), L(8, 4), and M(5, 0). What is the most specific name for JKLM?","a": "Rhombus","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Square","Trapezoid","Rectangle","Rhombus"],"steps": ["Test side lengths and adjacent slopes to classify the quadrilateral.","Use the distance formula on all four sides and check if angles are 90°.","Pick the most specific shape matching those facts and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-08","std": "Geo.32","q": "A circle centered at (2, −1) passes through (5, 3). Does the point (−1, 3) also lie on this circle?","a": "Yes, because its distance from (2, −1) is 5, equal to the radius.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["No, because its distance from (2, −1) is √13.","No, because (−1, 3) is in Quadrant II.","Yes, because its distance from (2, −1) is 5, equal to the radius.","Yes, because its distance from (2, −1) is 25, equal to the radius."],"steps": ["Compare the point's distance from the center to the circle's radius.","Find the radius from (2, −1) to (5, 3), then the distance to (−1, 3).","Match the distances and select the true statement from the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.32-09","std": "Geo.32","q": "A rectangle has vertices A(0, 0), B(a, 0), C(a, b), and D(0, b). Which work proves the diagonals are congruent?","a": "AC = √(a² + b²) and BD = √(a² + b²), so AC = BD","lvl": 3,"choices": ["AC = √(a² + b²) and BD = √(a² − b²), so AC = BD","AC = a + b and BD = a + b, so AC = BD","The slopes of AC and BD are equal, so AC = BD","AC = √(a² + b²) and BD = √(a² + b²), so AC = BD"],"steps": ["Identify the diagonal endpoints: AC connects A to C, and BD connects B to D.","Write the distance formula for AC and for BD in terms of a and b.","Simplify both expressions to show they match and check your conclusion."]},{"id": "Geo.33-01","std": "Geo.33","q": "What is the slope of any line parallel to y = 3x − 7?","a": "3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["−3","3","−1/3","7"],"steps": ["Parallel lines always have the exact same slope.","In y = mx + b, identify the slope m as the coefficient of x.","Read off that slope value and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-02","std": "Geo.33","q": "What is the slope of any line perpendicular to y = (2/5)x + 1?","a": "−5/2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["5/2","2/5","−2/5","−5/2"],"steps": ["Perpendicular slopes are opposite reciprocals (flip and change sign).","Start with the given slope {2/5}, flip the fraction, and negate it.","Check that the two slopes multiply to −1, then match to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-03","std": "Geo.33","q": "What is the slope of the line passing through (1, 2) and (3, 8)?","a": "3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["6","2","1/3","3"],"steps": ["Slope measures rise over run using m = {(y2 − y1)/(x2 − x1)}.","Substitute the points to get m = {(8 − 2)/(3 − 1)}.","Subtract, simplify the fraction, and check your result with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-04","std": "Geo.33","q": "Write the equation of the line parallel to y = −2x + 5 that passes through the point (3, 1).","a": "y = −2x + 7","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = (1/2)x − 1/2","y = −2x + 7","y = −2x + 1","y = −2x − 5"],"steps": ["Use the same slope as the given line because the lines are parallel.","Plug m = −2 and (3, 1) into y = mx + b: 1 = −2 × 3 + b.","Solve for b, write y = mx + b, and check that (3, 1) satisfies it."]},{"id": "Geo.33-05","std": "Geo.33","q": "Write the equation of the line perpendicular to y = (1/3)x − 4 that passes through the point (2, 1).","a": "y = −3x + 7","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = −3x + 5","y = (1/3)x + 1/3","y = 3x − 5","y = −3x + 7"],"steps": ["Find the opposite reciprocal of {1/3} for the perpendicular slope.","Substitute that slope and (2, 1) into y = mx + b to solve for b.","Write the final equation and verify (2, 1) works before choosing."]},{"id": "Geo.33-06","std": "Geo.33","q": "Line 1 passes through (0, 2) and (4, 5). Line 2 passes through (1, 6) and (4, 2). How are Line 1 and Line 2 related?","a": "Perpendicular","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Perpendicular","Parallel","Neither parallel nor perpendicular","Same line"],"steps": ["Find the slope of each line with rise over run.","Compute m1 = {(5 − 2)/(4 − 0)} and m2 = {(2 − 6)/(4 − 1)}.","Check if m1 = m2 or m1 × m2 = −1, then match your answer to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-07","std": "Geo.33","q": "Find the value of k so that the line passing through (2, 3) and (6, k) is perpendicular to the line y = 2x − 1.","a": "1","lvl": 3,"choices": ["11","−5","1","5"],"steps": ["Find the perpendicular slope needed by taking the opposite reciprocal of 2.","Set {(k − 3)/(6 − 2)} equal to −{1/2} and clear the denominators.","Solve for k and verify the slope back into the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-08","std": "Geo.33","q": "Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of the segment with endpoints A(2, 1) and B(6, 5).","a": "y = −x + 7","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = −x + 7","y = −x + 3","y = x − 1","y = x + 7"],"steps": ["A perpendicular bisector hits the midpoint at a right angle to AB.","Find the midpoint of AB and take the opposite reciprocal of slope AB.","Use the midpoint and new slope to find b, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.33-09","std": "Geo.33","q": "A line through the origin and (a, b) is rotated 90° counterclockwise. How does this show perpendicular slopes have a product of −1?","a": "(a, b) maps to (−b, a), so the slopes are b/a and −a/b; product −1","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(a, b) maps to (−a, −b), so both slopes are b/a","(a, b) maps to (−b, a), so the slopes are b/a and b/a; product −1","(a, b) maps to (−b, a), so the slopes are b/a and −a/b; product −1","(a, b) maps to (b, −a), so the slopes are b/a and a/b; product 1"],"steps": ["Pick a point (a, b) on a line through (0, 0) and write its slope {b/a}.","Apply a 90° counterclockwise rotation: (a, b) maps to (−b, a).","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.34-01","std": "Geo.34","q": "A 6-foot-tall person casts a 4-foot shadow. At the same time, a nearby tree casts a 20-foot shadow. How tall is the tree?","a": "30 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30 feet","48 feet","13.3 feet","22 feet"],"steps": ["Sunlight forms similar right triangles, so height-to-shadow ratios match.","Set up the proportion {6/4} = {h/20} with h as the tree's height.","Cross-multiply to solve for h, then check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-02","std": "Geo.34","q": "To measure the distance across a pond, a surveyor stakes out two congruent triangles on land using SAS. If the side corresponding to the pond's width measures 85 m, what is the width of the pond?","a": "85 m","lvl": 1,"choices": ["85 m","42.5 m","170 m","Cannot be determined"],"steps": ["Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent (CPCTC).","Match the pond's width to its corresponding measured side on land.","Use that equal length directly and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-03","std": "Geo.34","q": "A 10-foot flagpole casts a 5-foot shadow. At the same time, a building casts a 35-foot shadow. How tall is the building?","a": "70 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["175 feet","40 feet","17.5 feet","70 feet"],"steps": ["Similar right triangles keep the ratio of height to shadow constant.","Write the proportion {10/5} = {h/35} for the building height h.","Solve for h and check that your height makes sense among the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-04","std": "Geo.34","q": "A mirror is placed flat on the ground 12 feet from a streetlamp and 3 feet from a person whose eyes are 5 feet above the ground. When the person sees the top of the lamp in the mirror, how tall is the streetlamp?","a": "20 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["36 feet","7.2 feet","20 feet","14 feet"],"steps": ["Equal reflection angles create two similar right triangles at the mirror.","Match height to ground distance in a proportion: {5/3} = {h/12}.","Solve for h and verify your result against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-05","std": "Geo.34","q": "A bridge truss is built from identical triangular steel frames, each having side lengths 5 m, 12 m, and 13 m. Which congruence criterion guarantees all the frames are congruent?","a": "SSS","lvl": 2,"choices": ["SAS","ASA","SSS","AAA"],"steps": ["Identify which triangle parts are given: three sides or angles.","Because all three side lengths match on every frame, pick the matching rule.","Confirm AAA does not prove congruence, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-06","std": "Geo.34","q": "Two similar triangular parks have a scale factor of 1:4 from the smaller park to the larger park. If the perimeter of the smaller park is 180 m, what is the perimeter of the larger park?","a": "720 m","lvl": 2,"choices": ["45 m","360 m","2,880 m","720 m"],"steps": ["Perimeters of similar figures scale by the same factor as their sides.","Multiply the smaller perimeter by the scale factor: 180 × 4.","Compute the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-07","std": "Geo.34","q": "A surveyor uses two similar right triangles to find the width of a river. The smaller triangle on land has legs of 15 m and 20 m. The larger triangle has a leg of 45 m corresponding to the 15 m leg. What is the width of the river (the leg corresponding to 20 m)?","a": "60 m","lvl": 3,"choices": ["30 m","75 m","60 m","50 m"],"steps": ["Find the scale factor between corresponding legs of the similar triangles.","Set up {15/45} = {20/w} using matching legs to solve for width w.","Calculate w and verify your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-08","std": "Geo.34","q": "A wheelchair ramp rises 2 feet vertically over a horizontal run of 16 feet. A second ramp built with the same slope rises 3.5 feet vertically. What is its horizontal run?","a": "28 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["32 feet","28 feet","17.5 feet","24 feet"],"steps": ["Equal slopes mean the ramps form similar right triangles.","Set up the rise-to-run proportion {2/16} = {3.5/x} for run x.","Solve for x and check that your run is reasonable in the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.34-09","std": "Geo.34","q": "A 5.5-foot-tall student stands 18 feet from the base of a lamppost and casts a 6-foot shadow away from the lamppost. How tall is the lamppost?","a": "22 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["16.5 feet","22 feet","23.5 feet","19.6 feet"],"steps": ["Find the full base of the large triangle by adding 18 + 6.","Set up the similar-triangle proportion {h/24} = {5.5/6}.","Solve for h and verify both triangles share the same height-to-base ratio."]},{"id": "Geo.35-01","std": "Geo.35","part": "a","q": "A 45°-45°-90° right triangle has legs of length 7 cm. What is the exact length of its hypotenuse?","a": "7√2 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["7 cm","7√2 cm","14 cm","7√3 cm"],"steps": ["In a 45°-45°-90° triangle, sides follow the ratio x : x : x√{2}.","Multiply the leg length 7 by √{2} to get the hypotenuse.","Keep the exact radical form with units and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-02","std": "Geo.35","part": "b","q": "In right triangle ABC with right angle at C, AC = 3, BC = 4, and AB = 5. What is sin(A)?","a": "4/5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["4/3","4/5","3/5","3/4"],"steps": ["Recall that sin(A) equals the opposite side divided by the hypotenuse.","Across from angle A is BC = 4, and the hypotenuse across from C is AB = 5.","Write the fraction {opposite/hypotenuse} and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-03","std": "Geo.35","part": "c","q": "If sin(32°) = cos(x°) for an acute angle x, what is the value of x?","a": "58","lvl": 1,"choices": ["148","58","68","32"],"steps": ["Sine and cosine of complementary angles are equal: sin(A) = cos(90° − A).","Set up x = 90 − 32 using the given angle measure.","Subtract to find x and verify both angles add to 90° in the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-04","std": "Geo.35","part": "a","q": "In a 30°-60°-90° right triangle, the hypotenuse has length 12 in. What is the exact length of the longer leg (opposite the 60° angle)?","a": "6√3 in.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["6√2 in.","6 in.","6√3 in.","12√3 in."],"steps": ["In a 30°-60°-90° triangle, sides follow the ratio x : x√{3} : 2x.","Divide hypotenuse 12 by 2 for the short leg x, then multiply by √{3}.","Write the exact longer leg with units and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-05","std": "Geo.35","part": "d","q": "A triangle has side lengths 9 cm, 12 cm, and 15 cm. Use the converse of the Pythagorean Theorem to determine whether it is a right triangle.","a": "Yes, because 9² + 12² = 15².","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because 9 + 12 > 15.","Yes, because 9² + 12² = 15².","No, because 9² + 12² = 21².","No, because 9 + 12 is not equal to 15."],"steps": ["Test whether a² + b² = c² using the longest side as c.","Compute 9² + 12² and compare that sum to 15².","Decide if the two sides match, then pick the matching choice."]},{"id": "Geo.35-06","std": "Geo.35","part": "b","q": "In a right triangle, the side opposite acute angle A has length 5 and the side adjacent to angle A has length 12. What is tan(A)?","a": "5/12","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12/13","5/13","5/12","12/5"],"steps": ["Recall that tan(A) equals opposite side over adjacent side.","Place opposite = 5 in the numerator and adjacent = 12 in the denominator.","Check that the fraction is simplified and match it to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-07","std": "Geo.35","part": "c","q": "In right triangle ABC with right angle at C, sin(A) = 7/25. What is the value of cos(B)?","a": "7/25","lvl": 3,"choices": ["7/25","25/7","24/25","7/24"],"steps": ["Acute angles A and B in a right triangle are complementary (sum to 90°).","Use the cofunction identity cos(B) = sin(A) for complementary angles.","Match cos(B) directly to the given ratio and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-08","std": "Geo.35","part": "e","q": "A 20-foot ladder leans against a vertical wall, forming a 60° angle with the horizontal ground. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?","a": "10√3 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["10 feet","10√3 feet","20√3 feet","10√2 feet"],"steps": ["Model the ladder, wall, and ground as a 30°-60°-90° right triangle.","With hypotenuse 20, find the short leg 20 ÷ 2, then scale by √{3}.","Match the vertical height (opposite 60°) to the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.35-09","std": "Geo.35","part": "e","q": "Find the exact area of a regular hexagon with side length 6 cm.","a": "54√3 cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["108 cm²","108√3 cm²","36√3 cm²","54√3 cm²"],"steps": ["Split the regular hexagon into 6 congruent equilateral triangles of side 6.","Find one triangle's height using a 30°-60°-90° triangle, then its area.","Multiply one triangle's area by 6 and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-01","std": "Geo.36","q": "Which three-dimensional geometric solid best models a standard soup can?","a": "Cylinder","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Cylinder","Sphere","Triangular prism","Cone"],"steps": ["Picture a soup can: two parallel circular bases joined by a curved side.","Match those features to the 3D solid with congruent circular ends.","Select the matching solid name and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-02","std": "Geo.36","q": "A rectangular garden is modeled as a rectangle measuring 12 ft by 8 ft. How many feet of fencing are needed to enclose its perimeter?","a": "40 ft","lvl": 1,"choices": ["96 ft","40 ft","192 ft","20 ft"],"steps": ["Perimeter is the total distance around all four sides: P = 2l + 2w.","Substitute l = 12 and w = 8 into 2 × 12 + 2 × 8.","Add the products to find the fencing length and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-03","std": "Geo.36","q": "A basketball is modeled as a sphere with radius 12 cm. What is its volume in terms of π?","a": "2304π cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2304π cm³","6912π cm³","576π cm³","1152π cm³"],"steps": ["Use the sphere volume formula V = {4/3} × π × r³.","Cube the radius: 12³ = 1,728, then set up {4/3} × 1,728π.","Multiply by 4, divide by 3, keep π, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-04","std": "Geo.36","q": "A cylindrical water tank has a radius of 5 ft and a height of 10 ft. Using π ≈ 3.14, approximately how many cubic feet of water can it hold?","a": "785 ft³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["157 ft³","314 ft³","785 ft³","1,570 ft³"],"steps": ["Use the cylinder volume formula V = π × r² × h.","Plug in r = 5, h = 10, and π ≈ 3.14: 3.14 × 5² × 10.","Compute the product carefully and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-05","std": "Geo.36","q": "A metal block is modeled as a rectangular prism measuring 4 cm by 5 cm by 10 cm. If its mass is 1,600 g, what is its density in g/cm³?","a": "8 g/cm³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4 g/cm³","16 g/cm³","80 g/cm³","8 g/cm³"],"steps": ["Density equals mass divided by volume: D = {mass/volume}.","First find the prism's volume: 4 × 5 × 10.","Divide 1,600 g by that volume and check your density with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-06","std": "Geo.36","q": "A circular pond has a diameter of 20 m and is surrounded by a uniform 2-meter-wide circular walkway. What is the area of the walkway in terms of π?","a": "44π m²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["44π m²","24π m²","4π m²","84π m²"],"steps": ["Subtract the inner pond area from the outer circle area: π(R² − r²).","Halve diameter 20 to get r = 10, then add 2 to get outer radius R = 12.","Evaluate (12² − 10²) × π and check your result against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-07","std": "Geo.36","q": "A grain silo is modeled by a cylinder of radius 6 m and height 10 m topped by a cone of radius 6 m and height 4 m. What is the total volume of the silo in terms of π?","a": "408π m³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["504π m³","360π m³","432π m³","408π m³"],"steps": ["Add the cylinder volume π × r² × h and cone volume {1/3} × π × r² × h.","Compute π × 6² × 10 for the cylinder and {1/3} × π × 6² × 4 for the cone.","Sum the two exact volumes in terms of π and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-08","std": "Geo.36","q": "A tree trunk is modeled as a right circular cylinder with circumference 6π ft and height 20 ft. What is its volume in terms of π?","a": "180π ft³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["120π ft³","180π ft³","60π ft³","720π ft³"],"steps": ["Use C = 2π × r to find the radius before calculating volume.","Solve 2π × r = 6π for r, then plug r and h = 20 into π × r² × h.","Multiply out the exact volume with π and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.36-09","std": "Geo.36","q": "A city park is a rectangle measuring 400 m by 250 m. If 2,500 people attend a festival in the park, what is the population density in people per square kilometer (1 km² = 1,000,000 m²)?","a": "25,000 people per km²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["25,000 people per km²","0.025 people per km²","2,500 people per km²","250 people per km²"],"steps": ["Find the park's area in m², then convert it to km² before dividing.","Multiply 400 × 250, then divide by 1,000,000 to get the area in km².","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "Geo.37-01","std": "Geo.37","q": "A central angle of a circle intercepts an arc of 80°. What is the measure of an inscribed angle that intercepts the same arc?","a": "40°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["160°","80°","40°","100°"],"steps": ["An inscribed angle measures half of its intercepted arc.","Set up {1/2} × 80° (or 80° ÷ 2) for the inscribed angle.","Compute the angle measure and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-02","std": "Geo.37","q": "An inscribed angle intercepts a semicircle (its sides pass through the endpoints of a diameter). What is the measure of this inscribed angle?","a": "90°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["45°","180°","120°","90°"],"steps": ["A semicircle has an arc measure of 180°.","Take half of the intercepted arc: {1/2} × 180°.","Find that angle measure and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-03","std": "Geo.37","q": "Line t is tangent to circle O at point T. What is the measure of the angle formed by radius OT and tangent line t?","a": "90°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60°","45°","90°","180°"],"steps": ["A tangent line is always perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency.","Recall the degree measure of an angle formed by perpendicular lines.","Select that angle measure and verify it against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-04","std": "Geo.37","q": "Inscribed angle ABC measures 52°. What is the measure of its intercepted arc AC?","a": "104°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["104°","52°","26°","128°"],"steps": ["An intercepted arc is twice the measure of its inscribed angle.","Multiply the inscribed angle by 2: 2 × 52°.","Compute the arc measure and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-05","std": "Geo.37","q": "Segment PA is tangent to circle O at point A. If PA = 8 cm and radius OA = 6 cm, what is the distance PO from the center O to point P?","a": "10 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["100 cm","2√7 cm","10 cm","14 cm"],"steps": ["Radius OA meets tangent PA at 90°, forming right triangle OAP.","Use the Pythagorean Theorem with hypotenuse PO: 6² + 8² = PO².","Add the squares, take the square root, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-06","std": "Geo.37","q": "Quadrilateral ABCD is inscribed in a circle. If angle A measures 75°, what is the measure of opposite angle C?","a": "105°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["150°","105°","90°","75°"],"steps": ["Opposite angles of a cyclic (inscribed) quadrilateral are supplementary.","Set up 75° + angle C = 180°, so angle C = 180° − 75°.","Subtract to find angle C and check that both angles sum to 180°."]},{"id": "Geo.37-07","std": "Geo.37","q": "Two tangents from an external point P touch circle O at points A and B. If central angle AOB measures 130°, what is the measure of circumscribed angle APB?","a": "50°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["230°","65°","130°","50°"],"steps": ["Quadrilateral OAPB has two 90° tangent-radius angles and sums to 360°.","Because 90° + 90° = 180°, angles AOB and APB are supplementary: 180° − 130°.","Subtract to find angle APB and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-08","std": "Geo.37","q": "A chord of length 16 cm is located 6 cm from the center of a circle. What is the radius of the circle?","a": "10 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2√73 cm","10 cm","8 cm","12 cm"],"steps": ["A perpendicular from the center bisects the chord into a right triangle.","Use half the chord (16 ÷ 2 = 8) and 6 as legs in 8² + 6² = r².","Take the square root to find r and check your answer with the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.37-09","std": "Geo.37","q": "Why must any inscribed angle that intercepts a diameter of a circle be a right angle?","a": "It intercepts a 180° arc, and an inscribed angle is half its arc","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A diameter always forms an isosceles right triangle","It is a central angle, so it equals 90°","It intercepts a 90° arc, and an inscribed angle equals its arc","It intercepts a 180° arc, and an inscribed angle is half its arc"],"steps": ["State the arc measure of a semicircle formed by a diameter.","Apply the inscribed angle theorem: angle = {1/2} × intercepted arc.","Connect the resulting measure to the definition of a right angle."]},{"id": "Geo.38-01","std": "Geo.38","q": "A packaging designer wants to use the least amount of cardboard possible to build a closed rectangular box that holds a fixed volume. Which geometric quantity should the designer minimize?","a": "Surface area","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Diagonal length","Surface area","Volume","Perimeter"],"steps": ["Cardboard makes up the outside faces of the box, not the inside space.","Match the total area of all outer faces to its geometric term.","Select that quantity and verify it against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-02","std": "Geo.38","q": "A closed rectangular shipping box measures 4 in. by 5 in. by 6 in. How many square inches of cardboard are needed to make the box (ignoring overlap)?","a": "148 in.²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["148 in.²","240 in.²","74 in.²","120 in.²"],"steps": ["Use rectangular prism surface area: SA = 2(lw + lh + wh).","Substitute 4, 5, and 6: 2 × (4 × 5 + 4 × 6 + 5 × 6).","Add the three face areas, multiply by 2, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-03","std": "Geo.38","q": "A farmer has 40 feet of fencing to build a rectangular pen. Which dimensions maximize the area of the pen?","a": "10 ft by 10 ft","lvl": 1,"choices": ["8 ft by 12 ft","20 ft by 20 ft","5 ft by 15 ft","10 ft by 10 ft"],"steps": ["Check that each choice has perimeter 2l + 2w = 40 ft.","For a fixed perimeter, a square maximizes area (or compare l × w).","Verify both perimeter and largest area, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-04","std": "Geo.38","q": "Two closed cylindrical cans both hold 100π cm³. Can A has radius 5 cm and height 4 cm; Can B has radius 2 cm and height 25 cm. Which can uses less metal, and what is its surface area?","a": "Can A, with surface area 90π cm²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Can A, with surface area 90π cm²","Can A, with surface area 40π cm²","Can B, with surface area 108π cm²","Both cans use the same amount of metal."],"steps": ["Find closed cylinder surface area: SA = 2π × r(r + h).","Evaluate SA for Can A (r = 5, h = 4) and Can B (r = 2, h = 25).","Pick the smaller surface area and match both can and value in the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-05","std": "Geo.38","q": "A shipping carton is a cube measuring 12 in. on each edge. What is the maximum number of smaller cubical boxes with edge length 4 in. that can fit inside the carton?","a": "27","lvl": 2,"choices": ["64","9","3","27"],"steps": ["Find how many 4 in. boxes fit along length, width, and height.","Divide 12 ÷ 4 along each edge, then cube that count (or divide 12³ ÷ 4³).","Multiply the counts along all three edges and check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-06","std": "Geo.38","q": "A farmer uses a straight barn wall as one side of a rectangular pen and has 60 ft of fencing for the other three sides. If the two sides perpendicular to the barn are each 15 ft long, what is the area of the pen?","a": "450 ft²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["900 ft²","450 ft²","225 ft²","675 ft²"],"steps": ["Only three sides use fencing: two 15 ft widths plus one length.","Find the third side: 60 − 2 × 15, then multiply length × width.","Compute the area in ft² and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-07","std": "Geo.38","q": "A designer packs 6 cylindrical soda cans (each with diameter 6 cm and height 12 cm) upright in a 2-by-3 rectangular box. What is the minimum volume of the box?","a": "2,592 cm³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2,160 cm³","1,296 cm³","2,592 cm³","5,184 cm³"],"steps": ["Multiply can diameter 6 cm by 2 and by 3 to get the box base dimensions.","Use box height 12 cm with the base length and width in V = l × w × h.","Multiply all three dimensions and check your volume against the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-08","std": "Geo.38","q": "An open-top cylindrical cup must hold 32π cm³ of liquid and have a radius of 4 cm. What is the required height and the resulting surface area of the cup (bottom and side only)?","a": "Height = 2 cm; surface area = 32π cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Height = 2 cm; surface area = 48π cm²","Height = 4 cm; surface area = 48π cm²","Height = 2 cm; surface area = 32π cm²","Height = 8 cm; surface area = 80π cm²"],"steps": ["Solve V = π × r² × h for h using V = 32π and r = 4.","For an open-top cup, use one base plus side: SA = π × r² + 2π × r × h.","Compute SA with r = 4 and your height h, then check the choices."]},{"id": "Geo.38-09","std": "Geo.38","q": "Two closed boxes both hold 216 in.³: a cube with 6 in. edges and a 4 in. by 6 in. by 9 in. prism. Which uses less material?","a": "The cube: 216 in.² versus 228 in.²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The prism: 216 in.² versus 252 in.²","They use the same material because their volumes are equal","The prism: 228 in.² versus 216 in.²","The cube: 216 in.² versus 228 in.²"],"steps": ["Find surface area for both boxes using SA = 6s² and 2(lw + lh + wh).","Evaluate 6 × 6² for the cube and 2(4 × 6 + 4 × 9 + 6 × 9) for the prism.","Compare the two totals, find the difference, and state which uses less."]},{"id": "Geo.1-10","std": "Geo.1","q": "Simplify the expression: 9√5 − 4√5.","a": "5√5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["5√5","5","13√5","5√0"]},{"id": "Geo.1-11","std": "Geo.1","q": "Divide and simplify completely: √72 / √2.","a": "6","lvl": 1,"choices": ["36","6","6√2","√70"]},{"id": "Geo.1-12","std": "Geo.1","q": "Which radical expression simplifies to a rational number?","a": "√2 × √18","lvl": 1,"choices": ["√6 × √8","√3 + √12","√2 × √18","√20 / √2"]},{"id": "Geo.1-13","std": "Geo.1","q": "A wooden board of length √75 feet has a piece of length √27 feet cut off. What is the exact remaining length in simplest radical form?","a": "2√3 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["4√3 feet","8√3 feet","2√6 feet","2√3 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.1-14","std": "Geo.1","q": "A rectangular banner has an area of 30 square meters and a length of √15 meters. What is the exact width of the banner in simplest radical form?","a": "2√15 meters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2√15 meters","2 meters","15√2 meters","30√15 meters"]},{"id": "Geo.1-15","std": "Geo.1","q": "A rectangle has width √6 cm and length (√6 + √24) cm. What is its exact area?","a": "18 cm²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12 cm²","18 cm²","6 + 2√6 cm²","18√6 cm²"]},{"id": "Geo.1-16","std": "Geo.1","q": "In a sequence of equilateral triangles, Triangle 1 has side length 2, Triangle 2 has side length 2√3, and Triangle 3 has side length 6. Each triangle's side length is multiplied by the same radical factor to produce the next triangle. What is the exact side length of Triangle 5?","a": "18","lvl": 3,"choices": ["9√3","12√3","18","18√3"]},{"id": "Geo.1-17","std": "Geo.1","q": "A square has a diagonal of length 10 cm. Since the diagonal of any square is √2 times its side length, what is the exact perimeter of the square in simplest radical form?","a": "20√2 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["10√2 cm","20 cm","40√2 cm","20√2 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.1-18","std": "Geo.1","q": "In a spiral of right triangles, Triangle 1 has legs of length 1 and 1 (giving hypotenuse √2). Each new right triangle uses the previous triangle's hypotenuse as one leg and has a second leg of length 1. What is the exact length of the hypotenuse of Triangle 7 in simplest radical form?","a": "2√2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2√2","√7","3","4√2"]},{"id": "Geo.2-10","std": "Geo.2","part": "c","q": "A school principal wants to compare classroom crowding across elementary schools of different sizes. Which quantity is most appropriate to define for this comparison?","a": "students per classroom","lvl": 1,"choices": ["total students","students per classroom","total classrooms","classrooms per grade"]},{"id": "Geo.2-11","std": "Geo.2","part": "d","q": "A runner times three 400-meter laps with a stopwatch accurate only to the nearest whole second, recording a total of 190 seconds. A calculator displays 190 / 3 = 63.333333 seconds per lap. Which reported average reflects an appropriate level of accuracy?","a": "63 seconds","lvl": 1,"choices": ["63.333 seconds","63.333333 seconds","63 seconds","60 seconds"]},{"id": "Geo.2-12","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "The density of a solid object is calculated using D = m / V. If mass m is measured in kilograms and volume V is measured in cubic meters, what are the units of density D?","a": "kilograms per cubic meter","lvl": 1,"choices": ["cubic meters per kilogram","kilograms times cubic meters","kilograms per square meter","kilograms per cubic meter"]},{"id": "Geo.2-13","std": "Geo.2","part": "b","q": "A line graph shows a company's annual revenue from 2018 to 2026, ranging between §1.2 million and §1.4 million. The vertical axis spans from §0 to §20 million in steps of §5 million. How does this choice of scale affect the graph?","a": "It makes the year-to-year revenue changes look almost flat, hiding the trend.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["It makes the year-to-year revenue changes look almost flat, hiding the trend.","It exaggerates small differences between years.","It alters the actual revenue values in the table.","It prevents the horizontal axis from showing years."]},{"id": "Geo.2-14","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "A landscaper spreads mulch to a uniform depth of 3 inches over a rectangular flowerbed that is 12 feet long and 8 feet wide. How many cubic feet of mulch are required?","a": "24 cubic feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["288 cubic feet","24 cubic feet","96 cubic feet","36 cubic feet"]},{"id": "Geo.2-15","std": "Geo.2","part": "d","q": "A surveyor measures the side of a square lot as 25 meters to the nearest meter, meaning the actual side length is between 24.5 meters and 25.5 meters. What is the maximum possible error in the calculated perimeter of 100 meters?","a": "2 meters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.5 meters","1 meter","2 meters","4 meters"]},{"id": "Geo.2-16","std": "Geo.2","part": "b","q": "Two bar graphs display the exact same battery-life test results (10 hours vs. 11 hours) for two phone models. Graph 1 has a vertical axis from 0 to 15 hours, while Graph 2 has a vertical axis from 9.5 to 12.5 hours. Why might an advertiser prefer Graph 2, and what should a viewer inspect?","a": "Graph 2 makes the 1-hour difference look dramatically larger; a viewer should check the vertical axis origin and scale.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Graph 2 removes measurement error; a viewer should check the sample size.","Graph 1 distorts the data by starting at 0; a viewer should always prefer Graph 2.","Graph 2 changes the average battery life to 12 hours; a viewer should check the units.","Graph 2 makes the 1-hour difference look dramatically larger; a viewer should check the vertical axis origin and scale."]},{"id": "Geo.2-17","std": "Geo.2","part": "c","q": "An agricultural scientist wants to model water-use efficiency across farms of different sizes producing different corn harvests. Which derived quantity is most appropriate for comparing efficiency?","a": "bushels of corn per gallon of water","lvl": 3,"choices": ["bushels of corn per gallon of water","total gallons of water used","acres per farm","bushels of corn per farmer"]},{"id": "Geo.2-18","std": "Geo.2","part": "a","q": "A rectangular swimming pool measures 25 yards long, 10 yards wide, and 6 feet deep. A pump drains the pool at a constant rate of 150 cubic feet per minute. How many minutes will it take to drain the full pool? (1 yard = 3 feet)","a": "90 minutes","lvl": 3,"choices": ["10 minutes","90 minutes","30 minutes","270 minutes"]},{"id": "Geo.3-10","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two sides of a polygon lie on the lines y = 3x − 4 and y = x + 6. What are the coordinates of the vertex where these two lines intersect?","a": "(5, 11)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(11, 5)","(1, −1)","(5, 11)","(2, 8)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-11","std": "Geo.3","q": "Find the coordinates of the vertex formed by the intersection of the vertical line x = −3 and the line y = −2x + 5.","a": "(−3, 11)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(−3, −1)","(11, −3)","(4, −3)","(−3, 11)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-12","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two edges of a rectangular region lie on the lines y = −4 and x = 7. What are the coordinates of the corner vertex where these edges meet?","a": "(7, −4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(7, −4)","(−4, 7)","(7, 4)","(−7, −4)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-13","std": "Geo.3","q": "A triangle is bounded by the lines y = 0, x = 5, and y = 2x − 2. What are the coordinates of its three vertices?","a": "(1, 0), (5, 0), and (5, 8)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0, −2), (5, 0), and (5, 8)","(1, 0), (5, 0), and (5, 8)","(−1, 0), (5, 0), and (5, 8)","(1, 0), (5, 0), and (5, 12)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-14","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two edges of a pentagon lie on the lines 3x − y = 5 and x + 2y = 11. What are the coordinates of the vertex where these two edges intersect?","a": "(3, 4)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, 3)","(2, 1)","(3, 4)","(3, −4)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-15","std": "Geo.3","q": "Two sides of a triangle lie on the lines y = (2/3)x + 2 and y = −(3/2)x + 15. What are the coordinates of the vertex formed by these two lines?","a": "(6, 6)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(6, 4)","(9, 8)","(4, 9)","(6, 6)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-16","std": "Geo.3","q": "A trapezoid is bounded by the lines x = 1, x = 4, y = x + 3, and y = −x + 1. What are the coordinates of all four vertices?","a": "(1, 0), (1, 4), (4, 7), and (4, −3)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(1, 0), (1, 4), (4, 7), and (4, −3)","(1, 0), (1, 4), (4, 7), and (4, 3)","(0, 1), (4, 1), (7, 4), and (−3, 4)","(−1, 2), (1, 4), (4, 7), and (4, −3)"]},{"id": "Geo.3-17","std": "Geo.3","q": "A triangle is bounded by the lines y = (3/4)x + 4, y = −(3/4)x + 4, and y = 1. Find the coordinates of its vertices and then calculate its perimeter.","a": "18 units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["12 units","18 units","14 units","20 units"]},{"id": "Geo.3-18","std": "Geo.3","q": "A student claims that the four lines y = 2x + 1, y = 2x − 5, y = −x + 4, and y = 2x + 7 determine a closed parallelogram. Is the student correct, and why?","a": "No, because three of the lines are parallel (slope 2), so the four lines cannot enclose a parallelogram.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Yes, because all four lines have different y-intercepts.","Yes, because two pairs of lines intersect at right angles.","No, because three of the lines are parallel (slope 2), so the four lines cannot enclose a parallelogram.","No, because lines with negative slopes cannot form a parallelogram."]},{"id": "Geo.4-10","std": "Geo.4","q": "The volume of a rectangular prism is V = lwh. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = V / (lw)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["h = V − lw","h = Vlw","h = (lw) / V","h = V / (lw)"]},{"id": "Geo.4-11","std": "Geo.4","q": "The sum of the interior angles of an n-sided polygon is S = 180(n − 2). Rearrange the formula to solve for n.","a": "n = (S / 180) + 2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["n = (S / 180) + 2","n = (S / 180) − 2","n = (S + 2) / 180","n = 180S + 2"]},{"id": "Geo.4-12","std": "Geo.4","q": "Density is given by D = m / V, where m is mass and V is volume. Rearrange the formula to solve for V.","a": "V = m / D","lvl": 1,"choices": ["V = mD","V = m / D","V = D / m","V = m − D"]},{"id": "Geo.4-13","std": "Geo.4","q": "The area of a circle is A = πr². Rearrange the formula to solve for the positive radius r.","a": "r = √(A / π)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["r = A / π","r = √A / π","r = √(A / π)","r = √(Aπ)"]},{"id": "Geo.4-14","std": "Geo.4","q": "The volume of a square pyramid is V = (1/3)s²h, where s is the base edge length and h is the height. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = 3V / s²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["h = V / (3s²)","h = 3V − s²","h = s² / (3V)","h = 3V / s²"]},{"id": "Geo.4-15","std": "Geo.4","q": "The area of a circular sector is A = (θ / 360) × πr². Rearrange the formula to solve for the central angle θ.","a": "θ = (360A) / (πr²)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["θ = (360A) / (πr²)","θ = A / (360πr²)","θ = (360πr²) / A","θ = 360A − πr²"]},{"id": "Geo.4-16","std": "Geo.4","q": "The surface area of a closed rectangular prism is S = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh. Rearrange the formula to solve for h.","a": "h = (S − 2lw) / (2l + 2w)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["h = (S − 2lw) / (2lw)","h = (S − 2lw) / (2l + 2w)","h = S / (2l + 2w) − 2lw","h = (S + 2lw) / (2l + 2w)"]},{"id": "Geo.4-17","std": "Geo.4","q": "The surface area of a sphere is S = 4πr², and its diameter is d = 2r. Rearrange to express the positive diameter d directly in terms of S.","a": "d = √(S / π)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["d = 2√(S / π)","d = √(S / (4π))","d = √(S / π)","d = S / π"]},{"id": "Geo.4-18","std": "Geo.4","q": "The x-coordinate of the midpoint of a segment is given by xm = (x1 + x2) / 2. Rearrange the formula to solve for x2, and then find x2 when xm = 5 and x1 = −3.","a": "x2 = 2xm − x1; x2 = 13","lvl": 3,"choices": ["x2 = 2xm + x1; x2 = 7","x2 = (xm / 2) − x1; x2 = 5.5","x2 = 2xm − x1; x2 = 7","x2 = 2xm − x1; x2 = 13"]},{"id": "Geo.5-10","std": "Geo.5","q": "Which ordered pair is a solution to the linear equation x − 4y = 12?","a": "(4, −2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(4, −2)","(4, 2)","(−4, 2)","(12, 1)"]},{"id": "Geo.5-11","std": "Geo.5","q": "The graph of y = 5 is a horizontal line in the coordinate plane. Which statement is true about every point on this line?","a": "Its y-coordinate is 5, and its x-coordinate can be any real number.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Its x-coordinate is 5, and its y-coordinate can be any real number.","Its y-coordinate is 5, and its x-coordinate can be any real number.","Both coordinates must be positive integers.","Only the point (0, 5) satisfies the equation."]},{"id": "Geo.5-12","std": "Geo.5","q": "What are the x-intercept and y-intercept plotted on the graph of 2x + 5y = 20?","a": "(10, 0) and (0, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(4, 0) and (0, 10)","(2, 0) and (0, 5)","(10, 0) and (0, 4)","(−10, 0) and (0, −4)"]},{"id": "Geo.5-13","std": "Geo.5","q": "A student graphs the equation y = (1/2)x + 3 and checks whether the point (5, 5.5) lies on the line. Which conclusion is correct?","a": "Yes, because (5, 5.5) satisfies y = (1/2)(5) + 3 = 5.5, and non-integer solutions also lie on the line.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No, because only points with integer coordinates lie on a line.","No, because (1/2)(5) + 3 equals 8.","Yes, because every point in Quadrant I lies on the line.","Yes, because (5, 5.5) satisfies y = (1/2)(5) + 3 = 5.5, and non-integer solutions also lie on the line."]},{"id": "Geo.5-14","std": "Geo.5","q": "The graph of the linear equation 5x + 2y = c passes through the point (4, −3). What is the value of c, and does the point (2, 2) also lie on this line?","a": "c = 14; yes, (2, 2) lies on the line","lvl": 2,"choices": ["c = 14; yes, (2, 2) lies on the line","c = 14; no, (2, 2) does not lie on the line","c = 26; yes, (2, 2) lies on the line","c = −7; no, (2, 2) does not lie on the line"]},{"id": "Geo.5-15","std": "Geo.5","q": "Which linear equation has the exact same set of plotted solutions (the exact same graph) as 6x − 9y = 18?","a": "2x − 3y = 6","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2x + 3y = 6","2x − 3y = 6","3x − 2y = 6","2x − 3y = 18"]},{"id": "Geo.5-16","std": "Geo.5","q": "Points A(−2, 7), B(1, 1), and C(4, −5) are plotted in the coordinate plane. Do all three points lie on the graph of a single linear equation, and if so, what is that equation?","a": "Yes; they all lie on the line y = −2x + 3.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Yes; they all lie on the line y = 2x − 1.","No; the slopes between consecutive points are not equal.","Yes; they all lie on the line y = −2x + 3.","Yes; they all lie on the line y = −2x − 3."]},{"id": "Geo.5-17","std": "Geo.5","q": "The graph of ax + by = 12 passes through (4, 0) and (0, −6). What are the values of a and b, and does the point (2, −3) lie on the graph?","a": "a = 3, b = −2; yes, (2, −3) lies on the graph","lvl": 3,"choices": ["a = 3, b = 2; no, (2, −3) does not lie on the graph","a = 4, b = −6; no, (2, −3) does not lie on the graph","a = −2, b = 3; yes, (2, −3) lies on the graph","a = 3, b = −2; yes, (2, −3) lies on the graph"]},{"id": "Geo.5-18","std": "Geo.5","q": "Why is the graph of a linear equation in two variables, such as y = 3x − 1, drawn as a continuous line rather than a set of isolated points?","a": "Every real x-value, including fractions and decimals, gives a solution (x, y), so the infinitely many solutions fill in a line with no gaps.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Every real x-value, including fractions and decimals, gives a solution (x, y), so the infinitely many solutions fill in a line with no gaps.","Only integer x-values are solutions, and the line just connects them to look neat.","The line is drawn only to show the slope; points between integers are not solutions.","The equation has exactly two solutions, the intercepts, and the line joins them."]},{"id": "Geo.6-10","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle is centered at the origin (0, 0) and has a radius of 8. What is its equation?","a": "x² + y² = 64","lvl": 1,"choices": ["x² + y² = 8","x² + y² = 64","x² + y² = 16","(x − 8)² + (y − 8)² = 64"]},{"id": "Geo.6-11","std": "Geo.6","part": "b","q": "To find the distance between P(4, −3) and Q(−2, 5) using the Pythagorean Theorem, what are the lengths of the horizontal and vertical legs, and what is PQ?","a": "Legs 6 and 8; PQ = 10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Legs 2 and 2; PQ = 2√2","Legs 6 and 8; PQ = 14","Legs 6 and 8; PQ = 10","Legs 2 and 8; PQ = √68"]},{"id": "Geo.6-12","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (−4, 2) and (8, −6). What are the coordinates of the center of the circle?","a": "(2, −2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(6, −4)","(4, −4)","(−2, 2)","(2, −2)"]},{"id": "Geo.6-13","std": "Geo.6","part": "b","q": "A right triangle in the coordinate plane has vertices at A(−1, −2), B(7, −2), and C(7, 4). Use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the exact distance AC.","a": "10","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10","14","100","2√7"]},{"id": "Geo.6-14","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle has center (h, k) and radius r, and point (x, y) lies on the circle. Which right-triangle relationship produces the standard equation (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²?","a": "A right triangle with legs |x − h| and |y − k| and hypotenuse r","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A right triangle with legs x and y and hypotenuse r","A right triangle with legs |x − h| and |y − k| and hypotenuse r","A right triangle with legs |x + h| and |y + k| and hypotenuse r²","A right triangle with legs h and k and hypotenuse r"]},{"id": "Geo.6-15","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (1, −3) and (9, 3). What is the radius of the circle?","a": "5","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10","7","5","25"]},{"id": "Geo.6-16","std": "Geo.6","q": "A circle has center (−4, 3) and is tangent to the y-axis (touches the y-axis at exactly one point). What is the equation of the circle?","a": "(x + 4)² + (y − 3)² = 16","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x + 4)² + (y − 3)² = 9","(x − 4)² + (y + 3)² = 16","(x + 4)² + (y − 3)² = 4","(x + 4)² + (y − 3)² = 16"]},{"id": "Geo.6-17","std": "Geo.6","part": "a","q": "A circle has a diameter with endpoints (−5, 2) and (1, 10). What is the equation of the circle, and does the point (2, 7) lie inside, on, or outside the circle?","a": "(x + 2)² + (y − 6)² = 25; inside the circle","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x + 2)² + (y − 6)² = 25; inside the circle","(x + 2)² + (y − 6)² = 25; outside the circle","(x + 2)² + (y − 6)² = 25; on the circle","(x − 2)² + (y + 6)² = 100; inside the circle"]},{"id": "Geo.6-18","std": "Geo.6","part": "b","q": "Point P(x, 4) is 13 units away from Q(2, −1). Using the distance formula derived from the Pythagorean Theorem, what are the two possible values of x?","a": "x = 14 or x = −10","lvl": 3,"choices": ["x = 10 or x = −14","x = 14 or x = −10","x = 14 only","x = 15 or x = −11"]},{"id": "Geo.7-10","std": "Geo.7","q": "Which of the following is an example of univariate quantitative data?","a": "the daily battery life in hours of 40 smartphones","lvl": 1,"choices": ["the shoe size and height of 40 athletes","the brand of smartphone owned by 40 students","the daily battery life in hours of 40 smartphones","the age and resale price of 40 used cars"]},{"id": "Geo.7-11","std": "Geo.7","q": "A scatter plot of weekly hours spent playing video games and math quiz scores shows points sloping steadily downward from left to right. Which conclusion is best supported?","a": "More hours spent gaming per week is associated with lower quiz scores.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["More hours spent gaming per week is associated with higher quiz scores.","Gaming hours and quiz scores have no association.","Quiz scores remain constant regardless of gaming hours.","More hours spent gaming per week is associated with lower quiz scores."]},{"id": "Geo.7-12","std": "Geo.7","q": "Over the past 250 days, a company's web server crashed on 10 days. Based on this data, what is the empirical risk (probability) that the server crashes on a randomly chosen day?","a": "4%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["4%","0.4%","10%","25%"]},{"id": "Geo.7-13","std": "Geo.7","q": "A town tracks spring rainfall (in inches) and summer mosquito counts over 12 years and finds a strong positive linear association. If meteorologists forecast an unusually rainy spring, what risk assessment is most reasonable?","a": "The town should prepare for higher-than-average mosquito counts.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Mosquito counts will drop to zero.","The town should prepare for higher-than-average mosquito counts.","Rainfall only affects univariate data, not mosquito counts.","The town should reduce mosquito control because rainfall stays constant."]},{"id": "Geo.7-14","std": "Geo.7","q": "A traveler compares two flight connections to make a critical meeting. Connection 1 averages a 10-minute delay with 2% of flights delayed over 45 minutes. Connection 2 averages an 8-minute delay with 18% of flights delayed over 45 minutes. If a delay over 45 minutes causes the traveler to miss the meeting, which connection carries less risk?","a": "Connection 1, because it has a much lower probability of a delay over 45 minutes.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Connection 2, because its average delay is 2 minutes shorter.","Both connections have equal risk because both average under 15 minutes.","Connection 1, because it has a much lower probability of a delay over 45 minutes.","Connection 2, because 18% is greater than 2%."]},{"id": "Geo.7-15","std": "Geo.7","q": "A scatter plot of a phone's age (0 to 36 months) and its maximum battery capacity (percent) shows a strong negative linear association. What does this trend imply about the risk of poor battery life?","a": "Older phones carry a higher risk of reduced battery capacity.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Older phones have higher battery capacity.","Battery capacity is unrelated to phone age.","Battery capacity declines only after 36 months.","Older phones carry a higher risk of reduced battery capacity."]},{"id": "Geo.7-16","std": "Geo.7","q": "Two suppliers manufacture structural bolts that must withstand at least 500 lb of force. Supplier P's bolts have a mean breaking strength of 540 lb and a standard deviation of 35 lb. Supplier Q's bolts have a mean breaking strength of 530 lb and a standard deviation of 8 lb. Both distributions are symmetric. Which supplier carries a lower risk of bolts failing below 500 lb?","a": "Supplier Q, because 500 lb is nearly 4 standard deviations below its mean, making failure far less likely.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Supplier Q, because 500 lb is nearly 4 standard deviations below its mean, making failure far less likely.","Supplier P, because its mean of 540 lb is higher than 530 lb.","Both suppliers carry identical risk because both means exceed 500 lb.","Supplier P, because a larger standard deviation guarantees stronger bolts."]},{"id": "Geo.7-17","std": "Geo.7","q": "Experimental data from 200 mg to 600 mg of a fertilizer show a strong positive linear association with tomato yield. A grower uses the linear trend to predict that applying 5,000 mg will yield ten times as many tomatoes. What is the main statistical risk in this reasoning?","a": "Extrapolating far beyond the observed data range ignores the risk that excess fertilizer could damage the plants.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Linear associations cannot be used to make any predictions.","Extrapolating far beyond the observed data range ignores the risk that excess fertilizer could damage the plants.","Fertilizer mass is categorical rather than quantitative.","A positive linear association always turns negative immediately at 600 mg."]},{"id": "Geo.7-18","std": "Geo.7","q": "A quality-control team tests two batches of 500 batteries. Batch 1 has a mean life of 12.0 hours with 10 batteries lasting under 10 hours. Batch 2 has a mean life of 12.2 hours with 45 batteries lasting under 10 hours. Any battery under 10 hours needs a costly warranty replacement. Which production process should be adopted?","a": "Batch 1, because its failure rate is 2% (10/500) versus 9% (45/500), which outweighs its slightly lower mean.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Batch 2, because its higher mean of 12.2 hours means less risk.","Batch 1, because its failure rate is 10% versus 45% for Batch 2.","Batch 1, because its failure rate is 2% (10/500) versus 9% (45/500), which outweighs its slightly lower mean.","Either batch, because both means are above 10 hours, so the risk is the same."]},{"id": "Geo.8-10","std": "Geo.8","q": "A hospital database contains 20,000 patient records with columns for Age (years), Blood Type, Height (cm), and Number of Visits. Which column contains categorical data?","a": "Blood Type","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Age (years)","Height (cm)","Number of Visits","Blood Type"]},{"id": "Geo.8-11","std": "Geo.8","q": "A spreadsheet of 8,000 online orders accidentally imported 120 identical order rows twice. Which spreadsheet tool is specifically designed to fix this issue?","a": "Remove Duplicates","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Remove Duplicates","Sort Descending","Insert Chart","Freeze Top Row"]},{"id": "Geo.8-12","std": "Geo.8","q": "In a database of 3,000 marathon runners, which column represents continuous quantitative data rather than discrete quantitative or categorical data?","a": "Finish time in minutes","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Number of marathons completed","Finish time in minutes","Shirt size (S, M, L, XL)","Runner bib number"]},{"id": "Geo.8-13","std": "Geo.8","q": "A spreadsheet column of 10,000 product prices contains entries formatted as '§12.50' stored as text strings instead of numeric values. What happens if you try to compute the mean price with a spreadsheet formula?","a": "The formula may ignore the text entries or return an error until they are converted to numeric format.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The spreadsheet automatically deletes the entire column.","Text prices are treated as negative numbers.","The formula may ignore the text entries or return an error until they are converted to numeric format.","The mean will automatically equal 10,000."]},{"id": "Geo.8-14","std": "Geo.8","q": "A school tracks 12,000 library checkouts with columns for Book Title, Genre, Grade Level, and Days Borrowed. Which spreadsheet function quickly counts how many checkouts belong to the 'Science Fiction' genre?","a": "COUNTIF","lvl": 2,"choices": ["AVERAGE","MEDIAN","MAX","COUNTIF"]},{"id": "Geo.8-15","std": "Geo.8","q": "A CSV file of 6,000 customer records stores 'City, State' together in one column (for example, 'Birmingham, AL'). Before grouping customers by state in software, what data-cleaning step is needed?","a": "Split the column into separate City and State columns using the comma delimiter.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Split the column into separate City and State columns using the comma delimiter.","Merge the column with the customer name column.","Sort the combined column by text length.","Delete all commas so each entry becomes one word."]},{"id": "Geo.8-16","std": "Geo.8","q": "A researcher merges two 25,000-row environmental datasets. Dataset 1 records dates as 'MM/DD/YYYY' (such as 03/08/2026) and Dataset 2 records dates as 'DD/MM/YYYY' (such as 08/03/2026). What problem occurs if the merged table is sorted chronologically without standardizing dates first?","a": "Software will misread days as months for part of the data, placing records in the wrong order and distorting monthly summaries.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The file size will double automatically.","Software will misread days as months for part of the data, placing records in the wrong order and distorting monthly summaries.","Software will delete all numeric columns.","Every date will revert to the year 2000."]},{"id": "Geo.8-17","std": "Geo.8","q": "A fitness app exports 100,000 workout logs containing impossible negative entries like '-15' in the 'Calories Burned' column and several sensor-glitch entries of '99,999'. Before running summary statistics, what is the best technology-assisted workflow?","a": "Filter or sort to isolate impossible values, document and correct or exclude invalid entries, and then calculate summaries on the cleaned dataset.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Replace all negative values with 99,999 to keep the sample size constant.","Leave all values unchanged because spreadsheets automatically fix impossible entries.","Filter or sort to isolate impossible values, document and correct or exclude invalid entries, and then calculate summaries on the cleaned dataset.","Delete every row whose calories burned is above the median."]},{"id": "Geo.8-18","std": "Geo.8","q": "A city dataset has 40,000 pothole repair tickets with columns for District, Repair Cost, and Days to Complete. How does a pivot table make this dataset manageable, and which summaries could it produce?","a": "It groups all 40,000 rows by District automatically, so it can report the average repair cost and the average days to complete for each district.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It sorts the rows alphabetically, so it can report the single largest ticket number in the city.","It deletes repeated districts, so each district keeps only one ticket.","It turns District into a number, so it can report the mean district.","It groups all 40,000 rows by District automatically, so it can report the average repair cost and the average days to complete for each district."]},{"id": "Geo.9-10","std": "Geo.9","q": "A dot plot displays the number of books read over the summer by 11 students: two dots at 2, three dots at 3, four dots at 4, one dot at 5, and one dot at 9. What is the median number of books read?","a": "4","lvl": 1,"choices": ["4","3","3.5","5"]},{"id": "Geo.9-11","std": "Geo.9","q": "A box plot displays the scores of 80 students on a geometry exam. Approximately what percentage of the students scored between the first quartile (Q1) and the third quartile (Q3)?","a": "50%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["25%","50%","75%","100%"]},{"id": "Geo.9-12","std": "Geo.9","q": "A track coach records each runner's weekly training mileage and 5K race time (in minutes) for 35 athletes. Which display is most appropriate to represent this bivariate data?","a": "scatter plot","lvl": 1,"choices": ["dot plot","histogram","scatter plot","box plot"]},{"id": "Geo.9-13","std": "Geo.9","q": "A box plot of 40 daily high temperatures has minimum 58°F, Q1 = 64°F, median = 70°F, Q3 = 75°F, and maximum 82°F. About how many days had a high temperature of 75°F or higher?","a": "10 days","lvl": 2,"choices": ["20 days","30 days","7 days","10 days"]},{"id": "Geo.9-14","std": "Geo.9","q": "A histogram of 30 quiz scores has intervals: 50-60 (1 student), 60-70 (2 students), 70-80 (4 students), 80-90 (10 students), and 90-100 (13 students). How is the shape of this distribution best described?","a": "Skewed left","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Skewed left","Skewed right","Symmetric","Uniform"]},{"id": "Geo.9-15","std": "Geo.9","q": "For a small data set of 12 shoe sizes, a dot plot is easy to draw by hand. Why is a histogram or box plot generated by technology preferred when the data set grows to 10,000 continuous reaction times?","a": "Plotting 10,000 individual continuous dots would be unreadable, whereas histograms and box plots summarize large data sets clearly.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Dot plots cannot display quantitative data.","Plotting 10,000 individual continuous dots would be unreadable, whereas histograms and box plots summarize large data sets clearly.","Technology cannot draw histograms for fewer than 10,000 values.","Box plots display every individual reaction time exactly."]},{"id": "Geo.9-16","std": "Geo.9","q": "A club's 15 members have ages tightly clustered from 15 to 17, except for one advisor who is 42. How do a dot plot and a box plot each represent this outlier?","a": "A dot plot shows the exact gap and cluster of every age, while a box plot marks 42 as an isolated outlier point (or stretches the upper whisker).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A dot plot hides the 42, while a box plot moves the median to 28.","A box plot shows every individual age, while a dot plot removes the maximum.","A dot plot shows the exact gap and cluster of every age, while a box plot marks 42 as an isolated outlier point (or stretches the upper whisker).","Neither plot can display a value as large as 42."]},{"id": "Geo.9-17","std": "Geo.9","q": "A data set of 8 test scores is {62, 68, 70, 74, 78, 82, 86, 94}. What is the five-number summary needed to construct a box plot?","a": "Min = 62, Q1 = 69, Median = 76, Q3 = 84, Max = 94","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Min = 62, Q1 = 68, Median = 76, Q3 = 86, Max = 94","Min = 62, Q1 = 70, Median = 76, Q3 = 82, Max = 94","Min = 62, Q1 = 69, Median = 74, Q3 = 84, Max = 94","Min = 62, Q1 = 69, Median = 76, Q3 = 84, Max = 94"]},{"id": "Geo.9-18","std": "Geo.9","q": "A student uses technology to plot car weight (x) and fuel economy in mpg (y) for 500 vehicles. Why is a scatter plot better than two separate histograms here?","a": "A scatter plot keeps each car's weight paired with its fuel economy; two histograms show each variable alone and lose which weight goes with which mpg.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A scatter plot keeps each car's weight paired with its fuel economy; two histograms show each variable alone and lose which weight goes with which mpg.","A scatter plot shows the median of each variable, while histograms cannot show a center.","Two histograms would lose the outliers, while a scatter plot keeps them.","A scatter plot works only for small data sets, so two histograms lose nothing here."]},{"id": "Geo.10-10","std": "Geo.10","part": "a","q": "Why do both mean absolute deviation (MAD) and standard deviation avoid simply averaging the signed deviations (x − mean) directly?","a": "Because the positive and negative deviations from the mean always cancel out and sum to zero.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Because signed deviations are always positive.","Because the positive and negative deviations from the mean always cancel out and sum to zero.","Because the mean is always larger than every data value.","Because averaging signed deviations produces the median."]},{"id": "Geo.10-11","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "Calculate the population standard deviation of the data set {6, 6, 10, 10}.","a": "2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0","4","2","8"]},{"id": "Geo.10-12","std": "Geo.10","q": "Store A has a median daily sale of §420 (IQR = §50) and Store B has a median daily sale of §390 (IQR = §110). Both sales distributions are skewed right. Which comparison is correct?","a": "Store A has both higher typical daily sales and more consistent daily sales.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Store B has higher typical daily sales and more consistent daily sales.","Store A has higher typical daily sales, while Store B is more consistent.","Store B has higher typical daily sales, while Store A is more consistent.","Store A has both higher typical daily sales and more consistent daily sales."]},{"id": "Geo.10-13","std": "Geo.10","part": "a","q": "When computing the population standard deviation, after squaring each deviation from the mean and averaging those squared deviations, why is the final step to take the square root?","a": "To return the measure of spread back to the original units of the data.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["To return the measure of spread back to the original units of the data.","To make standard deviation smaller than mean absolute deviation.","To remove all negative values from the original dataset.","To convert the mean into the median."]},{"id": "Geo.10-14","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "Calculate the population standard deviation of the data set {3, 6, 9, 12, 15}, rounded to the nearest hundredth.","a": "4.24","lvl": 2,"choices": ["3.60","4.24","4.74","18.00"]},{"id": "Geo.10-15","std": "Geo.10","q": "Two symmetric data sets show the heights (in cm) of two plant varieties. Variety X has mean 24 cm and standard deviation 1.5 cm. Variety Y has mean 27 cm and standard deviation 4.2 cm. Which conclusion is best supported?","a": "Variety Y is taller on average, while Variety X grows to more uniform heights.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Variety X is taller on average and has more variable heights.","Variety Y is both taller on average and more uniform in height.","Variety Y is taller on average, while Variety X grows to more uniform heights.","Both varieties have the same spread because both are symmetric."]},{"id": "Geo.10-16","std": "Geo.10","part": "a","q": "For the data set {4, 8, 8, 12}, compute both the mean absolute deviation (MAD) and the population standard deviation (rounded to the nearest hundredth), and compare them.","a": "MAD = 2 and population standard deviation is 2.83; standard deviation is larger.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["MAD = 2.83 and population standard deviation is 2; MAD is larger.","MAD = 2 and population standard deviation is 2; they are equal.","MAD = 4 and population standard deviation is 8; standard deviation is double.","MAD = 2 and population standard deviation is 2.83; standard deviation is larger."]},{"id": "Geo.10-17","std": "Geo.10","part": "b","q": "Data set A is {1, 2, 5, 8, 9}. Data set B is formed by adding 20 to every value in Data set A: {21, 22, 25, 28, 29}. How does the population standard deviation of Data set B compare to that of Data set A?","a": "They are equal (both about 3.16), because adding 20 shifts the mean by 20 without changing distances from the mean.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["They are equal (both about 3.16), because adding 20 shifts the mean by 20 without changing distances from the mean.","Data set B's standard deviation is 20 greater than Data set A's.","Data set B's standard deviation is 20 times Data set A's.","Data set B's standard deviation is smaller because its values are larger."]},{"id": "Geo.10-18","std": "Geo.10","q": "Two neighborhoods have home prices (in thousands of dollars). Neighborhood 1: {210, 220, 225, 230, 240, 245, 250}. Neighborhood 2: {190, 200, 205, 210, 215, 220, 750}. Using statistics appropriate to the shapes of the distributions, how do the two neighborhoods compare?","a": "Using median and IQR because Neighborhood 2 is skewed right by 750, Neighborhood 1 has a higher typical price (§230k vs. §210k), while Neighborhood 2 has a slightly smaller IQR (§20k vs. §25k).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Using mean and standard deviation, Neighborhood 2 has a higher typical price (§284.3k vs. §231.4k) and smaller spread.","Using median and IQR because Neighborhood 2 is skewed right by 750, Neighborhood 1 has a higher typical price (§230k vs. §210k), while Neighborhood 2 has a slightly smaller IQR (§20k vs. §25k).","Using median and IQR, Neighborhood 2 has a higher typical price (§230k vs. §210k) and larger IQR.","Using mean and range, both neighborhoods have identical typical prices."]},{"id": "Geo.11-10","std": "Geo.11","q": "A strongly left-skewed distribution of exam scores has a few very low scores (such as 15% and 20%) while most scores cluster between 85% and 95%. How does the mean compare to the median?","a": "The mean is less than the median.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The mean is greater than the median.","The mean and median are always equal.","The mean is less than the median.","The mean cannot be calculated when data are skewed."]},{"id": "Geo.11-11","std": "Geo.11","q": "Removing an extreme low outlier from a data set of student reaction times will cause the standard deviation to do what?","a": "Decrease","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Increase","Stay exactly the same","Become negative","Decrease"]},{"id": "Geo.11-12","std": "Geo.11","q": "In a symmetric, bell-shaped distribution with no outliers, how do the mean and median compare?","a": "They are approximately equal.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["They are approximately equal.","The mean is much larger than the median.","The median is much larger than the mean.","The mean is equal to the interquartile range."]},{"id": "Geo.11-13","std": "Geo.11","q": "Eight students recorded their daily commute times in minutes: {8, 10, 11, 12, 12, 13, 14, 48}. What happens to the median if the outlier 48 is removed?","a": "It stays the same at 12 minutes.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["It decreases from 12 minutes to 11.5 minutes.","It stays the same at 12 minutes.","It increases from 12 minutes to 12.5 minutes.","It decreases by 4.5 minutes."]},{"id": "Geo.11-14","std": "Geo.11","q": "Two factories produce 100-gram chocolate bars. Factory A's bar weights are symmetric with mean 100 g and standard deviation 1.2 g. Factory B's bar weights are skewed left with mean 98 g, median 100 g, and standard deviation 4.5 g. What does this difference suggest about Factory B?","a": "Factory B has several underweight bars pulling its mean below 100 g and increasing its spread.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Factory B produces more consistent bars than Factory A.","Factory B has several overweight bars pulling its mean downward.","Factory B has several underweight bars pulling its mean below 100 g and increasing its spread.","Both factories have identical quality because both medians equal 100 g."]},{"id": "Geo.11-15","std": "Geo.11","q": "A basketball player scored 14, 16, 18, 18, 20, and 22 points in her first six games (a mean of 18 points). If she scores 4 points in her seventh game due to an early injury, by how much does her mean points per game decrease?","a": "2 points","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1 point","4 points","14 points","2 points"]},{"id": "Geo.11-16","std": "Geo.11","q": "A science class of 15 students had a mean lab score of 84. One student's score of 28 (a low outlier) was discovered to be a data-entry error and was removed. What is the new mean of the remaining 14 students?","a": "88","lvl": 3,"choices": ["88","86","87.7","90"]},{"id": "Geo.11-17","std": "Geo.11","q": "Four technicians at a repair shop earn annual salaries (in thousands of dollars) of {42, 42, 46, 46}. When the shop owner's salary of §94,000 is included, the set becomes {42, 42, 46, 46, 94}. How does the population standard deviation change (rounded to the nearest tenth)?","a": "It increases from §2.0k to §20.1k.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It increases from §2.0k to §10.0k.","It increases from §2.0k to §20.1k.","It increases from §4.0k to §20.1k.","It increases from §2.0k to §54.0k."]},{"id": "Geo.11-18","std": "Geo.11","q": "Service P's user ages are symmetric around a mean of 35 years (SD 6). Service Q's user ages are bimodal with peaks at 18 and 52 years, also with a mean of 35 (SD 17). Why does the mean alone give a misleading picture of Service Q's audience?","a": "Q's mean falls in the gap between two age groups, so few Q users are near 35, while P's users cluster near 35.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Q's larger standard deviation means its mean was calculated incorrectly.","Both services have the same mean, so the mean describes both audiences equally well.","Q's mean falls in the gap between two age groups, so few Q users are near 35, while P's users cluster near 35.","A bimodal distribution has its mean equal to its median, so most Q users are 35."]},{"id": "Geo.12-10","std": "Geo.12","q": "A scatter plot shows the mileage (x) and resale value (y) of 30 used sedans. As mileage increases, resale value decreases roughly along a straight line, except for one antique collector sedan plotted far above the rest of the points. How is this scatter plot best described?","a": "Negative linear association with a high outlier","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Positive linear association with a low outlier","No association with two clusters","Nonlinear curved association with no outliers","Negative linear association with a high outlier"]},{"id": "Geo.12-11","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "For a data point (8, 15), a linear model predicts y = 19. What is the residual (actual minus predicted) for this point?","a": "−4","lvl": 1,"choices": ["−4","4","7","−7"]},{"id": "Geo.12-12","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "A graphing calculator computes the least-squares regression line in the form y = ax + b and displays a = −1.4 and b = 32.5. What is the equation of the line of best fit?","a": "y = −1.4x + 32.5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["y = 32.5x − 1.4","y = −1.4x + 32.5","y = 1.4x + 32.5","y = −1.4x − 32.5"]},{"id": "Geo.12-13","std": "Geo.12","q": "A scatter plot of tree age in years (x) versus trunk diameter in inches (y) shows one group of young saplings (ages 1 to 5) and a second group of mature trees (ages 30 to 40) with no data between ages 6 and 29, following an overall upward straight-line trend. Which description identifies all key features?","a": "Positive linear association with two clusters and a gap","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Negative linear association with two clusters and a gap","Positive linear association with evenly spaced data and no gaps","Positive linear association with two clusters and a gap","No association because of the gap in ages"]},{"id": "Geo.12-14","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "A residual plot for a linear model displays points scattered randomly above and below the horizontal axis (y = 0) with no curved pattern or fanning out. What does this residual plot indicate?","a": "A linear model is an appropriate fit for the data.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The slope of the linear model must be zero.","A quadratic model would fit much better than a linear model.","There is no correlation between the two variables.","A linear model is an appropriate fit for the data."]},{"id": "Geo.12-15","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "Use technology or the least-squares formulas to find the equation of the line of best fit for the data points (1, 10), (2, 8), (3, 7), and (4, 3).","a": "y = −2.2x + 12.5","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = −2.2x + 12.5","y = −2.3x + 12.5","y = 2.2x + 1.5","y = −2.2x + 7"]},{"id": "Geo.12-16","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "Four data points are (1, 3), (2, 6), (3, 7), and (4, 10). Model 1 is y = 2x + 1 and Model 2 is y = 2.2x + 1. Compute the sum of squared residuals (SSR) for each model to determine which fits better.","a": "Model 2 fits better (SSR = 0.8 vs. SSR = 2).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Model 1 fits better (SSR = 0.8 vs. SSR = 2).","Model 2 fits better (SSR = 0.8 vs. SSR = 2).","Model 1 fits better (SSR = 2 vs. SSR = 4).","Both models fit equally well because both have SSR = 2."]},{"id": "Geo.12-17","std": "Geo.12","part": "b","q": "Find the least-squares line of best fit for the four data points (2, 15), (4, 11), (6, 9), and (8, 5), and determine the sum of its four residuals.","a": "y = −1.6x + 18; sum of residuals is 0","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = −1.5x + 18; sum of residuals is 1","y = −1.6x + 15; sum of residuals is 0","y = −1.6x + 18; sum of residuals is 0","y = −1.6x + 18; sum of residuals is 0.8"]},{"id": "Geo.12-18","std": "Geo.12","part": "a","q": "A student fits the line y = 3x + 2 to four data points and gets residuals of +4, +5, +3, and +4. How should the student adjust the y-intercept (keeping the slope 3) to minimize the sum of squared residuals, and how does the SSR change?","a": "Increase the y-intercept by 4 to y = 3x + 6; SSR drops from 66 to 2.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Decrease the y-intercept by 4 to y = 3x − 2; SSR drops from 66 to 2.","Increase the y-intercept by 16 to y = 3x + 18; SSR drops from 66 to 0.","Increase the y-intercept by 4 to y = 3x + 6; SSR drops from 16 to 2.","Increase the y-intercept by 4 to y = 3x + 6; SSR drops from 66 to 2."]},{"id": "Geo.13-10","std": "Geo.13","q": "A calculator computes a correlation coefficient of r = −0.62 between daily outdoor temperature and hot chocolate sales. What does the negative sign of r indicate?","a": "As outdoor temperature increases, hot chocolate sales tend to decrease.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["As outdoor temperature increases, hot chocolate sales tend to decrease.","As outdoor temperature increases, hot chocolate sales tend to increase.","Hot chocolate sales are negative numbers.","There is no linear relationship between temperature and sales."]},{"id": "Geo.13-11","std": "Geo.13","q": "Order the following correlation coefficients from weakest linear association to strongest linear association: r = 0.35, r = −0.82, r = −0.12, r = 0.68.","a": "−0.12, 0.35, 0.68, −0.82","lvl": 1,"choices": ["−0.82, −0.12, 0.35, 0.68","−0.12, 0.35, 0.68, −0.82","0.68, 0.35, −0.12, −0.82","−0.82, 0.68, 0.35, −0.12"]},{"id": "Geo.13-12","std": "Geo.13","q": "Which inequality describes all mathematically possible values of the correlation coefficient r?","a": "−1 ≤ r ≤ 1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0 ≤ r ≤ 1","−100 ≤ r ≤ 100","−1 ≤ r ≤ 1","r ≥ 0"]},{"id": "Geo.13-13","std": "Geo.13","q": "What is the exact correlation coefficient r for the data set {(1, 14), (3, 10), (5, 6), (7, 2)}?","a": "−1","lvl": 2,"choices": ["−2","1","−0.5","−1"]},{"id": "Geo.13-14","std": "Geo.13","q": "A biologist measures leaf length in centimeters and insect mass in grams and computes r = 0.78. If the biologist converts all leaf lengths to inches and all insect masses to ounces, what is the new correlation coefficient?","a": "0.78","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.78","0.31","1.00","−0.78"]},{"id": "Geo.13-15","std": "Geo.13","q": "Compute the exact correlation coefficient r for the data set {(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 5), (4, 4), (5, 6)}.","a": "0.9","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.81","0.9","0.95","1.0"]},{"id": "Geo.13-16","std": "Geo.13","q": "A student swaps the x- and y-variables on a scatter plot (putting the old y on the horizontal axis and the old x on the vertical axis). If the original data had r = −0.84, how do the correlation coefficient r and the slope of the least-squares regression line behave?","a": "r stays −0.84, while the slope generally changes.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["r changes to +0.84, and the slope stays the same.","Both r and the slope are inverted to their reciprocals.","r stays −0.84, while the slope generally changes.","Both r and the slope remain unchanged."]},{"id": "Geo.13-17","std": "Geo.13","q": "Compute the correlation coefficient r (rounded to the nearest hundredth) for the data set {(0, 10), (2, 8), (4, 4), (6, 2)}, and interpret its direction and strength.","a": "r = −0.99; very strong negative linear correlation","lvl": 3,"choices": ["r = −0.98; very strong negative linear correlation","r = 0.99; very strong positive linear correlation","r = −0.70; moderate negative linear correlation","r = −0.99; very strong negative linear correlation"]},{"id": "Geo.13-18","std": "Geo.13","q": "A student computes r = 0.02 for a bivariate data set and concludes, 'There is no relationship of any kind between x and y.' Why can this conclusion be false?","a": "r measures only linear association; points on a U-shaped parabola or a circle can have r near 0 but a strong nonlinear relationship.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["r measures only linear association; points on a U-shaped parabola or a circle can have r near 0 but a strong nonlinear relationship.","r near 0 means the data have outliers, which always hide a strong linear relationship.","The conclusion is always true, because r = 0 means the variables are unrelated.","r near 0 means the slope is 0, so y must be constant."]},{"id": "Geo.14-10","std": "Geo.14","q": "A report finds a strong positive correlation between the number of backyard swimming pools in a city and the number of sunglasses sold each year. Does building more swimming pools cause people to buy more sunglasses?","a": "No; a warmer, sunnier climate or larger population likely drives both.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Yes; owning a pool forces residents to purchase sunglasses.","No; a warmer, sunnier climate or larger population likely drives both.","Yes; any strong positive correlation proves direct causation.","No; sunglasses cause people to build swimming pools."]},{"id": "Geo.14-11","std": "Geo.14","q": "Which pair of variables most likely represents a causal relationship rather than correlation alone?","a": "Hanging more weight on a spring and increasing how far the spring stretches","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Number of churches in a city and number of restaurants in the city","A student's height and the student's vocabulary score across grades 1 through 12","Hanging more weight on a spring and increasing how far the spring stretches","Monthly snow-shovel sales and monthly hot-soup sales"]},{"id": "Geo.14-12","std": "Geo.14","q": "What is a lurking (confounding) variable in an observational study?","a": "An unmeasured third variable that influences both variables being studied, creating an association between them","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The dependent variable plotted on the y-axis of a scatter plot","A data-entry mistake that changes the sign of the slope","The variable that a researcher deliberately changes in an experiment","An unmeasured third variable that influences both variables being studied, creating an association between them"]},{"id": "Geo.14-13","std": "Geo.14","q": "Data across 50 towns show a strong positive correlation (r = 0.89) between the number of public libraries and the number of traffic accidents per year. What is the most plausible lurking variable?","a": "Total town population","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Total town population","Average book length","Annual rainfall","Speed limit outside libraries"]},{"id": "Geo.14-14","std": "Geo.14","q": "Hospital records show that patients given high-dose pain medication have longer hospital stays than patients who are not. A blogger claims the medication causes longer hospital stays. What is the main flaw in the blogger's claim?","a": "More severe injuries or illnesses cause both stronger medication use and longer hospital stays.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Hospital stays cannot be measured as quantitative data.","More severe injuries or illnesses cause both stronger medication use and longer hospital stays.","Longer hospital stays cannot be correlated with medication doses.","The correlation coefficient must be negative for causation to exist."]},{"id": "Geo.14-15","std": "Geo.14","q": "Which research design provides the strongest evidence that a new practice app actually causes higher geometry exam scores?","a": "Randomly assigning students to either use the app or not use the app and comparing their exam scores","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Surveying students who chose to download the app and checking their grades","Plotting app downloads and national exam averages over the past ten years","Randomly assigning students to either use the app or not use the app and comparing their exam scores","Interviewing ten teachers about whether they like the app"]},{"id": "Geo.14-16","std": "Geo.14","q": "Industry data from 2010 to 2024 show a strong negative correlation (r = −0.85) between annual music CD sales and average smartphone screen size. Why does this strong correlation fail to prove that larger phone screens caused CD sales to drop?","a": "Both variables changed over the same time period due to broader technology trends rather than one directly causing the other.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A negative correlation can never indicate a causal link.","r = −0.85 is too weak to show any pattern on a scatter plot.","CD sales are categorical data, so correlation cannot be computed.","Both variables changed over the same time period due to broader technology trends rather than one directly causing the other."]},{"id": "Geo.14-17","std": "Geo.14","q": "A large observational health survey finds that adults who eat breakfast daily have lower rates of heart disease than adults who skip breakfast. Which statement best explains why this survey alone cannot prove that eating breakfast prevents heart disease?","a": "Adults who eat breakfast daily may also exercise more, smoke less, or have other healthier habits that confound the result.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Adults who eat breakfast daily may also exercise more, smoke less, or have other healthier habits that confound the result.","Observational surveys cannot find correlations between health behaviors.","Heart disease has no relationship to diet or lifestyle.","Eating breakfast actually causes heart disease because the correlation is negative."]},{"id": "Geo.14-18","std": "Geo.14","q": "A school district finds a strong positive correlation between the number of graphing calculators per household and student SAT math scores. A board member proposes giving every household three extra calculators to raise scores. Why may this plan fail?","a": "Correlation does not show causation; a lurking variable such as household income or advanced math courses likely drives both calculator ownership and scores.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The correlation is positive, so adding calculators would lower SAT scores.","Correlation does not show causation; a lurking variable such as household income or advanced math courses likely drives both calculator ownership and scores.","Three calculators is too few; the correlation proves more calculators cause higher scores.","The plan will work, because a strong correlation proves owning calculators raises scores."]},{"id": "Geo.15-10","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A bike rental shop models the total cost C (in dollars) for h hours as C = 6.5h + 10. What is the predicted cost to rent a bike for 4 hours?","a": "§36","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§26","§46.50","§36","§66"]},{"id": "Geo.15-11","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "The temperature T (in °F) of a cooling cup of tea after m minutes is modeled by T = −2.4m + 185. What does the constant term 185 represent?","a": "The initial temperature of the tea is 185°F at m = 0 minutes.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The tea cools by 185°F each minute.","It takes 185 minutes for the tea to cool completely.","The room temperature is 185°F.","The initial temperature of the tea is 185°F at m = 0 minutes."]},{"id": "Geo.15-12","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "A solar panel's total energy output E (in kilowatt-hours) over d sunny days is modeled by E = 18d + 5. What does the slope 18 represent in this context?","a": "The panel produces 18 kilowatt-hours of energy for each additional sunny day.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The panel produces 18 kilowatt-hours of energy for each additional sunny day.","The panel already stored 18 kilowatt-hours before day 0.","The panel produces 5 kilowatt-hours every 18 days.","It takes 18 days to generate 5 kilowatt-hours."]},{"id": "Geo.15-13","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A printer's ink cartridge starts full and prints pages according to the model I = 100 − 0.4p, where I is the percent of ink remaining and p is the number of pages printed. How many pages have been printed when 32% of the ink remains?","a": "170 pages","lvl": 2,"choices": ["80 pages","170 pages","330 pages","68 pages"]},{"id": "Geo.15-14","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "A linear regression model predicts a runner's 10K race time y (in minutes) from weekly training miles x: y = −0.55x + 68. Which statement correctly interprets the slope?","a": "For each additional mile trained per week, the predicted 10K race time decreases by 0.55 minutes.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["For each additional minute of racing, the runner trains 0.55 fewer miles.","A runner who trains 0 miles per week finishes in −0.55 minutes.","For each additional mile trained per week, the predicted 10K race time decreases by 0.55 minutes.","Every runner's 10K race time decreases by 68 minutes per mile trained."]},{"id": "Geo.15-15","std": "Geo.15","q": "A delivery drone climbs at a constant rate from an altitude of 40 feet at t = 2 seconds to 130 feet at t = 8 seconds. What is the linear model for altitude A (in feet) after t seconds, and what is the predicted altitude at t = 15 seconds?","a": "A = 15t + 10; 235 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A = 15t + 40; 265 feet","A = 11.25t + 17.5; 186.25 feet","A = 15t − 10; 215 feet","A = 15t + 10; 235 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.15-16","std": "Geo.15","q": "A caterer offers two pricing plans for a banquet of n guests. Plan 1 charges §18 per guest plus a §450 setup fee. Plan 2 charges §24 per guest plus a §150 setup fee. If a school expects 65 guests, which plan is cheaper and by how much?","a": "Plan 1 is cheaper by §90.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Plan 1 is cheaper by §90.","Plan 2 is cheaper by §90.","Plan 1 is cheaper by §300.","Both plans cost the exact same amount for 65 guests."]},{"id": "Geo.15-17","std": "Geo.15","part": "b","q": "For passenger cars weighing between 2,500 lb and 5,000 lb, fuel efficiency m (in miles per gallon) is modeled by m = −0.008w + 54, where w is weight in pounds. What does the slope mean, and why is predicting m for a 9,000-pound truck unreliable?","a": "Each additional 1,000 pounds lowers predicted efficiency by 8 mpg; 9,000 pounds is far beyond the data range and gives an impossible −18 mpg.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Each additional pound lowers predicted efficiency by 8 mpg; 9,000 pounds gives 126 mpg.","Each additional 1,000 pounds lowers predicted efficiency by 8 mpg; 9,000 pounds is far beyond the data range and gives an impossible −18 mpg.","Each additional 1,000 pounds raises predicted efficiency by 8 mpg; the model only works for trucks.","The slope has no meaning, and 9,000 pounds gives 54 mpg."]},{"id": "Geo.15-18","std": "Geo.15","part": "a","q": "A club's T-shirt fundraiser has a linear profit model P (in dollars) for s shirts sold. Selling 20 shirts results in a profit of −§120, and selling 50 shirts results in a profit of §240. What is the profit model, and what is the minimum number of shirts the club must sell to earn at least §600 in profit?","a": "P = 12s − 360; 80 shirts","lvl": 3,"choices": ["P = 4s − 200; 200 shirts","P = 12s − 120; 60 shirts","P = 12s − 360; 80 shirts","P = 12s + 360; 20 shirts"]},{"id": "Geo.16-10","std": "Geo.16","q": "A solid sphere is sliced by any flat plane that passes through its interior. What is the shape of every possible cross-section?","a": "Circle","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Ellipse","Square","Triangle","Circle"]},{"id": "Geo.16-11","std": "Geo.16","q": "A square pyramid is sliced by a plane parallel to its square base (between the base and the apex). What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Square","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Square","Triangle","Trapezoid","Circle"]},{"id": "Geo.16-12","std": "Geo.16","q": "A sphere of radius 10 cm is sliced by a plane 6 cm from its center. What are the shape and radius of the cross-section?","a": "A circle with radius 8 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A circle with radius 10 cm","A circle with radius 8 cm","A circle with radius 4 cm","An ellipse with axes 16 cm and 20 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.16-13","std": "Geo.16","q": "An isosceles trapezoid is rotated 360° around its line of symmetry passing through the midpoints of both parallel bases. What three-dimensional solid is generated?","a": "Frustum of a cone (truncated cone)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Cylinder","Sphere","Frustum of a cone (truncated cone)","Triangular prism"]},{"id": "Geo.16-14","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right circular cone is sliced by a plane that is parallel to one slant edge of the cone and does not pass through the apex. What shape is the cross-section?","a": "A region bounded by a parabola and a line segment","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A circle","An ellipse","A triangle","A region bounded by a parabola and a line segment"]},{"id": "Geo.16-15","std": "Geo.16","q": "A right circular cylinder is sliced by a plane that intersects only the curved lateral surface at a 45° angle to the bases (without hitting either base). What is the shape of the cross-section?","a": "Ellipse","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Ellipse","Circle","Rectangle","Triangle"]},{"id": "Geo.16-16","std": "Geo.16","q": "An isosceles triangle with base 10 cm and height 12 cm is rotated 360° around its base (the 10 cm side). What three-dimensional solid is formed, and what are its dimensions?","a": "Two congruent cones joined base-to-base, each with radius 12 cm and height 5 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A single cone with radius 5 cm and height 12 cm","Two congruent cones joined base-to-base, each with radius 12 cm and height 5 cm","Two congruent cones joined base-to-base, each with radius 5 cm and height 12 cm","A cylinder with radius 12 cm and height 10 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.16-17","std": "Geo.16","q": "A cube has edge length 6 cm. A flat plane slices through the three vertices that are directly adjacent to one corner vertex (forming face diagonals across three faces). What exact shape is the cross-section, and what is the length of each side?","a": "An equilateral triangle with side length 6√2 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A right triangle with legs 6 cm and hypotenuse 6√2 cm","An equilateral triangle with side length 6 cm","An equilateral triangle with side length 6√2 cm","A square with side length 6√2 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.16-18","std": "Geo.16","q": "A circle of radius 3 cm lies in a plane with its center located 7 cm away from a line L in the same plane. If the circle is rotated 360° around line L, what three-dimensional object is generated, and what is the diameter of its central hole?","a": "A torus (donut shape) with a central hole diameter of 8 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A hollow cylinder with a central hole diameter of 14 cm","A sphere with a diameter of 14 cm","A torus (donut shape) with a central hole diameter of 4 cm","A torus (donut shape) with a central hole diameter of 8 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.17-10","std": "Geo.17","q": "Find the volume of a square pyramid with a base side length of 6 m and a perpendicular height of 10 m.","a": "120 m³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["120 m³","360 m³","60 m³","20 m³"]},{"id": "Geo.17-11","std": "Geo.17","q": "Find the surface area of a sphere with radius 5 cm. Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "100π cm²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["25π cm²","100π cm²","50π cm²","(500/3)π cm²"]},{"id": "Geo.17-12","std": "Geo.17","part": "a","q": "A cube of side length s can be dissected into three congruent square pyramids, each with base area B = s² and height h = s. Which volume formula does this dissection argument support?","a": "V = (1/3)Bh for a pyramid","lvl": 1,"choices": ["V = (1/2)Bh for a pyramid","V = 3Bh for a pyramid","V = (1/3)Bh for a pyramid","V = (4/3)πr³ for a sphere"]},{"id": "Geo.17-13","std": "Geo.17","part": "b","q": "A right circular cone has a base radius of 5 cm and a slant height of 13 cm. Find the perpendicular height of the cone and its volume in terms of π.","a": "Height = 12 cm; volume = 100π cm³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Height = 12 cm; volume = 300π cm³","Height = 8 cm; volume = (200/3)π cm³","Height = 13 cm; volume = (325/3)π cm³","Height = 12 cm; volume = 100π cm³"]},{"id": "Geo.17-14","std": "Geo.17","part": "a","q": "To informally justify the volume formula of a cylinder of radius r and height h, regular n-sided prisms are inscribed inside the cylinder as n increases without bound. What happens as n approaches ∞?","a": "The polygon's area approaches πr², so the prism's volume Bh approaches πr²h.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The polygon's area approaches πr², so the prism's volume Bh approaches πr²h.","The prism's height approaches 0, so the volume approaches 0.","The polygon's area approaches 2πr, so the volume approaches 2πrh.","The prism's volume approaches (1/3)πr²h."]},{"id": "Geo.17-15","std": "Geo.17","q": "A solid wooden block is a rectangular prism measuring 8 in. by 6 in. by 5 in. A smaller rectangular notch measuring 4 in. by 3 in. by 5 in. is completely cut out of one corner. What is the remaining volume of the block?","a": "180 in.³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["300 in.³","180 in.³","60 in.³","216 in.³"]},{"id": "Geo.17-16","std": "Geo.17","q": "A grain silo consists of a right circular cylinder of radius 6 ft and height 15 ft topped by a right circular cone of radius 6 ft and slant height 10 ft. Including the flat circular floor at the bottom (but not the interior circle between the cylinder and cone), what is the total exterior surface area of the silo in terms of π?","a": "276π ft²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["240π ft²","312π ft²","276π ft²","336π ft²"]},{"id": "Geo.17-17","std": "Geo.17","part": "b","q": "A regular square pyramid has a base edge of 12 cm and a total surface area of 384 cm². Find its volume.","a": "384 cm³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["480 cm³","1,152 cm³","576 cm³","384 cm³"]},{"id": "Geo.17-18","std": "Geo.17","part": "a","q": "A hemisphere of radius r sits next to a cylinder of radius r and height r with an inverted cone (apex at the bottom center, base radius r at the top, height r) removed. At every height y (0 ≤ y ≤ r), both solids have cross-sectional area π(r² − y²). What volume does Cavalieri's Principle give for a full sphere?","a": "V = 2(πr³ − (1/3)πr³) = (4/3)πr³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["V = 2(πr³ − (1/3)πr³) = (4/3)πr³","V = πr³ − (1/3)πr³ = (2/3)πr³","V = 2(πr³ + (1/3)πr³) = (8/3)πr³","V = 2πr³ − (1/3)πr³ = (5/3)πr³"]},{"id": "Geo.18-10","std": "Geo.18","q": "A rectangle has vertices at (−3, −1), (5, −1), (5, 4), and (−3, 4). What is its area?","a": "40 square units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["26 square units","40 square units","15 square units","20 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-11","std": "Geo.18","q": "A triangle has vertices at P(2, 1), Q(10, 1), and R(6, 7). What is its area?","a": "24 square units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["48 square units","28 square units","24 square units","14 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-12","std": "Geo.18","q": "Find the exact length of the side connecting vertices M(−2, 3) and N(4, −5) using the distance formula.","a": "10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["14","2√7","100","10"]},{"id": "Geo.18-13","std": "Geo.18","q": "A trapezoid has consecutive vertices at A(1, 2), B(9, 2), C(7, 6), and D(3, 6). What is its area?","a": "24 square units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["24 square units","48 square units","32 square units","16 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-14","std": "Geo.18","q": "A right triangle has vertices at A(−2, 1), B(3, 1), and C(3, 13). What is its perimeter?","a": "30 units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["17 units","30 units","34 units","60 units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-15","std": "Geo.18","q": "A triangle has exact side lengths √10, √20, and √18. A student rounds each side length to the nearest tenth (3.2, 4.5, and 4.2) before adding to get 11.9, while geometry software reports 11.88. Why do the two results differ slightly?","a": "Rounding intermediate side lengths before adding introduced cumulative rounding error; keeping full precision until the final step gives 11.88.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Geometry software uses a different distance formula that ignores square roots.","The student computed area instead of perimeter.","Rounding intermediate side lengths before adding introduced cumulative rounding error; keeping full precision until the final step gives 11.88.","The perimeter of a triangle with radical sides cannot be approximated."]},{"id": "Geo.18-16","std": "Geo.18","q": "A kite has consecutive vertices at A(0, 5), B(3, 2), C(0, −4), and D(−3, 2). What are its exact perimeter and exact area?","a": "Perimeter = 6√2 + 6√5; Area = 27 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Perimeter = 3√2 + 3√5; Area = 27 square units","Perimeter = 6√2 + 6√5; Area = 54 square units","Perimeter = 12√7; Area = 27 square units","Perimeter = 6√2 + 6√5; Area = 27 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-17","std": "Geo.18","q": "Find the exact area of the pentagon with consecutive vertices at A(0, 0), B(6, 0), C(8, 3), D(4, 7), and E(0, 4).","a": "39 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["39 square units","56 square units","42 square units","35 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.18-18","std": "Geo.18","q": "A tilted rectangle has consecutive vertices at P(−1, 2), Q(5, 5), R(7, 1), and S(1, −2). What are its exact perimeter and exact area?","a": "Perimeter = 10√5; Area = 30 square units","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Perimeter = 5√5; Area = 30 square units","Perimeter = 10√5; Area = 30 square units","Perimeter = 10√5; Area = 15 square units","Perimeter = 10√5; Area = 6√5 square units"]},{"id": "Geo.19-10","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar hexagons have a scale factor of 4 from the smaller to the larger. What is the ratio of the area of the smaller hexagon to the area of the larger hexagon?","a": "1:16","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1:4","1:8","1:16","1:64"]},{"id": "Geo.19-11","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar rectangular prisms have corresponding edge lengths of 2 cm and 10 cm. If the smaller prism has a volume of 12 cm³, what is the volume of the larger prism?","a": "1,500 cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60 cm³","300 cm³","120 cm³","1,500 cm³"]},{"id": "Geo.19-12","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar pentagons have perimeters of 24 in. and 36 in. What is the scale factor from the smaller pentagon to the larger pentagon?","a": "3/2","lvl": 1,"choices": ["3/2","2/3","9/4","12"]},{"id": "Geo.19-13","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar spheres have volumes in the ratio 8:125. What is the ratio of their surface areas?","a": "4:25","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2:5","4:25","8:125","16:625"]},{"id": "Geo.19-14","std": "Geo.19","q": "A blueprint of a park uses a scale of 1 inch = 20 feet. If a pond has an area of 3.5 square inches on the blueprint, what is the actual area of the pond in square feet?","a": "1,400 square feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["70 square feet","28,000 square feet","1,400 square feet","700 square feet"]},{"id": "Geo.19-15","std": "Geo.19","q": "Two similar triangles have areas of 18 cm² and 50 cm². If a side of the smaller triangle is 9 cm, what is the length of the corresponding side of the larger triangle?","a": "15 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["25 cm","27 cm","18 cm","15 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.19-16","std": "Geo.19","q": "A cylindrical soup can holds 16 ounces of soup. A company designs a similar family-size can by multiplying both the radius and the height by 1.5. How many ounces of soup will the family-size can hold, and by what factor does its surface area increase?","a": "54 ounces; surface area increases by a factor of 2.25","lvl": 3,"choices": ["54 ounces; surface area increases by a factor of 2.25","24 ounces; surface area increases by a factor of 1.5","36 ounces; surface area increases by a factor of 2.25","54 ounces; surface area increases by a factor of 3.375"]},{"id": "Geo.19-17","std": "Geo.19","q": "A solid cone of height 12 cm is sliced by a plane parallel to its base at a distance of 4 cm below the apex, creating a smaller similar cone on top and a frustum on the bottom. What fraction of the original cone's total volume is in the bottom frustum?","a": "26/27","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2/3","26/27","8/9","1/27"]},{"id": "Geo.19-18","std": "Geo.19","q": "A rectangular prism with dimensions l, w, and h is scaled to kl, kw, and kh. Which statement correctly justifies how its surface area and volume change?","a": "Each face area is a product of two scaled lengths, so surface area is multiplied by k²; volume is a product of three, so it is multiplied by k³.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Every measurement is multiplied by k, so surface area and volume are both multiplied by k.","Surface area is multiplied by 2k because each face appears twice, and volume by 3k because there are three dimensions.","Each face area is a product of two scaled lengths, so surface area is multiplied by k²; volume is a product of three, so it is multiplied by k³.","Surface area is multiplied by k³ and volume by k², because volume is inside the surface."]},{"id": "Geo.20-10","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has an area of 60 m². What is the area of a sector with a central angle of 120°?","a": "20 m²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30 m²","15 m²","40 m²","20 m²"]},{"id": "Geo.20-11","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a radius of 10 cm. What is the exact length of an arc intercepted by a 180° central angle? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "10π cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["10π cm","20π cm","50π cm","5π cm"]},{"id": "Geo.20-12","std": "Geo.20","q": "A central angle θ (in degrees) intercepts an arc of length s on a circle of radius r. Which proportion correctly derives the arc length formula?","a": "s / (2πr) = θ / 360","lvl": 1,"choices": ["s / (πr²) = θ / 360","s / (2πr) = θ / 360","s / (2πr) = 360 / θ","s / r = θ / 180"]},{"id": "Geo.20-13","std": "Geo.20","q": "A lawn sprinkler sprays water up to 15 feet away and rotates through a central angle of 144°. What is the exact area of the lawn watered by the sprinkler in terms of π?","a": "90π ft²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12π ft²","45π ft²","90π ft²","180π ft²"]},{"id": "Geo.20-14","std": "Geo.20","q": "The minute hand of a clock is 12 inches long. How far does the tip of the minute hand travel in 25 minutes? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "10π inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["5π inches","12π inches","60π inches","10π inches"]},{"id": "Geo.20-15","std": "Geo.20","q": "A circle has a radius of 6 cm. What is the exact length of an arc intercepted by a central angle of π/3 radians?","a": "2π cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["2π cm","6π cm","4π cm","12π cm"]},{"id": "Geo.20-16","std": "Geo.20","q": "A windshield wiper pivots from a fixed point through an angle of 120°. The rubber wiper blade covers the region from 6 inches to 18 inches away from the pivot. What is the exact area cleaned by the blade in terms of π?","a": "96π in.²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["108π in.²","96π in.²","48π in.²","120π in.²"]},{"id": "Geo.20-17","std": "Geo.20","q": "A sector of a circle has an area of 27π cm² and a central angle of 120°. What is the exact perimeter of the sector (including both straight radii and the curved arc)?","a": "18 + 6π cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["6π cm","9 + 6π cm","18 + 6π cm","18 + 18π cm"]},{"id": "Geo.20-18","std": "Geo.20","q": "Two concentric circles have radii of 8 cm and 12 cm. A sector of the smaller circle has a central angle of 135°. What central angle (in degrees) is needed in the larger circle so that its sector has the exact same area?","a": "60°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["90°","45°","75°","60°"]},{"id": "Geo.21-10","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Which function rule represents a reflection across the line y = x?","a": "(x, y) → (y, x)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(x, y) → (y, x)","(x, y) → (x, −y)","(x, y) → (−y, −x)","(x, y) → (−x, y)"]},{"id": "Geo.21-11","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "Which transformation rule preserves BOTH distance and angle measure?","a": "(x, y) → (x − 5, y + 2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(x, y) → (3x, 3y)","(x, y) → (x − 5, y + 2)","(x, y) → (x, 4y)","(x, y) → (2x, −y)"]},{"id": "Geo.21-12","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Apply the transformation function T(x, y) = (−y, x) to the input point (5, −2). What is the output point?","a": "(2, 5)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(−2, 5)","(2, −5)","(2, 5)","(−5, 2)"]},{"id": "Geo.21-13","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "A rectangle with vertices (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 2), and (0, 2) undergoes the horizontal shear (x, y) → (x + 2y, y). What does this transformation preserve?","a": "Neither distance nor angle measure","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Both distance and angle measure","Angle measure, but not distance","Distance, but not angle measure","Neither distance nor angle measure"]},{"id": "Geo.21-14","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "A point P(x, y) is reflected across the x-axis using (x, y) → (x, −y) and then translated using (x, y) → (x + 3, y − 5), producing the image P'(7, −1). What were the original coordinates of P?","a": "(4, −4)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, −4)","(4, 4)","(10, 6)","(−4, 4)"]},{"id": "Geo.21-15","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "A graphic designer reflects a shape across the y-axis and then reflects the result across the x-axis. What single function rule represents this composition, and which single transformation is it equivalent to?","a": "(x, y) → (−x, −y); a 180° rotation about the origin","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(x, y) → (y, x); a reflection across y = x","(x, y) → (−x, −y); a 180° rotation about the origin","(x, y) → (x, y); the identity transformation","(x, y) → (−y, x); a 90° counterclockwise rotation about the origin"]},{"id": "Geo.21-16","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "Let F(x, y) = (x + 4, y) and G(x, y) = (y, x). Compare the image of point P(1, 3) when F is applied first and then G versus when G is applied first and then F.","a": "F then G gives (3, 5), while G then F gives (7, 1); order matters.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Both orders give (5, 3); order does not matter.","F then G gives (7, 1), while G then F gives (3, 5); order matters.","F then G gives (3, 5), while G then F gives (7, 1); order matters.","Both orders give (3, 5); order does not matter."]},{"id": "Geo.21-17","std": "Geo.21","part": "b","q": "A right triangle with vertices (0, 0), (3, 0), and (0, 4) undergoes the vertical stretch (x, y) → (x, 2y). How do the image's side lengths and acute angle measures compare to the preimage?","a": "Neither distance nor all angle measures are preserved; the hypotenuse changes from 5 to √73 and both acute angles change.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["All side lengths double so the hypotenuse becomes 10, while all angle measures stay unchanged.","Angle measures stay unchanged, while only the vertical leg doubles.","Distance is preserved because the horizontal leg stays 3, and all angles stay unchanged.","Neither distance nor all angle measures are preserved; the hypotenuse changes from 5 to √73 and both acute angles change."]},{"id": "Geo.21-18","std": "Geo.21","part": "a","q": "A point (x, y) is rotated 90° clockwise about the origin, then reflected across the line y = x, and finally translated by (x, y) → (x − 2, y + 6). What single function rule represents this three-step composition?","a": "(x, y) → (−x − 2, y + 6)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(x, y) → (−x − 2, y + 6)","(x, y) → (x − 2, −y + 6)","(x, y) → (y − 2, −x + 6)","(x, y) → (−y − 2, x + 6)"]},{"id": "Geo.22-10","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Triangle XYZ has vertex X at (−4, 2). What are the coordinates of X' after a translation 6 units right and 5 units down?","a": "(2, −3)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(−10, 7)","(2, −3)","(2, 7)","(−10, −3)"]},{"id": "Geo.22-11","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "An isosceles trapezoid that is not a rectangle is drawn on graph paper. How many lines of symmetry does it have?","a": "1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2","0","1","4"]},{"id": "Geo.22-12","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(2, 1), B(5, 1), and C(2, 3). Its image has vertices A'(−1, 2), B'(−1, 5), and C'(−3, 2). Which single transformation maps triangle ABC onto triangle A'B'C'?","a": "Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin","Reflection across the line y = x","Rotation 180° about the origin","Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin"]},{"id": "Geo.22-13","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Point M(6, 1) is reflected across the vertical line x = 2. What are the coordinates of M'?","a": "(−2, 1)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(−2, 1)","(−6, 1)","(2, 1)","(6, −3)"]},{"id": "Geo.22-14","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "A regular hexagon is centered at the origin. Which list gives ALL angles of rotation between 0° and 360° that map the hexagon onto itself?","a": "60°, 120°, 180°, 240°, and 300°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["45°, 90°, 135°, 180°, 225°, 270°, and 315°","60°, 120°, 180°, 240°, and 300°","90°, 180°, and 270°","72°, 144°, 216°, and 288°"]},{"id": "Geo.22-15","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "Triangle PQR has vertices P(1, 2), Q(4, 2), and R(1, 4). Triangle P'Q'R' has vertices P'(−1, 1), Q'(−1, −2), and R'(1, 1). Which sequence of transformations maps PQR onto P'Q'R'?","a": "Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin, then translation 3 units left and 2 units up","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then translation 3 units left and 2 units up","Reflection across the y-axis, then translation 2 units down","Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin, then translation 3 units left and 2 units up","Rotation 180° about the origin, then translation 3 units right and 2 units down"]},{"id": "Geo.22-16","std": "Geo.22","part": "a","q": "Point A(5, 3) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the center point C(2, 1) (not the origin). What are the coordinates of image A'?","a": "(0, 4)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−3, 5)","(4, −2)","(5, 4)","(0, 4)"]},{"id": "Geo.22-17","std": "Geo.22","part": "c","q": "Which quadrilateral has BOTH exactly 2 lines of symmetry along its diagonals AND 180° rotational symmetry, without having 90° rotational symmetry?","a": "A non-square rhombus","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A non-square rhombus","A non-square rectangle","A square","An isosceles trapezoid"]},{"id": "Geo.22-18","std": "Geo.22","part": "b","q": "Triangle JKL has vertices J(1, 1), K(4, 1), and L(4, 3). Triangle J'K'L' has vertices J'(3, −2), K'(3, 1), and L'(5, 1). Which sequence of two rigid motions maps JKL onto J'K'L'?","a": "Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 2 units right and 3 units down","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then translation 2 units right and 3 units down","Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 2 units right and 3 units down","Reflection across the x-axis, then translation 2 units right and 1 unit down","Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 3 units right and 2 units down"]},{"id": "Geo.23-10","std": "Geo.23","q": "Point Q lies directly on the line of reflection m. Where is its image Q' located after a reflection across line m?","a": "At point Q itself (Q' = Q)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["One unit away from line m","On the opposite side of the origin from Q","At point Q itself (Q' = Q)","At the midpoint of line m"]},{"id": "Geo.23-11","std": "Geo.23","q": "Under a rotation of the plane by 110° counterclockwise about a center point O, which point remains fixed (maps to itself)?","a": "The center of rotation O","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Every point on the x-axis","No points in the plane remain fixed","Every point at distance 1 from O","The center of rotation O"]},{"id": "Geo.23-12","std": "Geo.23","q": "Segment AB is translated along a direction not parallel to line AB to form image segment A'B'. What type of quadrilateral is always formed by connecting A, B, B', and A' in order?","a": "Parallelogram","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Parallelogram","Kite","Trapezoid with only one pair of parallel sides","Regular square"]},{"id": "Geo.23-13","std": "Geo.23","q": "Point A(1, 4) is reflected across line m to produce image A'(1, −6). What is the equation of line m, and what is the perpendicular distance from A to line m?","a": "y = −1; distance = 5 units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["x = 1; distance = 5 units","y = −1; distance = 5 units","y = −1; distance = 10 units","y = 1; distance = 3 units"]},{"id": "Geo.23-14","std": "Geo.23","q": "Under a rotation about center O, point P maps to P' and point Q maps to Q'. If angle POP' = 48° and OQ = 7 cm, what are the measure of angle QOQ' and the length of OQ'?","a": "angle QOQ' = 48° and OQ' = 7 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["angle QOQ' = 96° and OQ' = 7 cm","angle QOQ' = 48° and OQ' = 14 cm","angle QOQ' = 48° and OQ' = 7 cm","angle QOQ' = 132° and OQ' = 7 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.23-15","std": "Geo.23","q": "Triangle ABC is reflected across line m to form triangle A'B'C'. How are line segments AA' and BB' related to each other?","a": "They are parallel to each other because both are perpendicular to line m.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["They are perpendicular to each other.","They must always have the exact same length.","They intersect on line m at the same point.","They are parallel to each other because both are perpendicular to line m."]},{"id": "Geo.23-16","std": "Geo.23","q": "Point P(1, 2) is reflected across line m to produce image P'(5, 6). Using the definition of reflection in terms of a perpendicular bisector, what is the equation of line m?","a": "y = −x + 7","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = −x + 7","y = x + 1","y = −x + 4","y = x + 7"]},{"id": "Geo.23-17","std": "Geo.23","q": "A figure is reflected across line r and then reflected across line s, where lines r and s intersect at point O forming an acute angle of 35°. What single transformation is equivalent to this composition?","a": "A rotation of 70° about point O","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A rotation of 35° about point O","A rotation of 70° about point O","A translation of 70 units","A reflection across the angle bisector of lines r and s"]},{"id": "Geo.23-18","std": "Geo.23","q": "Which statement is a precise definition of a translation along directed segment AB for a point P not on line AB?","a": "P maps to P' so that segment PP' is parallel to AB, has the same length as AB, and points in the same direction as from A to B.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["P maps to P' so that segment PP' is perpendicular to AB and has the same length as AB.","P maps to P' so that segment PP' is parallel to AB and points in the opposite direction from A to B.","P maps to P' so that segment PP' is parallel to AB, has the same length as AB, and points in the same direction as from A to B.","P maps to P' so that P' is the midpoint of segment PB."]},{"id": "Geo.24-10","std": "Geo.24","q": "Polygon WXYZ is rotated 90° clockwise and then reflected across the y-axis to form polygon W'X'Y'Z'. Are the two polygons congruent, and why?","a": "Yes, because rotations and reflections are rigid motions that preserve size and shape.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because a reflection reverses orientation.","No, because combining two transformations always changes side lengths.","Only if WXYZ is a regular polygon.","Yes, because rotations and reflections are rigid motions that preserve size and shape."]},{"id": "Geo.24-11","std": "Geo.24","q": "Circle 1 has radius 6 cm and Circle 2 has radius 6 cm. Why are these two circles congruent in terms of rigid motions?","a": "A translation carrying the center of Circle 1 to the center of Circle 2 maps Circle 1 exactly onto Circle 2.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A translation carrying the center of Circle 1 to the center of Circle 2 maps Circle 1 exactly onto Circle 2.","A dilation with scale factor 6 maps Circle 1 onto Circle 2.","Circles cannot be congruent because they have no straight sides.","They are congruent only if they have the exact same center."]},{"id": "Geo.24-12","std": "Geo.24","q": "Quadrilateral ABCD is mapped onto quadrilateral PQRS by a reflection. If angle B = 115° and CD = 9 cm, what are angle Q and side length RS?","a": "angle Q = 115° and RS = 9 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["angle Q = 65° and RS = 9 cm","angle Q = 115° and RS = 9 cm","angle Q = 115° and RS = 18 cm","angle Q = 65° and RS = 4.5 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.24-13","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 2), B(5, 2), and C(1, 5). Triangle DEF has vertices D(−1, 2), E(−5, 2), and F(−1, 5). Are the triangles congruent, and which single rigid motion maps ABC onto DEF?","a": "Yes; reflection across the y-axis","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes; translation 2 units left","Yes; rotation 180° about the origin","Yes; reflection across the y-axis","No; the triangles have opposite orientations so they are not congruent."]},{"id": "Geo.24-14","std": "Geo.24","q": "Two rectangles both have a perimeter of 24 cm: Rectangle 1 measures 8 cm by 4 cm, and Rectangle 2 measures 7 cm by 5 cm. Are the two rectangles congruent?","a": "No, because rigid motions preserve side lengths and the side lengths do not match.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because both are rectangles with the same perimeter.","Yes, because a rotation maps one rectangle onto the other.","Yes, because both have four 90° angles.","No, because rigid motions preserve side lengths and the side lengths do not match."]},{"id": "Geo.24-15","std": "Geo.24","q": "Segment AB has endpoints A(0, 0) and B(3, 4). Segment CD has endpoints C(2, −1) and D(6, 2). Are segments AB and CD congruent?","a": "Yes, because both segments have length 5, so a translation and rotation map AB onto CD.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because both segments have length 5, so a translation and rotation map AB onto CD.","No, because segments AB and CD have different slopes.","No, because AB has length 5 and CD has length 7.","No, because neither endpoint of CD is at the origin."]},{"id": "Geo.24-16","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 1), B(4, 1), and C(1, 3). Triangle PQR has vertices P(1, 4), Q(1, 1), and R(3, 4). Which sequence of rigid motions proves triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR?","a": "Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin, then translation 5 units up","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then translation 5 units up","Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin, then translation 5 units up","Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 3 units up","Translation 3 units up, then reflection across the y-axis"]},{"id": "Geo.24-17","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle JKL has vertices J(0, 0), K(4, 0), and L(0, 3). Triangle MNP has vertices M(2, 1), N(2, 6), and P(5, 1). Are the two triangles congruent?","a": "No; JKL is a right triangle with legs 3 and 4, while MNP is a right triangle with legs 3 and 5, so no rigid motion maps JKL onto MNP.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Yes; both are right triangles with a leg of length 3 and a side of length 5.","Yes; a 90° rotation and translation map JKL onto MNP.","No; JKL is a right triangle with legs 3 and 4, while MNP is a right triangle with legs 3 and 5, so no rigid motion maps JKL onto MNP.","No; triangle MNP is not a right triangle."]},{"id": "Geo.24-18","std": "Geo.24","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(2, 1), B(6, 1), and C(6, 4). Triangle DEF has vertices D(0, 4), E(0, 8), and F(3, 8). Which sequence of rigid motions maps A to D, B to E, and C to F to prove congruence?","a": "Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 1 unit left and 2 units up","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then translation 1 unit left and 2 units up","Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 2 units left and 1 unit up","Reflection across the y-axis, then translation 2 units right and 3 units up","Reflection across the line y = x, then translation 1 unit left and 2 units up"]},{"id": "Geo.25-10","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "If triangle JKL is congruent to triangle RST, which angle must be congruent to angle K?","a": "angle S","lvl": 1,"choices": ["angle S","angle R","angle T","angle L"]},{"id": "Geo.25-11","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In triangles PQR and XYZ, PQ = XY, QR = YZ, and PR = XZ. Which congruence criterion proves triangle PQR is congruent to triangle XYZ?","a": "SSS","lvl": 1,"choices": ["SAS","SSS","ASA","AAS"]},{"id": "Geo.25-12","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In triangles ABC and DEF, angle A = angle D, AB = DE, and angle B = angle E. Which congruence criterion proves triangle ABC is congruent to triangle DEF?","a": "ASA","lvl": 1,"choices": ["AAS","SAS","ASA","SSS"]},{"id": "Geo.25-13","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Segments AC and BD intersect at point E such that AE = CE and BE = DE. Using the vertical angles at E, which congruence criterion proves triangle AEB is congruent to triangle CED?","a": "SAS","lvl": 2,"choices": ["SSS","ASA","AAS","SAS"]},{"id": "Geo.25-14","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is congruent to triangle FGH. If AB = 8 cm, BC = 11 cm, and the perimeter of triangle FGH is 29 cm, what is the length of side FH?","a": "10 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10 cm","8 cm","11 cm","19 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.25-15","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In triangles WXY and ZMN, angle W = angle Z and WY = ZN. What ONE additional congruence statement is needed to prove triangle WXY is congruent to triangle ZMN specifically by ASA?","a": "angle Y = angle N","lvl": 2,"choices": ["angle X = angle M","angle Y = angle N","WX = ZM","XY = MN"]},{"id": "Geo.25-16","std": "Geo.25","part": "a","q": "Triangle MNP is congruent to triangle QRS. If angle M = (4x + 6)°, angle Q = (6x − 18)°, and angle N = (3x + 5)°, what is the measure of angle S?","a": "85°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["54°","41°","85°","95°"]},{"id": "Geo.25-17","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "In quadrilateral ABCD, diagonal AC is drawn. Given AB is parallel to CD and angle B = angle D, which congruence criterion proves triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA?","a": "AAS","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SAS","SSS","ASA","AAS"]},{"id": "Geo.25-18","std": "Geo.25","part": "b","q": "Triangles ABC and DEF have angle A = angle D, AB = DE, and angle B = angle E. After rigid motions map A to D and B to E, with C and F on the same side of line DE, why must C land on F?","a": "Angle A = angle D puts ray AC on ray DF, and angle B = angle E puts ray BC on ray EF; two rays meet at only one point, so C lands on F.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Angle A = angle D puts ray AC on ray DF, and angle B = angle E puts ray BC on ray EF; two rays meet at only one point, so C lands on F.","AB = DE forces AC = DF, so C lands on F.","Any two triangles with one pair of equal sides are congruent, so C lands on F.","The angles add to 180°, so the third sides must be equal and C lands on F."]},{"id": "Geo.26-10","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "A line segment of length 20 inches is dilated by a scale factor of 1/4. What is the length of the dilated image segment?","a": "5 inches","lvl": 1,"choices": ["80 inches","5 inches","16 inches","4 inches"]},{"id": "Geo.26-11","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Point B(−3, 5) is dilated with center at the origin (0, 0) and scale factor 4. What are the coordinates of image B'?","a": "(−12, 20)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(1, 9)","(−3, 20)","(−12, 20)","(12, −20)"]},{"id": "Geo.26-12","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "Line m has slope 3/5. After a dilation centered at point O with scale factor 2, what is the slope of the image line m'?","a": "3/5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["6/5","3/10","−5/3","3/5"]},{"id": "Geo.26-13","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "The line y = −(1/2)x + 4 is dilated with center at the origin (0, 0) and scale factor 1/2. What is the equation of the image line?","a": "y = −(1/2)x + 2","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = −(1/2)x + 2","y = −(1/4)x + 2","y = −(1/2)x + 8","y = −x + 2"]},{"id": "Geo.26-14","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Segment AB has endpoints A(2, 0) and B(2, 6). After a dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2.5, what is the length of image segment A'B'?","a": "15","lvl": 2,"choices": ["8.5","15","12","37.5"]},{"id": "Geo.26-15","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "Line p has equation y = 2x − 4 and is dilated with center C(2, 0) and scale factor 3. What is the equation of the image line p'?","a": "y = 2x − 4","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 2x − 12","y = 6x − 12","y = 2x − 4","y = 2x + 4"]},{"id": "Geo.26-16","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Under a dilation with scale factor 3, point A(4, 5) maps to image A'(10, 11). What are the coordinates of the center of dilation C?","a": "(1, 2)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(0, 0)","(2, 3)","(7, 8)","(1, 2)"]},{"id": "Geo.26-17","std": "Geo.26","part": "a","q": "Line m has equation y = 3x + 2. It is dilated with center C(0, 5) and scale factor 2. What is the equation of the image line m'?","a": "y = 3x − 1","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 3x − 1","y = 3x + 4","y = 6x − 1","y = 3x + 8"]},{"id": "Geo.26-18","std": "Geo.26","part": "b","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 2), B(4, 2), and C(1, 6). It is dilated with center P(−1, 0) and scale factor 1/2 to form triangle A'B'C'. What is the perimeter of triangle A'B'C'?","a": "6","lvl": 3,"choices": ["3","6","12","24"]},{"id": "Geo.27-10","std": "Geo.27","q": "Are all equilateral triangles similar to one another?","a": "Yes, because rigid motions and a dilation can map any equilateral triangle onto any other equilateral triangle.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because their side lengths can be different.","Only if they have the same perimeter.","Yes, because rigid motions and a dilation can map any equilateral triangle onto any other equilateral triangle.","No, because a dilation changes the 60° interior angles."]},{"id": "Geo.27-11","std": "Geo.27","q": "Triangle ABC is dilated by scale factor 1/3 centered at the origin and then translated 4 units up to form triangle DEF. How are triangles ABC and DEF related?","a": "They are similar, but not congruent.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["They are both similar and congruent.","They are neither similar nor congruent.","They are congruent, but not similar.","They are similar, but not congruent."]},{"id": "Geo.27-12","std": "Geo.27","q": "Square A has side length 10 cm and Square B has side length 4 cm. What scale factor maps Square A onto Square B?","a": "2/5","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2/5","5/2","6","4/25"]},{"id": "Geo.27-13","std": "Geo.27","q": "Are all rhombuses similar to one another?","a": "No, because rhombuses can have different interior angle measures, and similarity transformations preserve angle measure.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because all four sides of any rhombus are congruent.","No, because rhombuses can have different interior angle measures, and similarity transformations preserve angle measure.","Yes, because every rhombus can be dilated into a square.","No, because a rhombus has no lines of symmetry."]},{"id": "Geo.27-14","std": "Geo.27","q": "Triangle PQR has vertices P(0, 0), Q(4, 0), and R(0, 2). Triangle STU has vertices S(2, 1), T(10, 1), and U(2, 5). Which similarity transformation maps PQR onto STU?","a": "Dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2, then translation 2 units right and 1 unit up","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Translation 2 units right and 1 unit up, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2","Dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 1/2, then translation 2 units right and 1 unit up","Dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2, then translation 2 units right and 1 unit up","Dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2, then translation 1 unit right and 2 units up"]},{"id": "Geo.27-15","std": "Geo.27","q": "Rectangle J has length 12 cm and width 9 cm. Rectangle K has length 8 cm and width 6 cm. Are the two rectangles similar, and what scale factor maps Rectangle J onto Rectangle K?","a": "Yes; scale factor 2/3","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes; scale factor 3/2","No; the side lengths differ by 4 cm and 3 cm.","Yes; scale factor 4/9","Yes; scale factor 2/3"]},{"id": "Geo.27-16","std": "Geo.27","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(2, 0), B(6, 0), and C(2, 4). Triangle DEF has vertices D(0, 1), E(0, 3), and F(−2, 1). Which similarity transformation maps triangle ABC onto triangle DEF?","a": "Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 1/2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 1/2","Rotation 90° clockwise about the origin, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 1/2","Reflection across the line y = x, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 1/2","Rotation 90° counterclockwise about the origin, then dilation centered at the origin with scale factor 2"]},{"id": "Geo.27-17","std": "Geo.27","q": "Circle 1 has equation (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16, and Circle 2 has equation (x + 1)² + (y − 4)² = 36. Which similarity transformation maps Circle 1 onto Circle 2?","a": "Translation by (x, y) → (x − 4, y + 6), then dilation centered at (−1, 4) with scale factor 3/2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Translation by (x, y) → (x + 4, y − 6), then dilation centered at (−1, 4) with scale factor 3/2","Translation by (x, y) → (x − 4, y + 6), then dilation centered at (−1, 4) with scale factor 3/2","Translation by (x, y) → (x − 4, y + 6), then dilation centered at (−1, 4) with scale factor 9/4","Translation by (x, y) → (x − 4, y + 6), then dilation centered at (−1, 4) with scale factor 2/3"]},{"id": "Geo.27-18","std": "Geo.27","q": "Right triangle ABC has vertices A(0, 0), B(3, 0), and C(0, 4). Right triangle DEF has vertices D(1, 2), E(7, 2), and F(1, 8). Are the two triangles similar, and why?","a": "No, because no single scale factor maps legs of 3 and 4 onto legs of 6 and 6.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Yes, because all right triangles are similar.","Yes, because a dilation by scale factor 2 and translation (x + 1, y + 2) map ABC onto DEF.","No, because no single scale factor maps legs of 3 and 4 onto legs of 6 and 6.","No, because triangle DEF is not a right triangle."]},{"id": "Geo.28-10","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "Triangle PQR is similar to triangle XYZ with a scale factor of 4 from PQR to XYZ. If PR = 7 cm, what is the length of XZ?","a": "28 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["11 cm","1.75 cm","112 cm","28 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.28-11","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Which of the following is NOT a valid criterion for proving two triangles are similar?","a": "SSA","lvl": 1,"choices": ["SSA","AA","SSS","SAS"]},{"id": "Geo.28-12","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "Triangle ABC is similar to triangle MNP. If angle B = 52° and the ratio of side AB to side MN is 1:3, what is the measure of angle N?","a": "52°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["156°","52°","17.3°","128°"]},{"id": "Geo.28-13","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Triangle ABC has side lengths 6, 8, and 10. Triangle DEF has side lengths 9, 12, and 16. Are the two triangles similar?","a": "No, because the side ratios are not all equal (9/6 = 12/8 = 1.5, but 16/10 = 1.6).","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, by SSS similarity with scale factor 1.5.","Yes, because both triangles have even side lengths.","No, because the side ratios are not all equal (9/6 = 12/8 = 1.5, but 16/10 = 1.6).","Yes, by AA similarity."]},{"id": "Geo.28-14","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "A 6-foot-tall streetlight pole casts a 4-foot shadow at the same time a nearby oak tree casts a 22-foot shadow. Using similar right triangles, how tall is the oak tree?","a": "33 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["24 feet","14.7 feet","28 feet","33 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.28-15","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Segments AE and BD intersect at point C such that AC = 6, BC = 8, CE = 9, and CD = 12. Using the vertical angles at C, which criterion proves triangle ABC is similar to triangle EDC?","a": "SAS similarity","lvl": 2,"choices": ["SAS similarity","AA similarity","SSS similarity","ASA congruence"]},{"id": "Geo.28-16","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "In right triangle ABC (right angle at C), altitude CD is drawn to hypotenuse AB. If AD = 4 cm and DB = 9 cm, use similar triangles ADC and CDB to find the length of altitude CD.","a": "6 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["6.5 cm","6 cm","13 cm","36 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.28-17","std": "Geo.28","part": "a","q": "In triangle ABC, segment DE is drawn parallel to side BC with point D on side AB and point E on side AC. If AD = 6, DB = 4, and DE = 9, what is the length of side BC?","a": "15","lvl": 3,"choices": ["6","13.5","15","12"]},{"id": "Geo.28-18","std": "Geo.28","part": "b","q": "Triangles ABC and DEF have AB/DE = AC/DF = k and angle A = angle D. Which sequence of transformations verifies SAS similarity?","a": "Dilate DEF by scale factor k so D'E' = AB, D'F' = AC, and angle D' = angle A; then rigid motions map D'E'F' onto ABC by SAS congruence.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Dilate DEF by scale factor 1/k and then translate it, because dilations change angle measures.","Rotate DEF until angle D lies on angle A; the triangles are then congruent, so no dilation is needed.","Dilate ABC by scale factor k so its sides match DEF, then reflect it.","Dilate DEF by scale factor k so D'E' = AB, D'F' = AC, and angle D' = angle A; then rigid motions map D'E'F' onto ABC by SAS congruence."]},{"id": "Geo.29-10","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "Using a compass and straightedge, a student constructs the angle bisector of angle ABC. What relationship holds for every point P on the angle bisector?","a": "Point P is equidistant from rays BA and BC.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Point P is equidistant from rays BA and BC.","Point P is equidistant from points A and C.","Point P is the midpoint of segment AC.","Segment BP is always perpendicular to segment AC."]},{"id": "Geo.29-11","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "Which property is sufficient by itself to guarantee that a quadrilateral is a parallelogram?","a": "Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["One pair of opposite sides is parallel.","Both pairs of opposite sides are parallel.","Two pairs of consecutive sides are congruent.","Its diagonals are congruent."]},{"id": "Geo.29-12","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "In dynamic geometry software, a student draws triangle ABC and constructs segment DE connecting the midpoint D of AB to the midpoint E of AC. As vertex A is dragged, what relationship always holds?","a": "DE is parallel to BC and DE = (1/2)BC.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["DE is perpendicular to BC and DE = (1/2)BC.","DE is parallel to BC and DE = BC.","DE is parallel to BC and DE = (1/2)BC.","DE divides the triangle into two equal areas."]},{"id": "Geo.29-13","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "A student uses geometry software to inscribe a quadrilateral inside a circle (all four vertices on the circle) and drags the vertices around the circle while measuring all four interior angles. Which conjecture always holds?","a": "Opposite interior angles are always supplementary (sum to 180°).","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Opposite interior angles are always congruent.","All four interior angles must be 90°.","Adjacent interior angles are always supplementary.","Opposite interior angles are always supplementary (sum to 180°)."]},{"id": "Geo.29-14","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "A student constructs a quadrilateral with four congruent sides in geometry software, but dragging a vertex turns it into a non-square rhombus. What ONE additional constraint guarantees the figure is always a square?","a": "At least one interior angle is 90°.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["At least one interior angle is 90°.","Opposite sides are parallel.","The diagonals are perpendicular.","The diagonals bisect each other."]},{"id": "Geo.29-15","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "Using geometry software, a student constructs the three perpendicular bisectors of the sides of several acute, right, and obtuse triangles. What conjecture about their intersection point (the circumcenter) is always true?","a": "All three perpendicular bisectors meet at a single point equidistant from all three vertices of the triangle.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The intersection point always lies strictly inside the triangle.","All three perpendicular bisectors meet at a single point equidistant from all three vertices of the triangle.","All three perpendicular bisectors meet at a single point equidistant from all three sides.","The three perpendicular bisectors only intersect in equilateral triangles."]},{"id": "Geo.29-16","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "A quadrilateral has one pair of opposite sides that are BOTH parallel and congruent, and its diagonals are perpendicular to each other. What is the most specific quadrilateral guaranteed by these properties?","a": "Rhombus","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Square","Rectangle","Rhombus","Isosceles trapezoid"]},{"id": "Geo.29-17","std": "Geo.29","part": "a","q": "In geometry software, a student draws circle O and constructs two tangent segments PA and PB from an exterior point P to points of tangency A and B on the circle. As point P is dragged outside the circle, which two relationships ALWAYS remain true?","a": "PA = PB, and radii OA and OB are each perpendicular to the tangent segments.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["PA = OA, and angle APB is always 90°.","PA = PB, and chord AB always passes through center O.","Radii OA and OB are parallel to the tangent segments.","PA = PB, and radii OA and OB are each perpendicular to the tangent segments."]},{"id": "Geo.29-18","std": "Geo.29","part": "b","q": "A student conjectures, 'If a quadrilateral has congruent diagonals, then it must be a rectangle.' Which response gives a counterexample and a property that makes the conclusion true?","a": "An isosceles trapezoid has congruent diagonals but is not a rectangle; adding that the diagonals bisect each other makes it a rectangle.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An isosceles trapezoid has congruent diagonals but is not a rectangle; adding that the diagonals bisect each other makes it a rectangle.","A rhombus has congruent diagonals but is not a rectangle; adding four congruent sides makes it a rectangle.","A kite is a counterexample; adding one pair of parallel sides makes it a rectangle.","There is no counterexample; every quadrilateral with congruent diagonals is a rectangle."]},{"id": "Geo.30-10","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which of the following is a defined geometric term rather than an undefined notion?","a": "Ray","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Point","Ray","Line","Plane"]},{"id": "Geo.30-11","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which statement precisely defines ray AB using the undefined notions of point and line?","a": "The set consisting of endpoint A, point B, all points on line AB between A and B, and all points C on line AB such that B is between A and C","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A line that goes on forever in both directions through A and B","Only the points on line AB that lie between A and B","The set consisting of endpoint A, point B, all points on line AB between A and B, and all points C on line AB such that B is between A and C","Two segments joined at point A"]},{"id": "Geo.30-12","std": "Geo.30","q": "A circle is defined as the set of all points in a plane at a given distance r from a given point O. What is the fixed distance r called?","a": "The radius","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The diameter","The chord","The circumference","The radius"]},{"id": "Geo.30-13","std": "Geo.30","q": "A student defines a circle as \"the set of all points at a distance of 5 cm from a fixed point O.\" Why is this definition imprecise in three-dimensional space?","a": "Without specifying \"in a plane,\" all points 5 cm from O in three-dimensional space form a sphere rather than a circle.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Without specifying \"in a plane,\" all points 5 cm from O in three-dimensional space form a sphere rather than a circle.","A circle cannot have a radius of 5 cm.","The definition must use the word \"line segment\" instead of \"point.\"","Fixed point O must lie on the circle itself."]},{"id": "Geo.30-14","std": "Geo.30","q": "Using the undefined notion of distance along a line, which statement precisely defines the midpoint M of line segment AB?","a": "The point M on line segment AB such that the distance AM equals the distance MB","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Any point M in the plane whose distance to A equals its distance to B","The point M on line segment AB such that the distance AM equals the distance MB","The point M on line AB that is farthest from both A and B","A point M that looks roughly halfway between A and B"]},{"id": "Geo.30-15","std": "Geo.30","q": "A student defines perpendicular lines as \"two lines that cross like a plus sign.\" Why is this definition mathematically imprecise?","a": "Comparing lines to a visual symbol does not specify the angle measure; a precise definition states that the lines intersect to form congruent adjacent (90°) right angles.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Perpendicular lines never intersect.","Perpendicular lines must always be horizontal and vertical.","Comparing lines to a visual symbol does not specify the angle measure; a precise definition states that the lines intersect to form congruent adjacent (90°) right angles.","A plus sign only has two angles instead of four."]},{"id": "Geo.30-16","std": "Geo.30","q": "How is a 1° angle precisely defined using the undefined notion of distance around a circular arc?","a": "The measure of a central angle that intercepts an arc equal to 1/360 of the circumference of a circle centered at the angle's vertex","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The measure of an angle whose rays are 1 cm apart at their endpoints","The measure of an angle that intercepts an arc of length 1 cm on any circle","The measure of a central angle that intercepts an arc equal to 1/180 of the circumference of a circle","The measure of a central angle that intercepts an arc equal to 1/360 of the circumference of a circle centered at the angle's vertex"]},{"id": "Geo.30-17","std": "Geo.30","q": "A student defines the perpendicular bisector of segment AB as \"a line that is perpendicular to segment AB.\" What essential condition is missing, and what counterexample shows the flaw?","a": "It omits that the line must pass through the midpoint of AB; a line perpendicular to AB at endpoint A satisfies the student's definition but does not bisect AB.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["It omits that the line must pass through the midpoint of AB; a line perpendicular to AB at endpoint A satisfies the student's definition but does not bisect AB.","It omits that the line must be parallel to AB; any parallel line is a counterexample.","It omits that the line must be a line segment; a ray is a counterexample.","Nothing is missing because every line perpendicular to AB passes through its midpoint."]},{"id": "Geo.30-18","std": "Geo.30","q": "Which chain correctly avoids circular reasoning by building a precise definition of a triangle directly from undefined notions?","a": "Use undefined notions (point, line) to define line segment, then define a triangle as the union of three line segments joining three noncollinear points.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Define a point as the corner of a triangle, then define a triangle as a shape with three points.","Use undefined notions (point, line) to define line segment, then define a triangle as the union of three line segments joining three noncollinear points.","Define a line as a straight edge of a triangle, then define a triangle using three lines.","Define a triangle as any closed figure with three curved or straight sides."]},{"id": "Geo.31-10","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Line m is the perpendicular bisector of segment AB, and point P lies on line m. If PA = 13 cm, what is the length of segment PB?","a": "13 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["6.5 cm","26 cm","13 cm","13√2 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.31-11","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "In triangle PQR, angle P measures 54° and angle Q measures 73°. What is the measure of angle R?","a": "53°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["63°","127°","19°","53°"]},{"id": "Geo.31-12","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "In parallelogram WXYZ, side WX has length 14 in. and angle W measures 108°. What are the length of opposite side ZY and the measure of opposite angle Y?","a": "ZY = 14 in. and angle Y = 108°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["ZY = 14 in. and angle Y = 108°","ZY = 14 in. and angle Y = 72°","ZY = 7 in. and angle Y = 108°","ZY = 28 in. and angle Y = 72°"]},{"id": "Geo.31-13","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. A pair of consecutive (same-side) interior angles measure (3x + 12)° and (5x − 8)°. What is the measure of the larger angle?","a": "102°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["78°","102°","22°","110°"]},{"id": "Geo.31-14","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "In triangle ABC, point D lies on side AB and point E lies on side AC such that AD = 8, DB = 6, AE = 12, and EC = 9. Is segment DE parallel to side BC, and why?","a": "Yes, because AD/DB = 4/3 equals AE/EC = 4/3, satisfying the converse of the Triangle Proportionality Theorem.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["No, because AD does not equal AE.","No, because DB is smaller than EC.","Yes, because AD/DB = 4/3 equals AE/EC = 4/3, satisfying the converse of the Triangle Proportionality Theorem.","Yes, because AD + DB = 14 equals AE + EC."]},{"id": "Geo.31-15","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "In rectangle PQRS, diagonals PR and QS intersect at point M. If PM = 4x − 3 and QM = 2x + 9, what is the full length of diagonal PR?","a": "42","lvl": 2,"choices": ["21","12","36","42"]},{"id": "Geo.31-16","std": "Geo.31","part": "a","q": "Point P lies on the perpendicular bisector of segment XY. If PX = 5x − 7, PY = 3x + 9, and XY = 24, what is the perimeter of triangle PXY?","a": "90","lvl": 3,"choices": ["90","57","66","74"]},{"id": "Geo.31-17","std": "Geo.31","part": "b","q": "In right triangle ABC (right angle at C) with legs a = BC, b = AC, and hypotenuse c = AB, altitude CD splits AB into AD = x and DB = y. Which chain of steps proves a² + b² = c² using similar triangles?","a": "Triangle ACD ~ ABC gives b/c = x/b, so b² = cx; triangle CBD ~ ABC gives a/c = y/a, so a² = cy; adding gives a² + b² = c(x + y) = c².","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Triangle ACD is congruent to CBD by SAS, so a = b and a² + b² = 2a² = c².","Triangle ACD ~ ABC gives b/c = x/b, so b² = cx; triangle CBD ~ ABC gives a/c = y/a, so a² = cy; adding gives a² + b² = c(x + y) = c².","Triangle ACD ~ ABC gives b/c = x/y, so b² = x² and a² = y²; adding gives c².","Triangle ACD ~ CBD gives x/y = a/b, so a² + b² = x² + y² = c²."]},{"id": "Geo.31-18","std": "Geo.31","part": "c","q": "A student conjectures: \"If a quadrilateral has one pair of opposite sides parallel and the other pair of opposite sides congruent, then it must be a parallelogram.\" Is this conjecture true or false, and why?","a": "False; an isosceles trapezoid has one pair of opposite sides parallel and the other pair congruent, yet it is not a parallelogram.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["True; any pair of parallel sides and any pair of congruent sides always force opposite sides to be parallel.","True; congruent legs force the non-parallel sides to have equal slopes.","False; an isosceles trapezoid has one pair of opposite sides parallel and the other pair congruent, yet it is not a parallelogram.","False; a rhombus is a counterexample because all four of its sides are congruent."]},{"id": "Geo.32-10","std": "Geo.32","q": "Does the point (5, −12) lie on the circle centered at the origin (0, 0) with radius 13?","a": "Yes, because 5² + (−12)² = 169 = 13².","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because (−12)² is negative.","No, because 5 + (−12) = −7, which does not equal 13.","Yes, because 5 + 12 = 17 > 13.","Yes, because 5² + (−12)² = 169 = 13²."]},{"id": "Geo.32-11","std": "Geo.32","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(0, 0), B(6, 0), and C(0, 4). Which algebraic statement proves triangle ABC is a right triangle?","a": "Side AB is horizontal (slope 0) and side AC is vertical (undefined slope), so AB is perpendicular to AC.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Side AB is horizontal (slope 0) and side AC is vertical (undefined slope), so AB is perpendicular to AC.","Side AB and side AC have equal lengths.","The sum of the x-coordinates equals 6.","The midpoint of BC lies on the y-axis."]},{"id": "Geo.32-12","std": "Geo.32","q": "In quadrilateral ABCD, diagonal AC has endpoints A(1, 3) and C(7, 9), and diagonal BD has endpoints B(2, 8) and D(6, 4). What do their midpoint calculations prove?","a": "Both midpoints are (4, 6), proving the diagonals bisect each other.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Both diagonals have length 6, proving ABCD is a rectangle.","Both midpoints are (4, 6), proving the diagonals bisect each other.","The diagonals are perpendicular because their slopes multiply to −1.","The midpoints are different, proving ABCD is a trapezoid."]},{"id": "Geo.32-13","std": "Geo.32","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(0, 0), B(8, 0), and C(2, 6). Points M and N are the midpoints of sides AC and BC, respectively. What are the coordinates of M and N, and what is the length of segment MN?","a": "M(1, 3), N(5, 3); MN = 4","lvl": 2,"choices": ["M(1, 3), N(5, 3); MN = 8","M(4, 0), N(5, 3); MN = √10","M(1, 3), N(5, 3); MN = 4","M(2, 6), N(4, 3); MN = √13"]},{"id": "Geo.32-14","std": "Geo.32","q": "Quadrilateral PQRS has consecutive vertices P(0, 2), Q(6, −1), R(8, 3), and S(2, 6). Using slopes and side lengths, what is the most specific classification for PQRS?","a": "Rectangle","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Square","Rhombus","Trapezoid","Rectangle"]},{"id": "Geo.32-15","std": "Geo.32","q": "A circle has center C(−3, 2) and passes through point P(1, 5). Does point Q(2, 2) also lie on this circle?","a": "Yes, because both distance CP and distance CQ equal 5.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Yes, because both distance CP and distance CQ equal 5.","No, because CQ = 1 while CP = 5.","No, because point Q has the same y-coordinate as the center C.","Yes, because both distance CP and distance CQ equal 25."]},{"id": "Geo.32-16","std": "Geo.32","q": "Quadrilateral ABCD has consecutive vertices A(−2, 0), B(6, 0), C(4, 4), and D(0, 4). Which set of coordinate calculations proves ABCD is an isosceles trapezoid?","a": "Slopes of AB and CD are both 0 (parallel), slopes of AD (2) and BC (−2) are unequal (not parallel), and legs AD = BC = 2√5.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["AB = 8 and CD = 4, so opposite sides are unequal.","Slopes of AB and CD are both 0 (parallel), slopes of AD (2) and BC (−2) are unequal (not parallel), and legs AD = BC = 2√5.","Midpoints of AC and BD are both (2, 2).","Slopes of AD and BC multiply to −1, so AD is perpendicular to BC."]},{"id": "Geo.32-17","std": "Geo.32","q": "A right triangle has vertices O(0, 0), A(2a, 0), and B(0, 2b) with a > 0 and b > 0, and M is the midpoint of hypotenuse AB. What are the coordinates of M and the exact distance OM?","a": "M(a, b); OM = √(a² + b²)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["M(2a, 2b); OM = 2√(a² + b²)","M(a, b); OM = a + b","M(a, b); OM = √(a² + b²)","M(a, b); OM = a² + b²"]},{"id": "Geo.32-18","std": "Geo.32","q": "A parallelogram has vertices O(0, 0), A(a, 0), B(a + c, b), and C(c, b). Which calculation proves that its diagonals bisect each other?","a": "The midpoints of diagonals OB and AC are both ((a + c)/2, b/2).","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Diagonals OB and AC both have length √((a + c)² + b²).","The slopes of OB and AC multiply to −1.","The midpoint of side OA is (a/2, 0) and the midpoint of side CB is (c + a/2, b).","The midpoints of diagonals OB and AC are both ((a + c)/2, b/2)."]},{"id": "Geo.33-10","std": "Geo.33","q": "What is the slope of any line parallel to the line 2x + 3y = 12?","a": "−2/3","lvl": 1,"choices": ["−2/3","2/3","3/2","−3/2"]},{"id": "Geo.33-11","std": "Geo.33","q": "Line h has equation y = 5. What is the slope of any line perpendicular to line h?","a": "Undefined (a vertical line)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0","Undefined (a vertical line)","−1/5","5"]},{"id": "Geo.33-12","std": "Geo.33","q": "How are the lines y = (3/4)x − 2 and 4x + 3y = 9 related?","a": "Perpendicular","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Parallel","Neither parallel nor perpendicular","Perpendicular","Coincident (the exact same line)"]},{"id": "Geo.33-13","std": "Geo.33","q": "Line m passes through points A(−1, 2) and B(3, 4). What is the equation of the line perpendicular to line m that passes through point C(2, 1)?","a": "y = −2x + 5","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = (1/2)x","y = 2x − 3","y = −2x + 1","y = −2x + 5"]},{"id": "Geo.33-14","std": "Geo.33","q": "Line 1 passes through (−2, 5) and (4, 8). Line 2 passes through (1, −3) and (5, k). For what value of k is Line 2 parallel to Line 1?","a": "−1","lvl": 2,"choices": ["−1","5","−5","1"]},{"id": "Geo.33-15","std": "Geo.33","q": "Two non-vertical parallel lines both cross the x-axis. How do right slope triangles drawn under each line prove that parallel lines have equal slopes?","a": "Corresponding angles with the x-axis are congruent and both triangles contain a 90° angle, so the slope triangles are similar by AA and have equal rise-to-run ratios.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["The two slope triangles must have the exact same hypotenuse length.","Corresponding angles with the x-axis are congruent and both triangles contain a 90° angle, so the slope triangles are similar by AA and have equal rise-to-run ratios.","Parallel lines always have the same y-intercept, which forces equal slopes.","The product of the two rise-to-run ratios must equal −1."]},{"id": "Geo.33-16","std": "Geo.33","q": "Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 2), B(5, 4), and C(3, 8). What is the equation of the line containing the altitude from vertex C perpendicular to side AB?","a": "y = −2x + 14","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = (1/2)x + 6.5","y = −2x + 8","y = −2x + 14","y = 2x + 2"]},{"id": "Geo.33-17","std": "Geo.33","q": "Find the shortest (perpendicular) distance from the point P(1, 7) to the line y = x + 2.","a": "2√2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["4","√2","4√2","2√2"]},{"id": "Geo.33-18","std": "Geo.33","q": "Three consecutive vertices of rectangle ABCD are A(0, 1), B(6, 3), and C(5, 6). Using parallel and perpendicular slope criteria, find the coordinates of the fourth vertex D.","a": "(−1, 4)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−1, 4)","(1, 4)","(−1, 5)","(11, 4)"]},{"id": "Geo.34-10","std": "Geo.34","q": "To find the distance across a ravine between stakes A and B, an engineer places stake C on the near side, measures angle BAC, side AC, and angle BCA, and builds an identical triangle on flat ground using those three measurements. Which congruence criterion guarantees the corresponding side equals AB?","a": "ASA","lvl": 1,"choices": ["SSS","ASA","SAS","AA"]},{"id": "Geo.34-11","std": "Geo.34","q": "On an architectural blueprint with a scale of 1 inch = 4 feet, a triangular roof gable has a base of 6 inches and a height of 2.5 inches. What are the base and height of the actual roof gable?","a": "Base = 24 feet, height = 10 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Base = 10 feet, height = 6.5 feet","Base = 1.5 feet, height = 0.625 feet","Base = 24 feet, height = 10 feet","Base = 24 feet, height = 2.5 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.34-12","std": "Geo.34","q": "A carpenter builds two rectangular wooden gate frames by joining a 3-foot rail and a 4-foot stile at a 90° corner before attaching a diagonal brace across the opposite ends. Which congruence criterion guarantees both diagonal braces have the exact same length?","a": "SAS","lvl": 1,"choices": ["AAA","ASA","AAS","SAS"]},{"id": "Geo.34-13","std": "Geo.34","q": "A projector sits 12 feet from a flat screen and projects an image that is 8 feet tall. If the projector is moved back to 18 feet from the screen without changing the projection angle, how tall will the projected image be?","a": "12 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12 feet","14 feet","5.3 feet","10 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.34-14","std": "Geo.34","q": "Two taut guy wires connect the top of a vertical 40-foot radio tower to anchor points on level ground on opposite sides, each 30 feet from the tower's base. How long is each wire, and which triangle congruence criterion proves the two wires are equal in length?","a": "50 feet each; SAS","lvl": 2,"choices": ["70 feet each; SSS","50 feet each; SAS","50 feet each; ASA","35 feet each; AA"]},{"id": "Geo.34-15","std": "Geo.34","q": "To measure the width DE across a canyon, a surveyor sets up two triangles ABC and EDC meeting at stake C such that segment AB is parallel to segment DE, AC = 18 m, CE = 54 m, and AB = 25 m. What is the width DE of the canyon?","a": "75 m","lvl": 2,"choices": ["61 m","50 m","75 m","8.3 m"]},{"id": "Geo.34-16","std": "Geo.34","q": "An attic ceiling slopes in a straight line from a height of 9 feet at an interior wall down to 0 feet at the outer wall, covering a horizontal distance of 12 feet. How far from the outer wall must a 6-foot-tall bookshelf be placed so its top edge just touches the sloping ceiling?","a": "8 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["4 feet","9 feet","6 feet","8 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.34-17","std": "Geo.34","q": "On a billiard table, a cue ball sits 2 feet from the top rail and a target ball sits 3 feet from the top rail, with a horizontal distance of 5 feet between them parallel to the rail. If the cue ball bounces off the top rail at equal incoming and outgoing angles to hit the target ball, how far horizontally from the cue ball should the bounce point be?","a": "2 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["2 feet","2.5 feet","3 feet","3.3 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.34-18","std": "Geo.34","q": "To estimate the height of a vertical cliff, a hiker stands a 5-foot vertical trekking pole 10 feet in front of her and lies with her eye on the level ground so the top of the pole aligns with the top of the cliff. If the base of the cliff is 150 feet beyond the pole, how tall is the cliff?","a": "80 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["75 feet","80 feet","300 feet","85 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.35-10","std": "Geo.35","part": "a","q": "In a 30°-60°-90° right triangle, the shorter leg (opposite the 30° angle) has length 9 cm. What is the length of the hypotenuse?","a": "18 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["9√2 cm","9√3 cm","18 cm","4.5 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.35-11","std": "Geo.35","part": "b","q": "In right triangle DEF with right angle at F, DF = 8, EF = 15, and DE = 17. What is cos(D)?","a": "8/17","lvl": 1,"choices": ["15/17","8/15","17/8","8/17"]},{"id": "Geo.35-12","std": "Geo.35","part": "d","q": "A triangle has side lengths 6 cm, 8 cm, and 11 cm. Use the converse of the Pythagorean Theorem to determine whether it is a right triangle.","a": "No, because 6² + 8² = 100, which does not equal 11² = 121.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["No, because 6² + 8² = 100, which does not equal 11² = 121.","Yes, because 6 + 8 > 11.","Yes, because 6² + 8² = 11².","No, because 6 + 8 = 14 does not equal 11."]},{"id": "Geo.35-13","std": "Geo.35","part": "a","q": "A square patio has a diagonal of length 10 feet. Using a 45°-45°-90° right triangle, what are the exact side length and exact area of the patio?","a": "Side = 5√2 feet; Area = 50 ft²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Side = 10√2 feet; Area = 200 ft²","Side = 5√2 feet; Area = 50 ft²","Side = 5 feet; Area = 25 ft²","Side = 5√3 feet; Area = 75 ft²"]},{"id": "Geo.35-14","std": "Geo.35","part": "c","q": "The angles (2x + 10)° and (3x − 20)° are acute, and sin(2x + 10)° = cos(3x − 20)°. What is the value of x?","a": "20","lvl": 2,"choices": ["30","16","20","38"]},{"id": "Geo.35-15","std": "Geo.35","part": "b","q": "Right triangle ABC has legs 6 and 8 and hypotenuse 10. Similar right triangle DEF has legs 15 and 20 and hypotenuse 25, with acute angle A corresponding to angle D (each opposite the shorter leg). Why is sin(A) equal to sin(D)?","a": "Because similar right triangles have proportional corresponding sides, so the opposite-to-hypotenuse ratios 6/10 and 15/25 both reduce to 3/5.","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Because both triangles have the same area.","Because sine always equals 3/5 for every right triangle.","Because the hypotenuse is always 2 units longer than the longer leg.","Because similar right triangles have proportional corresponding sides, so the opposite-to-hypotenuse ratios 6/10 and 15/25 both reduce to 3/5."]},{"id": "Geo.35-16","std": "Geo.35","part": "d","q": "To check whether a wall corner forms a 90° angle, a carpenter marks 8 ft along one wall and 15 ft along the other wall, where an exact 90° corner requires a 17 ft diagonal. If the carpenter measures the diagonal as 16.5 ft instead, what type of angle is at the corner?","a": "An acute angle (less than 90°), because 16.5² < 8² + 15².","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An acute angle (less than 90°), because 16.5² < 8² + 15².","An obtuse angle (greater than 90°), because 16.5² < 8² + 15².","A right angle, because 8 + 15 > 16.5.","An obtuse angle (greater than 90°), because 16.5 is the longest side."]},{"id": "Geo.35-17","std": "Geo.35","part": "e","q": "From a point on level ground 80 feet from the base of a radio tower, the angle of elevation to the top of the tower is 35°. Given sin(35°) ≈ 0.5736, cos(35°) ≈ 0.8192, and tan(35°) ≈ 0.7002, what is the height of the tower to the nearest tenth of a foot?","a": "56.0 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["45.9 feet","56.0 feet","65.5 feet","114.3 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.35-18","std": "Geo.35","part": "e","q": "Find the area of a regular pentagon with side length 8 cm to the nearest whole square centimeter. (Each central triangle has a 72° vertex angle, so half of it is a right triangle with acute angle 36° opposite half a side; use tan(36°) ≈ 0.7265.)","a": "110 cm²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["55 cm²","220 cm²","110 cm²","80 cm²"]},{"id": "Geo.36-10","std": "Geo.36","q": "Which three-dimensional geometric solid best models an unsharpened wooden hexagonal pencil (ignoring the eraser)?","a": "Hexagonal prism","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Hexagonal pyramid","Cone","Rectangular prism","Hexagonal prism"]},{"id": "Geo.36-11","std": "Geo.36","q": "An ice cream waffle cone is modeled as a right circular cone with radius 3 cm and height 10 cm. What is its volume in terms of π?","a": "30π cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30π cm³","90π cm³","10π cm³","60π cm³"]},{"id": "Geo.36-12","std": "Geo.36","q": "A circular soccer practice area has a radius of 15 yards. How many square yards of turf are needed to cover the area, in terms of π?","a": "225π yd²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30π yd²","225π yd²","450π yd²","15π yd²"]},{"id": "Geo.36-13","std": "Geo.36","q": "A lap swimming pool is 25 m long and 10 m wide. Its depth increases uniformly from 1.2 m at the shallow end to 2.8 m at the deep end, forming a trapezoidal prism. What is the total volume of water the pool can hold?","a": "500 m³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["700 m³","300 m³","500 m³","1,000 m³"]},{"id": "Geo.36-14","std": "Geo.36","q": "An athletic track is modeled as a 60 m by 40 m rectangle with a semicircle attached to each 40 m end. What is the exact distance of one complete lap around the outer boundary of the track, in terms of π?","a": "120 + 40π m","lvl": 2,"choices": ["200 + 40π m","120 + 80π m","120 + 20π m","120 + 40π m"]},{"id": "Geo.36-15","std": "Geo.36","q": "A solid copper rod is modeled as a cylinder with radius 2 cm and length 15 cm. If copper has a density of 8.9 g/cm³, what is the mass of the rod to the nearest gram (use π ≈ 3.14)?","a": "1,677 g","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1,677 g","838 g","188 g","3,354 g"]},{"id": "Geo.36-16","std": "Geo.36","q": "An astronomical observatory is modeled as a cylinder of radius 8 m and height 10 m topped with a hemispherical dome of radius 8 m. How many square meters of exterior wall and dome surface need to be painted (excluding the flat ground floor)? Leave your answer in terms of π.","a": "288π m²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["416π m²","288π m²","224π m²","352π m²"]},{"id": "Geo.36-17","std": "Geo.36","q": "A conical pile of gravel at a road-work site has a circular base circumference of 20π feet and a height of 9 feet. If each dump truck carries 100 cubic feet of gravel, what is the minimum number of truckloads needed to haul away the entire pile (use π ≈ 3.14)?","a": "10 truckloads","lvl": 3,"choices": ["9 truckloads","29 truckloads","10 truckloads","38 truckloads"]},{"id": "Geo.36-18","std": "Geo.36","q": "A wildlife preserve is modeled as a right triangle with legs 6 km and 8 km, containing a circular lake of radius 1 km where deer cannot live. If biologists count 418 deer across the land area of the preserve, what is the deer density to the nearest whole number of deer per square kilometer (use π ≈ 3.14)?","a": "20 deer per km²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["17 deer per km²","10 deer per km²","24 deer per km²","20 deer per km²"]},{"id": "Geo.37-10","std": "Geo.37","q": "In circle O, inscribed angle ABD and inscribed angle ACD both intercept minor arc AD. If angle ABD measures 37°, what is the measure of angle ACD?","a": "37°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["37°","74°","18.5°","143°"]},{"id": "Geo.37-11","std": "Geo.37","q": "From an external point P, segments PA and PB are both tangent to circle O at points A and B. If PA = 14 cm, what is the length of segment PB?","a": "14 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["7 cm","14 cm","28 cm","14√2 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.37-12","std": "Geo.37","q": "In circle O, inscribed angle PQR measures 43°. What is the measure of central angle POR that intercepts the same arc PR?","a": "86°","lvl": 1,"choices": ["43°","21.5°","86°","137°"]},{"id": "Geo.37-13","std": "Geo.37","q": "Triangle ABC is inscribed in circle O with side AB passing through center O (so AB is a diameter). If angle A measures 34°, what are the measures of angle C and angle B?","a": "angle C = 90° and angle B = 56°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["angle C = 68° and angle B = 78°","angle C = 90° and angle B = 34°","angle C = 112° and angle B = 34°","angle C = 90° and angle B = 56°"]},{"id": "Geo.37-14","std": "Geo.37","q": "Circle O has a radius of 13 cm. A chord AB lies at a perpendicular distance of 5 cm from center O. What is the total length of chord AB?","a": "24 cm","lvl": 2,"choices": ["24 cm","12 cm","16 cm","8 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.37-15","std": "Geo.37","q": "Quadrilateral PQRS is inscribed in a circle. If angle P measures (3x + 15)° and opposite angle R measures (2x + 35)°, what is the measure of angle P?","a": "93°","lvl": 2,"choices": ["26°","93°","87°","75°"]},{"id": "Geo.37-16","std": "Geo.37","q": "From external point P, tangents PA and PB touch circle O at points A and B, forming circumscribed angle APB = 56°. Point C lies on the major arc AB of the circle. What is the measure of inscribed angle ACB?","a": "62°","lvl": 3,"choices": ["28°","124°","62°","118°"]},{"id": "Geo.37-17","std": "Geo.37","q": "Point P lies outside circle O, and segment PT is tangent to the circle at point T with PT = 12 cm. Segment PO crosses the circle at point Q such that PQ = 8 cm. What is the radius r of circle O?","a": "5 cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["4 cm","10 cm","8 cm","5 cm"]},{"id": "Geo.37-18","std": "Geo.37","q": "Tangents PA and PB from external point P touch circle O at A and B. Why are circumscribed angle APB and central angle AOB always supplementary?","a": "Each radius is perpendicular to its tangent, so angles OAP and OBP are 90°; the angles of quadrilateral OAPB sum to 360°, leaving 180° for angles AOB and APB.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Each radius is perpendicular to its tangent, so angles OAP and OBP are 90°; the angles of quadrilateral OAPB sum to 360°, leaving 180° for angles AOB and APB.","Angle APB is an inscribed angle, so it is half of angle AOB.","Triangles OAP and OBP are congruent, so angle APB equals angle AOB.","PA = PB, so triangle APB is isosceles and its angles sum to 180°."]},{"id": "Geo.38-10","std": "Geo.38","q": "While designing a square courtyard, an architect solves a quadratic model for the side length x and obtains x = 14 meters and x = −6 meters. During the feasibility check of the modeling cycle, what should the architect do?","a": "Accept x = 14 meters and reject x = −6 meters because a physical length cannot be negative.","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Average the two solutions to get x = 4 meters.","Accept x = 14 meters and reject x = −6 meters because a physical length cannot be negative.","Use x = 6 meters because negative signs are ignored in geometry.","Reject both solutions because a quadratic equation cannot model a courtyard."]},{"id": "Geo.38-11","std": "Geo.38","q": "A packaging designer makes an open-top tray from a 10 in. by 14 in. rectangular sheet of cardboard by cutting a 2 in. by 2 in. square from each corner and folding up the sides. What is the volume of the tray?","a": "120 in.³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["280 in.³","192 in.³","120 in.³","60 in.³"]},{"id": "Geo.38-12","std": "Geo.38","q": "A food company wraps a rectangular paper label completely around the curved lateral side of a cylindrical can with radius 4 cm and height 11 cm (no overlap). What area of paper is needed for one label in terms of π?","a": "88π cm²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["44π cm²","176π cm²","120π cm²","88π cm²"]},{"id": "Geo.38-13","std": "Geo.38","q": "A store packs upright cylindrical spice jars (diameter 2 in., height 4 in.) into a rectangular shipping box measuring 10 in. by 6 in. by 7 in., standing on its 10 in. by 6 in. base so the box is 7 in. tall. Accounting for unusable vertical space, what is the maximum number of upright jars that fit in one box?","a": "15 jars","lvl": 2,"choices": ["15 jars","30 jars","26 jars","33 jars"]},{"id": "Geo.38-14","std": "Geo.38","q": "A landscape designer has 100 feet of stone edging to completely enclose a rectangular patio whose length must be 10 feet greater than its width. What dimensions and area satisfy this design?","a": "20 ft by 30 ft; Area = 600 ft²","lvl": 2,"choices": ["25 ft by 35 ft; Area = 875 ft²","20 ft by 30 ft; Area = 600 ft²","45 ft by 55 ft; Area = 2,475 ft²","20 ft by 25 ft; Area = 500 ft²"]},{"id": "Geo.38-15","std": "Geo.38","q": "A homeowner wants a cylindrical rainwater barrel that holds at least 36π ft³ of water, but it must stand upright under a porch ceiling that is 4 feet high. What is the minimum radius the barrel must have?","a": "3 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["9 feet","6 feet","3 feet","4.5 feet"]},{"id": "Geo.38-16","std": "Geo.38","q": "A candle maker wants an open-top cylindrical glass jar (bottom and curved side only) that holds 64π cm³ of wax. Design A has radius 4 cm and height 4 cm, while Design B has radius 2 cm and height 16 cm. Which design uses less glass, and by how much (in terms of π)?","a": "Design A uses less glass by 20π cm².","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Design B uses less glass by 20π cm².","Design A uses less glass by 8π cm².","Both designs use the exact same amount of glass because both hold 64π cm³.","Design A uses less glass by 20π cm²."]},{"id": "Geo.38-17","std": "Geo.38","q": "A building code requires a straight wheelchair ramp to have a slope no steeper than 1:12 (at least 12 ft of horizontal run for every 1 ft of vertical rise) and a width of 4 ft. For a doorway 2.5 ft above the sidewalk, what are the minimum horizontal run, the sloped ramp length (to the nearest tenth), and the area of non-slip grip tape needed to cover the ramp surface (to the nearest square foot)?","a": "Run = 30 ft; Ramp length ≈ 30.1 ft; Area ≈ 120 ft²","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Run = 30 ft; Ramp length ≈ 30.1 ft; Area ≈ 120 ft²","Run = 30 ft; Ramp length ≈ 32.5 ft; Area ≈ 130 ft²","Run = 24 ft; Ramp length ≈ 24.1 ft; Area ≈ 96 ft²","Run = 30 ft; Ramp length ≈ 30.1 ft; Area ≈ 60 ft²"]},{"id": "Geo.38-18","std": "Geo.38","q": "A juice carton measures 2 in. by 3 in. by 10 in. (60 in.³). A designer proposes a 3 in. by 4 in. by 5 in. carton (also 60 in.³). Which carton uses less cardboard, and what is one feasibility factor to check before switching?","a": "The 3 by 4 by 5 carton (94 in.² vs. 112 in.²); check whether it fits in hands, cup holders, shipping cases, and the filling machines.","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The 2 by 3 by 10 carton (112 in.² vs. 94 in.²); check the price of the juice.","The 3 by 4 by 5 carton (94 in.² vs. 112 in.²); check whether it fits in hands, cup holders, shipping cases, and the filling machines.","They use the same cardboard because their volumes are equal, so nothing needs checking.","The 3 by 4 by 5 carton (47 in.² vs. 56 in.²); check whether it holds 60 in.³."]}]};
